epos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/enyineer/checkstack/commit/f96bf593c3842b7ddf762e2ec33112ea28c945b8","node_id":"C_kwDOQrp5TdoAKGY5NmJmNTkzYzM4NDJiN2RkZjc2MmUyZWMzMzExMmVhMjhjOTQ1Yjg","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/enyineer/checkstack/commit/be74b01860dfc7602fd3b4e3d64ee1c332f6a5e9","sha":"be74b01860dfc7602fd3b4e3d64ee1c332f6a5e9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/enyineer/checkstack/commits/be74b01860dfc7602fd3b4e3d64ee1c332f6a5e9"}],"sha":"f96bf593c3842b7ddf762e2ec33112ea28c945b8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/enyineer/checkstack/commits/f96bf593c3842b7ddf762e2ec33112ea28c945b8"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/27201677?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/enyineer","id":27201677,"login":"enyineer","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI3MjAxNjc3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/enyineer/checkstack/commits/be74b01860dfc7602fd3b4e3d64ee1c332f6a5e9/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-07-24T09:02:24Z","email":"nico.enking@gmail.com","name":"Nico"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-07-24T09:02:24Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix: feedback round 1 — status pages, health/satellite, tables & tones (#485)\n\n* fix(announcement): render info severity in the blue info tone, not grey\n\nThe announcement plugin kept a private copy of the status tone table that\nonly declared ok/warn/down/unknown - the blue `info` tone from\n`@checkstack/ui` was missing entirely. `severityToTone(\"info\")` therefore\nfell through to `unknown`, so an info-severity announcement''s pill badge,\ncard accent stripe and global banner all rendered `*-status-unknown` grey,\nreading as \"inert/disabled\" rather than \"informational\". This affected the\nannouncements manage page, the dashboard and the public status-page widget.\n`AnnouncementBanner` carried a second, independent severity-to-class switch\nwith the same grey default.\n\n- Map `info` severity onto the `info` tone, matching the ramp incidents and\n  status pages already use, and alias `StatusTone` to the shared\n  `StatusPillTone` so the plugin can no longer lose a tone.\n- Delete the private tone table; re-export the shared `pillToneStyles`.\n- Derive the banner''s classes from `severityToTone` + the shared table so\n  banner and pill can no longer drift.\n- Add `text` / `tint` / `border` / `tintHover` class sets to\n  `pillToneStyles` (additive) so banner-like surfaces come from one table,\n  and document them plus the announcement mapping in the Storybook story.\n\nAdds a regression test asserting `info` never resolves to `unknown`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* fix(telemetry): stop anonymous page loads from logging auth errors\n\nOpening the app unauthenticated printed an error-level stack trace per\nstream plugin (\"RPC /api/metricstream/listLinkedStreamStatuses failed:\nAuthentication required\"). Two independent causes:\n\n- The dashboard is reachable anonymously (catalog reads are public, as are\n  the healthcheck / incident / SLO / anomaly signal sources), but the three\n  stream plugins'' `listLinkedStreamStatuses` is authenticated-only. Their\n  dashboard signal fillers queried it regardless of the caller, so every\n  anonymous page load fired three requests that could only come back 401.\n- A contract-level 4xx was logged at error with a full stack trace. That is\n  the authorization layer working as designed, and the access-log middleware\n  already reports every 4xx at warn with method, path and status.\n\nFixes:\n\n- Gate the linked-stream status lookup on the caller being authenticated,\n  and hold the dashboard''s \"loading\" report while the session resolves so\n  the overview cannot flash \"all healthy\" and then re-skeleton.\n- Log a 4xx `ORPCError` at debug without a stack (`isClientRejection`);\n  5xx keeps its loud error-level logging.\n\nThe three fillers were the same component apart from their client, source\nid and deriver, so the fetch/chunk/merge/report machinery moved into a\nshared `useLinkedStreamSignals` hook in `@checkstack/telemetry-frontend`.\nSide effect: the tracestream filler''s query is now namespaced under its\nplugin id like the other two, so the signal auto-invalidator refreshes it.\n\nVerified against a running backend: an anonymous POST to the procedure now\nproduces one warn access-log line and no stack, and an anonymous page load\nproduces no 4xx at all.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* feat(announcement): sortable, filterable manage table with deliberate lifecycle tones\n\nTwo changes to the announcements manage page.\n\n1. Lifecycle states get chosen colours instead of accidental grey.\n\n\"Scheduled\", \"Expired\" and \"Inactive\" all fell through a `default:` arm in\n`statusToTone` to the neutral grey `unknown` tone, so a scheduled\nannouncement was indistinguishable from an inert one and none of the three\nhad been picked on purpose. Colour is now split by what it answers:\n\n- A row is coloured by its SEVERITY (the leading dot, the mobile card\n  stripe), matching the banner, the dashboard card and the status-page\n  widget, which already worked that way.\n- A row''s lifecycle is stated in words on a neutral pill, so it no longer\n  puts a second, competing colour scale on the same line.\n- The stat strip keeps lifecycle colour, because each card IS a bucket:\n  active green, scheduled informational blue (deliberately not amber, which\n  means \"degraded\" product-wide), expired and inactive the inert grey - now\n  by explicit decision rather than by omission.\n\n2. The table sorts and filters.\n\n- Every column except Actions sorts. Severity by impact, status by\n  lifecycle, Created by raw timestamp rather than its \"3 days ago\" prose,\n  and the icon columns by the label their tooltip shows.\n- Title search plus severity / status / visibility facets, a Clear\n  affordance and a filtered-empty state. Facet values are parsed against\n  the schemas that define them, so an unrecognised value degrades to\n  unconstrained instead of becoming a filter nothing matches.\n- Reordering moved into its own sortable \"Order\" column showing each row''s\n  1-based position, which is what makes the arrows legible under another\n  sort. The arrows are disabled while a filter hides rows, since the\n  neighbour being swapped with would be off-screen - the rule the catalog''s\n  Groups tab already uses. The table''s \"deliberately not sortable\" note is\n  obsolete: showing the canonical position removes the constraint.\n\nAlso fixes a pre-existing panel-in-panel (the table painted its own\nbordered surface inside an already-opaque Card) by passing `surface={false}`,\nand places the filters in the CardHeader, the shape the automation list and\nruns pages converged on for a full-bleed table in a `p-0` CardContent.\n\n20 new tests cover the facet logic, the per-facet parsers and the sort ranks.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* feat(ui): give DataTable native facet filtering with URL-persisted state\n\nSearch and \"narrow by status/severity/type\" had no home in DataTable, which\nowned only a free-text box whose query lived in internal state a page could\nnot observe. Any surface needing to know what was filtered - to gate a\ncontrol, to render its own empty state, to put the view in a shareable link -\nabandoned the built-in search wholesale and hand-rolled everything. A survey\nof all 33 table-bearing surfaces found 18 rendering filter UI outside\nDataTable against 17 using only its built-in search, and of the 12 with real\nfacets only 2 routed them through the component at all. Zero mixed the two:\nit was all-in or all-out, every time. That produced six different renderings\nof the same select and three different \"show everything\" sentinels.\n\nDataTable now accepts:\n\n- `facets`: declarative {id, label, options, value} filters rendered beside\n  the search box, ANDed with each other and the search, with a Clear\n  affordance and a noResultsState that fires on facet emptiness.\n- `filters`/`onFiltersChange`/`onClearFilters`: the state is controllable, so\n  a page can observe it. Omit them and the table owns it internally, which\n  keeps the simple case free of routing.\n- `surface={false}` additionally insets the filter bar with a separating\n  rule, so a table nested full-bleed in a page''s Card no longer has its\n  controls flush against the card''s edges.\n\nNew exports:\n\n- `useDataTableFilters` persists to the URL, so a filtered view is shareable,\n  survives reload, and returns intact from a row''s detail page. Exposes\n  `active` for gating controls a filtered view makes ambiguous, a `debounced`\n  variant for server-side query inputs, and `paramPrefix` for two filtered\n  tables on one page.\n- `DataTableFilterBar` for list surfaces that are not tables.\n- `useDebouncedValue`, previously copied verbatim into six packages, each\n  with a comment noting no shared version existed.\n\nThe announcements table is migrated onto it as the first consumer and drops\nits local filter module. Docs updated - the page previously legislated the\nopposite (\"let DataTable own only the free-text search\").\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* feat(ui): add pill facets, and migrate automation onto the shared filter bar\n\nFirst wave of migrations onto DataTable''s native facets.\n\n- `DataTableFacet` gains `kind: \"select\" | \"pills\"`. A segmented pill row is\n  the right control for two or three short options a reader benefits from\n  seeing at a glance, and several surfaces had independently built one, so\n  the shared bar renders that variant rather than forcing every list into a\n  dropdown. Both variants share one state, sentinel and URL round-trip, and\n  the pills set `aria-pressed` - which two of the hand-rolled groups they\n  replace had omitted, leaving screen-reader users unable to tell which\n  filter was live.\n- `parsedFacetValue` reads a selection back as a domain value by parsing it\n  against the schema that defines it. Facet state is stringly-typed because\n  it round-trips through the URL, but a server-side filter needs the narrow\n  union its query input declares; parsing rather than casting means a stale\n  link degrades to unconstrained instead of smuggling an unknown value into\n  a request.\n- AutomationListPage and RunsPage drop their hand-rolled pill rows. Their\n  filters now persist to the URL, so a link to \"the failed runs of this\n  automation\" reopens filtered. RunsPage also gains the `surface={false}` it\n  was missing, fixing a panel-in-panel the survey flagged.\n- `useDebouncedValue` had been copied into six packages, each carrying a\n  comment noting no shared version existed. All six now import the one in\n  `@checkstack/ui`; the copies are deleted.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* feat(incident,maintenance): move status filters onto the shared filter bar\n\nBoth config pages carried the same hand-rolled pair: a status `<Select>` with\nits own \"all\" sentinel, and a raw `<input type=\"checkbox\">`. They were copies\nof each other down to the container''s class list.\n\nBoth now render `DataTableFilterBar`, with the status facet''s options and\nlabels derived from the plugin''s own status enum and its badge presenter - so\nthe dropdown and the rows can never disagree about what a status is called.\nThe selection persists to the URL, so a link to \"the incidents being\ninvestigated\" reopens filtered.\n\nThe \"Show resolved\" / \"Show completed\" toggles are deliberately NOT modelled\nas facets. A facet NARROWS; these WIDEN the list to include rows the endpoint\nexcludes by default, so expressing them as facets would invert their meaning.\nThey ride in the filter bar''s `children` slot instead - shared rendering,\nhonest semantics - and pick up the shared `Checkbox` in place of the raw\ninput.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* feat(catalog,healthcheck): finish the migration onto the shared filter bar\n\nCompletes the consolidation: every faceted list surface now renders one\ncontrol set over one state model.\n\n`@checkstack/ui` gains what these two surfaces genuinely needed, rather than\neither of them forking again:\n\n- `DataTableFacetControl` splits a facet''s PRESENTATIONAL half from its row\n  accessor. The catalog''s matching cannot be a `value(row): string` - a\n  system belongs to several groups and carries several tags, its health lives\n  in a separate status map, and the same three controls also narrow GROUPS, a\n  different row type. Supplying fake accessors would have been the hack.\n- `disabled`/`disabledReason` keep a control present-but-unavailable (the\n  catalog''s health filter before a health source is installed). A disabled\n  control says the capability exists and what would unlock it, which an\n  absent one cannot, and it keeps the parameter declared so a selection\n  arriving on a link still constrains.\n- A facet option may carry a `tone`, applied while selected. Reserved for a\n  dimension that genuinely IS a status: the run filter''s green Healthy / red\n  Failing is the product''s vocabulary, and the hand-rolled pills being\n  replaced carried exactly that colour. A shared control that could not\n  express it would be a downgrade on the surface where colour means most.\n  Tone never affects matching (guarded by a test).\n\nMigrated:\n\n- Catalog: `CatalogBrowseToolbar` becomes a thin wrapper over the shared bar;\n  the density toggle rides in `children`, since it narrows nothing and as a\n  facet would sit behind Clear, where \"clear filters\" would silently reset\n  row height. One toolbar still drives the browse grid and all three manage\n  tabs; GroupsTab''s reorder arrows stay gated on the filtered state.\n  Environments were filtered by an ad-hoc substring match in the page and now\n  go through the shared logic, matching descriptions too.\n- Health checks: the list toolbar (a self-declared copy of the catalog''s) and\n  both hand-rolled pill groups are deleted. Run-history filters gain URL\n  persistence, so a filtered run view is shareable, and both pill groups gain\n  the `aria-pressed` and labelled group they lacked.\n\nEvery existing URL parameter is preserved and guarded by tests, so links\nshared before the migration still reopen the same view - including the\ncatalog''s per-system \"view health checks\" link, whose server-side\nauthorization path is deliberately kept as a control with no row accessor.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* feat(ui): make filtering part of the column contract\n\nFiltering joins sorting and searching on `DataTableColumn`: providing\n`filterValue` is what makes a column filterable, with no separate boolean\nflag, exactly as `sortValue` makes it sortable.\n\nA status column already reads the row for `sortValue` and renders it in\n`cell`. Declaring the filter there too states the value ONCE, so the badge,\nthe sort and the filter cannot drift apart - previously the same value had to\nbe repeated in a standalone facet.\n\n`filterOptions` is optional. Omitted, the options are DERIVED from the\ndistinct values present in the data, sorted and labelled by the raw value.\nDeclare them when the raw values are not what a person should read\n(`authenticated` -> \"Authenticated only\"), when the order carries meaning\n(severity by impact, which deriving would sort alphabetically into critical /\ninfo / warning), or when an option must stay on offer though no row has it.\n\nOptions come from the FULL row set, never from what is currently visible.\nThat is also why a cell cannot simply publish its value upward for the table\nto collect: rows excluded by a filter never render, so reading options off\nthe rendered pills would let selecting one option delete every other option\nand strand the user with no way back. Data has to exist before the render\nthat consumes it. Guarded by a test.\n\nThe standalone `facets` prop keeps its place for a dimension no single column\nowns - the catalog''s group and tag filters match several values per row and\nnarrow two different row types. Column-derived facets render first, in column\norder, then those.\n\nAnnouncements is converted: severity, status and visibility now filter from\nthe columns that display them, and its facet module shrinks to the option\nlists whose labels and ordering cannot be derived.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* refactor(ui): move every table''s filters into the table itself\n\nThe earlier migration unified how filter controls are BUILT but left several\nrendering above their table as a detached bar, which I justified by the\nfiltering running server-side. That justification was wrong: where the\nnarrowing runs says nothing about where the control belongs, and a bar\nfloating above a card reads as unrelated to the list beneath it.\n\nAudited all 43 DataTable surfaces. Six had a bar that did not need to be\noutside; all six are now in the table''s own bar:\n\n- Incidents, maintenances and automation run history: the Status column\n  declares `filterValue`, so the control sits with the column it filters. The\n  selection still narrows the list query - that is what reduces the fetch -\n  and the column filter re-applying it over already-scoped rows is a no-op.\n- Health-check list: search, strategy and status move onto their columns; the\n  assigned-system control rides in as a control-only facet.\n- Health-check drawer: the run-status control moves into the runs table.\n\n`DataTable`''s `facets` now accepts a control WITHOUT a row accessor, rendered\nbut not applied. That is what lets a server-applied dimension - one with\nnothing on the row to match, like the health-check system, whose selection\nswaps the data source - stay in the table''s bar instead of forcing a second\nbar onto the page.\n\nFixes a trap the move exposed. With server-side filtering an empty `data`\nmeans either \"none exist\" or \"none match\", and three of these pages showed\ntheir onboarding empty state either way. Automation''s run history was worse:\nit replaced the whole table when the server returned nothing, taking the\nfilter controls with it, so a filter matching nothing could not be cleared.\nEach now suppresses `emptyState` while a filter is active and offers a \"no\nmatches, clear filters\" state instead.\n\nThree surfaces keep an external bar deliberately, each narrowing more than\none list: the catalog toolbar (browse grid + three manage tabs), the\nautomation list (one table per accordion group), and the drawer''s source\ncontrol (it scopes the charts as well as the runs, like the date range beside\nit). The history detail page''s list is not a DataTable at all.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* fix(ui): keep a filter-adjacent control in the filter row, not at the page edge\n\nIncidents and maintenances put their \"Show resolved\" / \"Show completed\"\ntoggle in `toolbar`, which DataTable right-aligns because that slot is for\nACTIONS. The result was the status dropdown pinned to the far left and its\ncompanion checkbox to the far right, reading as two unrelated controls.\n\nAdds `filterExtras`, which renders inside the filter row beside the facets -\nthe right home for a control that belongs with the filters but is not a facet:\na date range, a density toggle, or a boolean that WIDENS the list. Both pages\nmove onto it, and `toolbar` is documented as actions-only.\n\nAlso corrects the documentation of what `filterValue` on a column does. It\ncolocates the DECLARATION - the filter sits beside that column''s `cell` and\n`sortValue`, so the three cannot drift - and does NOT move the control to the\ncolumn''s header. Every filter renders in one shared bar above the table, in\ncolumn order. A previous commit message described this as the control\n\"sitting with the column it filters\", which was wrong.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* fix(incident): give the row one coloured dimension, not two\n\nAn incident row showed a coloured severity AND a coloured status, so two\ncompeting colour scales sat on one line: a red \"Investigating\" beside an\namber \"Major\" reads as a contradiction rather than as two independent facts.\nThe lifecycle is now stated in words on a neutral pill, leaving severity to\ncarry the row''s hue - the pill, the leading dot and the card accent stripe.\n\nThe public status page has always rendered the same incident this way\n(severity tinted, status on a muted chip), so the manage, detail, overview\nand system-history views now agree with what a customer sees.\n\n`presentIncidentStatus` no longer returns a tone. Nothing consumed it once\nthe badge went neutral, and a returned-but-unused tone is precisely how the\nstatus gets re-coloured later; a test pins its absence.\n\nAudited every surface that maps a domain value to a status tone. Maintenance\nis NOT the same bug: it has no severity field at all, so its lifecycle is its\nonly dimension and nothing competes with it - as for health checks, SLOs,\ngitops syncs, notifications, secrets, integrations and dependencies. The rule\nthat explains all of them is now written on the shared tone module: at most\nONE coloured dimension per row; urgency wins where both exist; an aggregate\nbucket card is the deliberate exception.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* refactor(ui): consolidate eight status pills into one\n\n`StatusPill` moves into `@checkstack/ui`, replacing six near-identical local\ncomponents (announcements, incidents, maintenance, health checks,\nnotifications, script packages) and three hand-rolled inline chips (the\npublic status page''s event card and detail page, the announcements widget).\nThey differed only in `label` vs `children`, whether they forwarded\n`className`, and whether they set `shrink-0` - they agreed on everything that\nmattered.\n\nThe shared pill absorbs the variations rather than flattening them:\n\n- `tone=\"neutral\"`, which had been hand-rolled in three places since the \"at\n  most one coloured dimension per row\" rule landed. Drops the dot: with no\n  hue to encode, a grey dot adds nothing.\n- `size=\"sm\"` for dense contexts, previously inline `text-[11px]` chips.\n- `shrink-0` unconditionally - a pill squashed by a greedy sibling is\n  unreadable, and its text is the accessible encoding of the status.\n\nDomain plugins keep their thin wrappers (`HealthStatusPill`,\n`getIncidentSeverityBadge`, ...): mapping a domain value to a tone and label\nIS domain knowledge, and only the chip moved.\n\nTwo related duplications found in the same sweep also go: the dependency\nplugin hand-wrote the pill''s classes inline in a `getImpactBadge` switch\nduplicated across its alert banner AND its editor - now one `ImpactBadge`\nover the tone mapping its own logic module already owned - and its private\ntone table now sources the triad from the shared one.\n\n`status-page-frontend`''s local `StatusPill` becomes `PublicStatusPill`: it is\nkeyed by the public status enum and draws from that enum''s own visual tokens,\nso it is a different component and the name now says so.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* feat(incident,maintenance,automation): let people search these lists\n\nAll three management lists had their search box switched off on my assumption\nthat you find a record by status rather than by typing its name. Every one of\nthem shows a title or name column, and none of the underlying queries\npaginate - the full set is already in the browser - so there was nothing to\ngain by withholding search.\n\n- Incidents gain search AND a severity filter. Severity had a column and a\n  sort but no filter, so \"show me the criticals\" meant reading the table.\n  Options are ordered by impact to match the column''s sort; deriving them\n  would have sorted alphabetically as critical / major / minor.\n- Maintenances gain search.\n- Automations gain search, page-level rather than table-level: that list\n  renders one table per group, so a table-owned box would search only its own\n  group and a match inside a collapsed group could never surface. Filtering\n  ahead of the grouping also makes empty groups disappear instead of leaving\n  a wall of empty accordions.\n\nEach search matches the title/name AND the second line the row renders (a\ndescription, or the group label), so it matches the words you can see.\n\nChecked the remaining `searchable={false}` tables: run-history and\ndelivery-attempt tables have no name to type, the catalog manage tabs are\nsearched by their shared toolbar, and the API-docs table lists one endpoint''s\nparameters. Those stay off deliberately.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* refactor(ui): source every status tone from the one shared table\n\nNineteen plugin modules each re-declared the tone-to-class table verbatim,\nsome reproducing every field of the shared one. They now take their classes\nfrom `pillToneStyles` while keeping their own domain mapping - which value\nmeans which tone - since that is real domain knowledge and is unit tested. A\nrepo-wide search for a hand-written triad row now returns only\n`core/ui/src/components/status-tone.ts`.\n\nSeveral hand-rolled pills went with them onto the shared `StatusPill`: the\nautomation run pill, the satellite status badge, the notification channel\npill, the SLO objective pill and both AI tool-card pills.\n\nFour rows stay local ON PURPOSE, each commented, because they are not the\nshared tone despite looking like it:\n\n- The dashboard''s `info` uses the `--info` token, a genuinely different hue\n  from `--status-info` (light: 217 91% 60% vs 214 90% 45%).\n- Integrations'' and notifications'' `unknown`/`neutral` are the muted\n  treatment - the ABSENCE of a tone - not the shared grey.\n- The queue''s \"processing\" uses opacity-softened muted classes matching\n  neither the shared table nor the pill''s neutral.\n\nOne real divergence was found and deliberately NOT normalised: the system\nincident panel borders at `/30` where the shared table uses `/20`. It is now\none documented map rather than a whole private table. Pills whose geometry\nhas no shared equivalent (the dependency canvas node''s animated halo, the\nincident panel''s compact chips, the dashboard''s non-triad signal tone) keep\ntheir markup and share only the classes.\n\nVerified the repo-wide suite is unchanged: 97 fail / 36 errors both with and\nwithout these edits (pre-existing backend HTTP/CORS and core/ui Tabs\nsuites). Scoped to the 18 affected packages: 1341 pass, 0 fail.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* refactor(ui): converge the tone exceptions that were drift, not design\n\nReviewed the four \"deliberate exceptions\" the tone de-duplication left. Three\nwere drift wearing an explanatory comment; one was genuine.\n\n- `neutralToneStyle` now ships from `@checkstack/ui`. Three plugins had each\n  written the same three muted strings by hand. It sits BESIDE\n  `pillToneStyles`, not in it - the absence of a tone is not a tone - and\n  `StatusPill`''s `tone=\"neutral\"` renders it.\n- Dashboard signals take the status ladder''s blue. In a single record `error`\n  and `warn` came from the ladder while `info` reached for the general-purpose\n  `--info` accent, so the same \"Watch\" signal appeared in two different blues\n  across the problem card, its chip and the fleet-header bar. `--status-info`\n  is also the darker L45 blue chosen so its text stays readable on exactly the\n  light card these render on. Its test is updated to the new expectation, with\n  a comment on why.\n- The system incident panel borders at `/20` like every other tinted border.\n  That was the last class-string divergence in the tone system; the one-off\n  map documenting it is gone.\n- The queue''s KPI tile and job-state pill use the shared neutral instead of\n  each carrying a slightly softer private variant, which made \"carries no\n  signal\" look like two different things on one page.\n\nKept, because it is real: `--info` and `--status-info` stay separate tokens.\nThe first is the general semantic palette (beside `--success`/`--warning`),\nthe second the colourblind-safe status ladder with its own contrast\nrationale. Non-status surfaces - `Alert`, plugin-type chips - keep the\ngeneral accent.\n\nFull suite unchanged from baseline: 97 fail / 36 errors with and without\n(pre-existing backend HTTP/CORS and core/ui Tabs suites).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* fix(healthcheck): stop labelling live satellite checks as \"Old checks\"\n\nOn a system with environments, a check assigned to BOTH the local core and a\nsatellite showed the satellite''s results under \"Old checks\" from the moment\nthe satellite first reported - while being the freshest data on the page.\n\nRoot cause: satellites are handed no environment information.\n`getAssignmentsForSatellite` returns no environments and\n`ingestSatelliteResult` has no `environmentId` field, so its insert\n(service.ts) omits the column and EVERY satellite result is written\nenv-less. The local executor meanwhile resolves the effective environments\nand writes a concrete id per run.\n\nThe overview then decided orphanhood structurally: an env-less slice must be\nhistorical, because a check that fans out per environment cannot still be\nwriting env-less runs. True while only the local executor wrote runs; false\nthe moment a satellite does.\n\nThe rule now means what its name says. An env-less slice is old only when it\nhas actually gone quiet - judged from its own run timestamps against the\ncheck interval, with five missed intervals of slack and a ten-minute floor,\nso a slow or backing-off probe is never mistaken for a dead one. A slice that\nhas never run is pending, not old. A concrete environment that left the\nsystem, or was disabled for the assignment, is still called old immediately:\nthat verdict is certain and making it wait would only delay a correct label.\n\nThe new condition is purely additive, so it can only REDUCE false \"old\"\nlabels - it can never newly mark a slice old.\n\nFixes the mislabelling only. The underlying gap - satellites getting no\nenvironment context, so they contribute no per-environment health - is the\nseparate change agreed with the reporter.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* feat(healthcheck,satellite): run satellite checks per environment, scopably\n\nSatellites were handed no environment information, so every result they\nreported was stored env-less. On a system with environments, satellite checks\ncontributed nothing to per-environment health - and were labelled \"Old\nchecks\" for it until the preceding fix.\n\nA satellite now fans out exactly as the local executor does:\n\n- `getAssignmentsForSatellite` resolves each assignment''s effective\n  environments and ships them with the assignment.\n- The agent schedules ONE run per environment (`expandRuns`) and reports each\n  result with its `environmentId`, so per-environment history, charts and\n  rollups finally include satellite results.\n- Satellite collectors now receive the `environment` run-context block, so\n  `{{ environment.<key> }}` resolves there exactly as it does locally.\n\nCrucially a satellite can be SCOPED to specific environments. Without that,\nfan-out alone would make every satellite probe every environment: a\nstaging-network satellite would start failing prod checks it cannot route to,\nand a single per-environment slice would merge results from satellites in\ndifferent networks. The new `satelliteEnvironmentIds` map scopes each\nsatellite - absent key = all environments (so every existing assignment is\nunchanged), `[]` = one env-less run, a list = those ids. Narrowing is\none-way: a satellite can never widen the assignment''s own selector, which is\nenforced by resolving the assignment''s set first and intersecting after.\n\nThe migration adds the column with NO backfill: \"key absent\" already means\n\"all environments\", so NULL reproduces today''s behaviour exactly.\n\nBoth protocol fields are optional, for skew in either direction. An older\nsatellite sends no `environmentId` and its runs store env-less as before; an\nolder core sends no environments and the agent falls back to one env-less\nrun. Covered by protocol and fan-out tests.\n\nUI for the per-satellite picker follows in the next commit.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* feat(healthcheck): let each satellite be scoped to specific environments\n\nCompletes per-environment satellite execution with the UI to configure it.\n\nThe Execution panel now shows, under each ASSIGNED satellite, an\n\"All environments / Specific environments\" choice plus a per-environment\ntick list - but only when the system actually has environments, so the\ncontrol never appears with nothing to decide.\n\nChoosing \"All\" DELETES that satellite''s key rather than writing out every\ncurrent environment id: absent means \"all\", so the satellite keeps following\nenvironments added later. Switching to \"Specific\" seeds from the environments\nthe assignment itself resolves to, so the first click narrows nothing until\nthe operator unticks something - the safe direction. Ticking none is a\ndeliberate opt-out and says so in the panel: that satellite runs the check\nonce, with no environment in context.\n\nAlso documents the model on the satellites concept page, including that a\nsatellite can only narrow the assignment''s selector, never widen it, and that\nan older satellite keeps reporting env-less until it is upgraded.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* fix(healthcheck): stop reporting unmeasured systems as healthy\n\nA system whose check had never produced a run reported `healthy`, so it\nshowed green in the catalog, kept its GROUP green, and read \"operational\" on\nthe public status page. A system with no checks at all did the same.\n\n`getSystemHealthStatus` started each check at `healthy` and each system''s\naggregate at `healthy`, then only ever downgraded - and with no runs to\nexamine, nothing downgraded them. `HealthCheckStatus` had no way to express\n\"not measured\", so absence of evidence became evidence of health. For a\nmonitoring product that is the worst possible default: the one state you must\nnever invent is the reassuring one.\n\nAdds `SystemHealthStatus` with `unknown`, for systems and their checks. It is\ndeliberately NOT a run status - a run that happened is always one of the\nthree, and the database enum stays three-valued.\n\n- A check with no runs is `unknown`. A system is `unknown` when no check\n  contributed a signal; one healthy check still makes it healthy, and the\n  unmeasured sibling stays visible on the system''s own page.\n- The catalog reports `unknown` by OMISSION, which its group rollup already\n  treats as \"no signal\" - so a group with an unmeasured member stops claiming\n  health. The rollup itself was never wrong; it was being told green.\n- The status page maps it to its existing `unknown`, ignored for the overall\n  banner unless everything is unknown.\n- A first measurement records a NULL `fromStatus` - the column was already\n  nullable for exactly this case - rather than pretending health beforehand.\n- Automations matching `unhealthy` do not fire for an unmeasured system.\n- Dependency warnings keep today''s behaviour on purpose: an unmeasured\n  upstream raises no warning, and a never-run check is dropped from the\n  evaluation instead of counting as a pass.\n\nThree tests asserted the old behaviour and are updated with the reasoning,\nincluding that pausing a system''s only check now leaves it `unknown`. Paused\nfailures still do not keep a system degraded - unchanged - but a system with\nnothing running is genuinely unmeasured.\n\nFull suite unchanged from baseline: 97 fail / 36 errors with and without.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* fix(healthcheck): stop the history page calling live environments \"Removed\"\n\nThe history page showed \"Removed environment\" beside runs whose environment\nwas perfectly healthy, while opening the same run showed it correctly.\n\nIt rendered `HealthCheckRunsTable` without passing `environmentLabels` at\nall. The prop was optional, so the table had no names to resolve a run''s\nenvironment id against and fell back to its \"this environment no longer\nexists\" label. Every other caller passed it - the history page was the only\none that did not, which is exactly why the bug appeared on one screen and\nnowhere else.\n\nThe page now resolves names from every environment in the instance, like the\nother screens, and holds its rows until they load so a still-loading\nenvironment cannot flash as removed either.\n\nHardened so it cannot recur: `environmentLabels` is now REQUIRED on both run\nlists. \"Removed environment\" asserts that an id is absent from the COMPLETE\nlist, which is only sound if the complete list was supplied - and while the\nprop was optional, forgetting it produced a confident lie rather than a\nvisible gap. The three cases (env-less, named, genuinely removed) now go\nthrough one tested helper, `resolveRunEnvironmentLabel`, instead of a lookup\nthat could not distinguish \"no list\" from \"not in the list\".\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* fix(healthcheck): evaluate health per probe location, not per environment alone\n\nA check''s runs were sliced by environment only, so local and satellite runs\nfor the same environment landed in one list and reached the threshold\nevaluator as an interleaved stream. In the default `consecutive` mode the\nstreak breaks on every alternation, no threshold is ever reached, and\nevaluation falls through to its healthy default - so a satellite failing\n100% of the time read as healthy for as long as a local check succeeded\nbetween its runs.\n\nA slice is now an (environment, source) pair, evaluated on its own window\nwith worst-wins across slices. This is the rule environments already\nfollowed; the source dimension was simply never considered. Both the system\nrollup and the system overview had the bug; both are fixed.\n\nKeying slices by source also retires a location''s verdict with it: a\nde-assigned satellite (or the core after `includeLocal` is off) stops\ncounting immediately instead of dragging the rollup until its failures age\nout of the window, per-satellite environment scoping is honoured when\nresolving slices, and \"has a live environment slice\" is answered per\nlocation so a satellite scoped to run env-less keeps its slice while the\ncore fans out.\n\nThe overview shows one row per slice and names the location as soon as a\ncheck runs from more than one place. `sliceCount` counts locations too, so\na check probed from the core and one satellite contributes 2 to the\ndashboard denominator rather than 1.\n\nThe service tests cannot reproduce the original masking (their mock resolves\nruns per slice, so it never interleaves); the guard for the mechanism lives\nin state-evaluator.test.ts, which asserts that an interleaved stream\nevaluates to healthy and fails loudly if the slicing is ever re-collapsed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KiVAPXAuVtVemtYvvyPXzp\n\n* fix(maintenance): make status colours agree across every surface\n\nA \"Scheduled\" maintenance was blue on the manage/list/detail pages, amber on\nthe catalog system card, and grey on the status page block and public detail\npage. The manage surfaces went through the canonical presentMaintenanceStatus\nmapping; the other three hardcoded Tailwind classes and never consulted it -\ntwo painting grey for EVERY status (so in-progress read grey too, with the raw\n\"in progress\" enum instead of the \"In Progress\" label), the catalog card\npainting amber for every status.\n\nAll three now derive tone and label from the canonical mapping. status-page-\nfrontend gains maintenanceStatusTone/maintenanceStatusLabel, replicated against\nthe shared pillToneStyles tones - the same platform-layer arrangement\nseverityTone.ts uses, since status-page-frontend must not import the domain\nmaintenance-* packages. A system under planned maintenance now reads blue\n(informational), not grey (inert/unknown). The catalog card takes the tone of\nwhichever window leads: amber in-progress, else blue scheduled.\n\nSeparately, StatusBadge now draws from the shared status tokens (--status-*)\ninstead of the generic --success/--warning/--info palette. Those palettes\ndiffer (e.g. --info 217 91% 60% vs --status-info 214 90% 45%), so a badge and a\npill of the same tone rendered two different blues for the same meaning. Health,\nincident, SLO, maintenance, anomaly and dependency badges now share one hue per\ntone with their pills.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KiVAPXAuVtVemtYvvyPXzp\n\n* fix(notifications): render markdown in incident/maintenance update messages\n\n@stuajnht reported an email showing the raw markdown of a link instead of a\nclickable link. The email renderer was fine; the shared sanitizeUpdateMessage\n(used by both incident and maintenance backends) backslash-escaped every\nmarkdown control character and forced the message onto one blockquoted line, so\na link arrived as \\[label\\]\\(href\\) and rendered as literal text everywhere.\n\nUpdate messages are authored as markdown and render as markdown on the web, so\nthey now do the same in notifications. sanitizeUpdateMessage still normalizes\nthe text (strips non-whitespace control characters, normalizes line endings,\ncollapses blank-line runs, bounds length) but no longer escapes markdown, and\nbuildUpdateMessageSuffix appends it as its own multi-line block. Safety is\nunchanged: only SMTP emits HTML, via markdownToHtml''s allow-list; every other\nstrategy renders markdown/mrkdwn/adaptive-card or plain text, none execute HTML.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KiVAPXAuVtVemtYvvyPXzp\n\n* refactor(healthcheck): share one execution engine between core and satellite\n\nThe satellite''s health-check execution loop was a hand-maintained copy of the\ncore queue executor''s. When the core grew a per-run templating pass, the copy\nwas never updated, so system/environment custom fields ({{ system.metadata.* }},\n{{ environment.* }}) silently failed to expand on satellite runs (reported by\n@stuajnht).\n\nIntroduce @checkstack/healthcheck-execution, a lean backend-only package holding\nthe shared engine (runHealthCheckCollection): render the strategy + collector\nx-templatable fields against the run''s env/system context, build the transport\nclient, run collectors, close the client, and assemble a sink-agnostic outcome.\nBoth the core queue executor and the satellite now run through it; each keeps\nonly its genuine edges as injected hooks (DB secret resolution + migrate-on-read\non the core; JIT socket secrets on the satellite; assertion/strip vs raw result\nmapping). Templating, secret-then-template ordering, and the per-collector\nfan-out can no longer drift between the two.\n\nThe engine lives outside @checkstack/backend-api deliberately, so the platform\nfoundation does not grow more health-check-specific execution logic. Evacuating\nthe pre-existing health-check contracts and registries from backend-api (they\nare wired into core-services and the RPC context) is a separate platform change.\n\nAlso: transport sub-phase timings are now measured at the probe and surfaced by\nsatellites, so a satellite run''s metadata.timings matches a local run''s - the\ncore cannot time a probe it did not run.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KiVAPXAuVtVemtYvvyPXzp\n\n* fix(healthcheck): react to satellite results through the same core path as local runs\n\ningestSatelliteResult only inserted the run row and invalidated the cache, so a\nsatellite-detected health change fired no notifications, no automations, no\ntransition record, and no realtime signal - the local executor does all of that\nafter every run. Satellite monitoring was effectively silent.\n\nExtract the local post-run path into one shared `persistRunAndReact` (reactive\n`health` entity write, cache reconcile, realtime signal, checkCompleted/\ncheckFailed hooks, transition record, subscriber notification) and route BOTH\nthe local executor and satellite ingest through it. The host binds the service\ndeps once and passes the router a narrowed reactor, so the two callers cannot\npass different deps and drift - which is how ingest had diverged into a\nsilent, duplicated copy in the first place. Ingest now splits into\n`processSatelliteResult` (assertions + ephemeral strip + name resolution) plus\nthe shared reactive path.\n\nAlso annotate a satellite collector''s transport error as `_collectorError` on\nthe stored result, matching local runs.\n\nTests: pin that ingest routes through the shared reactor (guarding against a\nregression back to insert-only), and extract the satellite executeAssignment\ninto a testable module with tests for custom-field template expansion,\nprobe-measured timings, the _collectorError annotation, and strategy-not-loaded.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KiVAPXAuVtVemtYvvyPXzp\n\n* fix(satellite): stop ENOENT crash-loop by keeping k8s-events-common in the image\n\n@stuajnht reported satellite releases 134/135 crash-loop at startup with\n`ENOENT reading .../@checkstack/k8s-events-common` (133 works). The k8s-events\ntelemetry pull executor imports that package eagerly at module load, but the\nsatellite image pruned it away, so the agent died before running any check.\n\nTwo root causes, both fixed:\n\n- k8s-events-common lived under plugins/, unlike its sibling telemetry contracts\n  (metricstream/logstream/tracestream-common) which are in core/. A core package\n  (the satellite) importing a plugins/ package is a dependency-direction\n  violation; it now lives in core/ alongside its siblings.\n- The image prune deleted every plugin except healthcheck-*/collector-* backends\n  by name pattern, silently dropping anything else the satellite needs. It is now\n  driven by the dependency graph: it keeps the transitive runtime-dependency\n  closure of the satellite plus every plugin it loads dynamically (those backends\n  as extra roots, so they are never pruned by accident). The \"which plugins does\n  the satellite load\" rule is a single shared predicate consumed by both the\n  runtime loader and the build-time prune, so they cannot drift. The workspace\n  discovery is likewise shared with the tsconfig-reference generator.\n\nVerified by building Dockerfile.satellite and starting the image: it loads all\n15 strategies + 28 collectors, runs the k8s-events executor registration without\nENOENT, and reaches normal core-connection retries instead of crash-looping.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KiVAPXAuVtVemtYvvyPXzp\n\n* fix(status-page): custom-domain serving, event rendering, announcement public remote\n\nResolves the reported Status Pages feedback:\n\n#1 Custom domains\n  - Route on X-Forwarded-Host (first hop) then Host, via a shared\n    resolveRequestHost, so a page behind a Host-rewriting edge proxy resolves\n    (routing previously read the raw Host and served the admin app).\n  - Emit public.html in the frontend build: the backend serves it to a\n    custom-domain host but it was never produced (since #341), so the SPA\n    fallback 404''d. Verified over real HTTP end to end.\n\n#2 Announcements block rendered blank on the public bundle\n  - Ship announcement-frontend as a public Module Federation remote\n    (checkstack.publicRemote) exposing a lean renderer-only entry, so the lean\n    public bundle (custom domain AND same-origin /statuspage/view) can loadRemote\n    it. Backend discovery syncs a plugins row for it; `bun run build:public-remotes`\n    builds every publicRemote plugin, wired into the Dockerfile and e2e pretest.\n  - Verified in a real browser: the remote loads over MF and the block renders.\n\n#3-#6 Detail pages\n  - Colour the incident/maintenance lifecycle status on its own line\n    (new incidentStatusTone, mirroring maintenanceStatusTone) on block + detail.\n  - Render update-message and description markdown (was raw source).\n  - Widget resolveDetail returns the item with ALL public updates (no maxUpdates\n    cap) + its description; the status-page service calls it, gated by the same\n    anti-enumeration boundary as the block.\n\nDocs: document the edge Host / X-Forwarded-Host requirement with reverse-proxy\nexamples on the custom-domain guide + docker-compose page; status-pages\narchitecture page covers resolveDetail and publicRemote.\n\nTests: live-behaviour widget + service tests, a public.html build-emission guard,\nand Playwright specs that render the REAL public bundle for #2-#6 (the published\npage is seeded via the API; the builder add-block UI is covered by\nstatus-page.spec). Also de-flake the infrastructure.empty queue sub-tab test\n(it asserted a fresh boot has no active/failed system jobs, which races).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KiVAPXAuVtVemtYvvyPXzp\n\n* fix(docker): copy top-level scripts into the builder stage\n\nThe builder stage runs `bun run build:public-remotes` (added for the\nannouncement public MF remote) but never COPYd the top-level `scripts/` dir, so\nthe Docker image build failed with `Module not found scripts/build-public-remotes.ts`.\nCopy `scripts/` into the builder (the runtime stage does not need it).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KiVAPXAuVtVemtYvvyPXzp\n\n* chore(deps): upgrade astro to v7 and starlight to 0.41\n\nClears the astro security advisories (GHSA-4g3v-8h47-v7g6, CVE-2026-59729;\nfixed in astro 7.1.0) that were gating the dependency-graph security check.\nastro 6 -> 7.1.3, @astrojs/starlight 0.38 -> 0.41.3 (peer astro ^7.0.2), sharp\n0.33 -> 0.35 (matches the root override). Migrates the docs sidebar to the\nStarlight v0.39+ schema (an autogenerated group''s `autogenerate` config must be\nwrapped in an `items` array). Docs build verified: 163 pages built under Astro 7.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KiVAPXAuVtVemtYvvyPXzp\n\n* fix(announcement): don''t restrict the published package to dist\n\n`core/announcement-frontend` is a CORE plugin consumed from SOURCE (`main`:\nsrc/index.tsx) - the admin app imports it directly - so `\"files\": [\"dist\"]`\n(copied from the third-party remote template) excluded `src/` from the published\npackage and broke the integration dev-server boot (\"could not resolve\n@checkstack/announcement-frontend\"). The public MF remote is served from the\nworkspace `dist/` on disk, not the npm package, so the field was never needed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KiVAPXAuVtVemtYvvyPXzp\n\n* fix(scripts): make tsconfig-references cycle pruning deterministic\n\nThe dependency-cycle back-edge pruning in `generate-tsconfig-references`\ndepended on package DISCOVERY order (both the DFS start-node order and child\norder), so a cycle (satellite-backend <-> healthcheck-backend) could prune a\nDIFFERENT edge in CI than the committed tsconfigs reflect - failing\n`typecheck:references:check` non-deterministically. Sort both orders\nlexicographically so the pruned set is identical in every environment.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KiVAPXAuVtVemtYvvyPXzp\n\n* fix(scripts): use toSorted() in cycle pruning (lint)\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KiVAPXAuVtVemtYvvyPXzp\n\n* fix(e2e): ship the announcement remote to e2e + fix health/empty-state assumptions\n\nFour E2E failures, all fixed:\n\n- Announcement spec: the `e2e_build` CI job built only frontend + docs and\n  uploaded only those dists, so the shards never had the announcement MF remote\n  and the public bundle''s loadRemote 404''d. It now runs `build:public-remotes`\n  and uploads `core/announcement-frontend/dist`.\n\n- catalog + incident `expandBrowseSections`: waited on the catalog browse health\n  rollup PILL, but this branch made an unmeasured system read \"unknown\", which\n  renders NO rollup pill - so the wait timed out. Replaced with a poll that\n  opens every collapsed section until stable (a manual open overrides the\n  all-healthy auto-collapse), independent of any pill.\n\n- automation \"runs page renders an empty state\": asserted the FILTERED empty\n  state (\"No runs match this filter\"), but a fresh automation with no active\n  filter shows \"No runs yet\". Accept either, and allow the runs query to load.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KiVAPXAuVtVemtYvvyPXzp\n\n* fix(auth): let team members and managers see and run their own team\n\nTeam read procedures were gated on the GLOBAL auth.teams.read rule, so a\nteam-scoped user (a per-team ReBAC grant, no global rule) was 403''d on\ngetTeams and denied listObjectRelations - breaking the \"Who can change this\"\nmodal for a team manager and locking managers out of their own team.\n\nBackend: the read/metadata procedures (getTeams, getTeam, listObjectRelations,\nlistObjectRelationsBulk, listSubjectRelations, listTeamCreateGrants,\nsearchUsers, resolveResourceNames, getResourceKinds) drop the global gate and\nscope in the handler - a global auth.teams.read holder or service sees every\nteam; everyone else sees only the team(s) they are a member or manager of.\ngetTeam returns undefined for a team with no stake (no existence leak) and\nlistObjectRelations hides grants from a caller with no stake while still\nreturning the public flag (a successful empty response, never a 403). Write\nprocedures keep enforcing assertTeamManagementAccess, so a team manager can\nmanage their own team; create/delete team stay admin-only. hasGlobalTeamsRead\nnow uses isAccessRuleSatisfied (so * and teams.manage both count).\n\nFrontend: the Teams page and nav are shown to a global teams.read holder OR any\nmember/manager of at least one team (not unconditionally). A new isInAnyTeam\nflag on the app-wide accessRules query (member OR manager, so manager-only\nteams count) is threaded through the sidebar''s nav model and exposed to every\nroute''s isVisible predicate via a new isInAnyTeam context field.\n\nDocs and the AI docs index updated to match.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KiVAPXAuVtVemtYvvyPXzp\n\n* chore(changeset): cover @checkstack/k8s-events-common relocation\n\nThe satellite ENOENT fix moved k8s-events-common from plugins/ to core/,\nrenaming its src files, but the changeset only bumped @checkstack/satellite.\nAdd k8s-events-common (patch) to the same changeset so changeset coverage is\ncomplete.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KiVAPXAuVtVemtYvvyPXzp\n\n* fix(healthcheck): stop unmeasured (unknown) systems reading as \"Degraded\"\n\nA system with no checks (or none run yet) has health status \"unknown\", but two\ndisplay paths only excluded \"healthy\" and let every other status fall through to\nthe amber \"Degraded\" label - so a check-less system falsely showed \"Degraded\" on\nits detail page/catalog rows and as a dashboard problem card, with no incident,\nfailing check, or failing dependency behind it.\n\n- deriveHealthcheckSignals (healthcheck-common): omit \"unknown\" alongside\n  \"healthy\" so the dashboard raises no signal for an unmeasured system. Its doc\n  already claimed both were omitted; the code only skipped healthy.\n- SystemHealthBadge (healthcheck-frontend): return no badge for \"unknown\". The\n  decision is extracted into a pure resolveHealthBadge helper with unit tests.\n\nThe dependency \"Degrading impact\" chips are unrelated (edge config, not live\nstate); the warning engine already maps an unmeasured upstream to operational.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KiVAPXAuVtVemtYvvyPXzp\n\n* feat(auth): delegate resource team-access editing to resource managers\n\nEditing a resource''s team access (\"Who can change this\": add/remove teams,\ntoggle Manage/Private) was gated on the GLOBAL auth.teams.manage rule on both\nfrontend and backend, so only a platform admin could do it - even a member of a\nteam that already manages the resource saw the editor read-only.\n\nwriteRelation/removeRelation/setObjectPublic now authorize per-object: the caller\nmay edit a resource''s team access when they can MANAGE that specific resource - a\nglobal auth.teams.manage admin, a holder of the resource''s own <type>.manage rule\n(e.g. catalog.system.manage), or a member of a team with an editor/owner grant on\nit. A viewer-only team (or no grant, no global rule) is read-only and CANNOT\nelevate its own or another team''s access. The check reuses the ReBAC engine\n(tupleStore.check action=manage), so team-private resources are respected: a\nglobal resource-manager not on a granted team cannot reach a private resource -\nonly a teams.manage admin or a granted team can. The first grant on an unscoped\nresource still needs a global rule.\n\nA new canManageObjectAccess query runs the SAME authorization; TeamAccessEditor\ngates its write controls on it instead of the global rule, so the UI matches what\na write would accept (no drift). The backend re-checks every write.\n\nBREAKING CHANGE: granting/revoking a team''s access to a resource is no longer\nadmin-only - it is delegated to whoever can manage that resource.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KiVAPXAuVtVemtYvvyPXzp\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"b00e5e86a3e83af27a47a1e4278e8084e58f5aff","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/enyineer/checkstack/git/trees/b00e5e86a3e83af27a47a1e4278e8084e58f5aff"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/enyineer/checkstack/git/commits/be74b01860dfc7602fd3b4e3d64ee1c332f6a5e9","verification":{"payload":"tree b00e5e86a3e83af27a47a1e4278e8084e58f5aff\nparent ca6c4c70fa2ed6f09e7b1dc0d1494d838728775c\nauthor Nico <nico.enking@gmail.com> 1784883744 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1784883744 +0200\n\nfix: feedback round 1 — status pages, health/satellite, tables & tones (#485)\n\n* fix(announcement): render info severity in the blue info tone, not grey\n\nThe announcement plugin kept a private copy of the status tone table that\nonly declared ok/warn/down/unknown - the blue `info` tone from\n`@checkstack/ui` was missing entirely. `severityToTone(\"info\")` therefore\nfell through to `unknown`, so an info-severity announcement''s pill badge,\ncard accent stripe and global banner all rendered `*-status-unknown` grey,\nreading as \"inert/disabled\" rather than \"informational\". This affected the\nannouncements manage page, the dashboard and the public status-page widget.\n`AnnouncementBanner` carried a second, independent severity-to-class switch\nwith the same grey default.\n\n- Map `info` severity onto the `info` tone, matching the ramp incidents and\n  status pages already use, and alias `StatusTone` to the shared\n  `StatusPillTone` so the plugin can no longer lose a tone.\n- Delete the private tone table; re-export the shared `pillToneStyles`.\n- Derive the banner''s classes from `severityToTone` + the shared table so\n  banner and pill can no longer drift.\n- Add `text` / `tint` / `border` / `tintHover` class sets to\n  `pillToneStyles` (additive) so banner-like surfaces come from one table,\n  and document them plus the announcement mapping in the Storybook story.\n\nAdds a regression test asserting `info` never resolves to `unknown`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* fix(telemetry): stop anonymous page loads from logging auth errors\n\nOpening the app unauthenticated printed an error-level stack trace per\nstream plugin (\"RPC /api/metricstream/listLinkedStreamStatuses failed:\nAuthentication required\"). Two independent causes:\n\n- The dashboard is reachable anonymously (catalog reads are public, as are\n  the healthcheck / incident / SLO / anomaly signal sources), but the three\n  stream plugins'' `listLinkedStreamStatuses` is authenticated-only. Their\n  dashboard signal fillers queried it regardless of the caller, so every\n  anonymous page load fired three requests that could only come back 401.\n- A contract-level 4xx was logged at error with a full stack trace. That is\n  the authorization layer working as designed, and the access-log middleware\n  already reports every 4xx at warn with method, path and status.\n\nFixes:\n\n- Gate the linked-stream status lookup on the caller being authenticated,\n  and hold the dashboard''s \"loading\" report while the session resolves so\n  the overview cannot flash \"all healthy\" and then re-skeleton.\n- Log a 4xx `ORPCError` at debug without a stack (`isClientRejection`);\n  5xx keeps its loud error-level logging.\n\nThe three fillers were the same component apart from their client, source\nid and deriver, so the fetch/chunk/merge/report machinery moved into a\nshared `useLinkedStreamSignals` hook in `@checkstack/telemetry-frontend`.\nSide effect: the tracestream filler''s query is now namespaced under its\nplugin id like the other two, so the signal auto-invalidator refreshes it.\n\nVerified against a running backend: an anonymous POST to the procedure now\nproduces one warn access-log line and no stack, and an anonymous page load\nproduces no 4xx at all.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* feat(announcement): sortable, filterable manage table with deliberate lifecycle tones\n\nTwo changes to the announcements manage page.\n\n1. Lifecycle states get chosen colours instead of accidental grey.\n\n\"Scheduled\", \"Expired\" and \"Inactive\" all fell through a `default:` arm in\n`statusToTone` to the neutral grey `unknown` tone, so a scheduled\nannouncement was indistinguishable from an inert one and none of the three\nhad been picked on purpose. Colour is now split by what it answers:\n\n- A row is coloured by its SEVERITY (the leading dot, the mobile card\n  stripe), matching the banner, the dashboard card and the status-page\n  widget, which already worked that way.\n- A row''s lifecycle is stated in words on a neutral pill, so it no longer\n  puts a second, competing colour scale on the same line.\n- The stat strip keeps lifecycle colour, because each card IS a bucket:\n  active green, scheduled informational blue (deliberately not amber, which\n  means \"degraded\" product-wide), expired and inactive the inert grey - now\n  by explicit decision rather than by omission.\n\n2. The table sorts and filters.\n\n- Every column except Actions sorts. Severity by impact, status by\n  lifecycle, Created by raw timestamp rather than its \"3 days ago\" prose,\n  and the icon columns by the label their tooltip shows.\n- Title search plus severity / status / visibility facets, a Clear\n  affordance and a filtered-empty state. Facet values are parsed against\n  the schemas that define them, so an unrecognised value degrades to\n  unconstrained instead of becoming a filter nothing matches.\n- Reordering moved into its own sortable \"Order\" column showing each row''s\n  1-based position, which is what makes the arrows legible under another\n  sort. The arrows are disabled while a filter hides rows, since the\n  neighbour being swapped with would be off-screen - the rule the catalog''s\n  Groups tab already uses. The table''s \"deliberately not sortable\" note is\n  obsolete: showing the canonical position removes the constraint.\n\nAlso fixes a pre-existing panel-in-panel (the table painted its own\nbordered surface inside an already-opaque Card) by passing `surface={false}`,\nand places the filters in the CardHeader, the shape the automation list and\nruns pages converged on for a full-bleed table in a `p-0` CardContent.\n\n20 new tests cover the facet logic, the per-facet parsers and the sort ranks.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* feat(ui): give DataTable native facet filtering with URL-persisted state\n\nSearch and \"narrow by status/severity/type\" had no home in DataTable, which\nowned only a free-text box whose query lived in internal state a page could\nnot observe. Any surface needing to know what was filtered - to gate a\ncontrol, to render its own empty state, to put the view in a shareable link -\nabandoned the built-in search wholesale and hand-rolled everything. A survey\nof all 33 table-bearing surfaces found 18 rendering filter UI outside\nDataTable against 17 using only its built-in search, and of the 12 with real\nfacets only 2 routed them through the component at all. Zero mixed the two:\nit was all-in or all-out, every time. That produced six different renderings\nof the same select and three different \"show everything\" sentinels.\n\nDataTable now accepts:\n\n- `facets`: declarative {id, label, options, value} filters rendered beside\n  the search box, ANDed with each other and the search, with a Clear\n  affordance and a noResultsState that fires on facet emptiness.\n- `filters`/`onFiltersChange`/`onClearFilters`: the state is controllable, so\n  a page can observe it. Omit them and the table owns it internally, which\n  keeps the simple case free of routing.\n- `surface={false}` additionally insets the filter bar with a separating\n  rule, so a table nested full-bleed in a page''s Card no longer has its\n  controls flush against the card''s edges.\n\nNew exports:\n\n- `useDataTableFilters` persists to the URL, so a filtered view is shareable,\n  survives reload, and returns intact from a row''s detail page. Exposes\n  `active` for gating controls a filtered view makes ambiguous, a `debounced`\n  variant for server-side query inputs, and `paramPrefix` for two filtered\n  tables on one page.\n- `DataTableFilterBar` for list surfaces that are not tables.\n- `useDebouncedValue`, previously copied verbatim into six packages, each\n  with a comment noting no shared version existed.\n\nThe announcements table is migrated onto it as the first consumer and drops\nits local filter module. Docs updated - the page previously legislated the\nopposite (\"let DataTable own only the free-text search\").\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* feat(ui): add pill facets, and migrate automation onto the shared filter bar\n\nFirst wave of migrations onto DataTable''s native facets.\n\n- `DataTableFacet` gains `kind: \"select\" | \"pills\"`. A segmented pill row is\n  the right control for two or three short options a reader benefits from\n  seeing at a glance, and several surfaces had independently built one, so\n  the shared bar renders that variant rather than forcing every list into a\n  dropdown. Both variants share one state, sentinel and URL round-trip, and\n  the pills set `aria-pressed` - which two of the hand-rolled groups they\n  replace had omitted, leaving screen-reader users unable to tell which\n  filter was live.\n- `parsedFacetValue` reads a selection back as a domain value by parsing it\n  against the schema that defines it. Facet state is stringly-typed because\n  it round-trips through the URL, but a server-side filter needs the narrow\n  union its query input declares; parsing rather than casting means a stale\n  link degrades to unconstrained instead of smuggling an unknown value into\n  a request.\n- AutomationListPage and RunsPage drop their hand-rolled pill rows. Their\n  filters now persist to the URL, so a link to \"the failed runs of this\n  automation\" reopens filtered. RunsPage also gains the `surface={false}` it\n  was missing, fixing a panel-in-panel the survey flagged.\n- `useDebouncedValue` had been copied into six packages, each carrying a\n  comment noting no shared version existed. All six now import the one in\n  `@checkstack/ui`; the copies are deleted.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* feat(incident,maintenance): move status filters onto the shared filter bar\n\nBoth config pages carried the same hand-rolled pair: a status `<Select>` with\nits own \"all\" sentinel, and a raw `<input type=\"checkbox\">`. They were copies\nof each other down to the container''s class list.\n\nBoth now render `DataTableFilterBar`, with the status facet''s options and\nlabels derived from the plugin''s own status enum and its badge presenter - so\nthe dropdown and the rows can never disagree about what a status is called.\nThe selection persists to the URL, so a link to \"the incidents being\ninvestigated\" reopens filtered.\n\nThe \"Show resolved\" / \"Show completed\" toggles are deliberately NOT modelled\nas facets. A facet NARROWS; these WIDEN the list to include rows the endpoint\nexcludes by default, so expressing them as facets would invert their meaning.\nThey ride in the filter bar''s `children` slot instead - shared rendering,\nhonest semantics - and pick up the shared `Checkbox` in place of the raw\ninput.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* feat(catalog,healthcheck): finish the migration onto the shared filter bar\n\nCompletes the consolidation: every faceted list surface now renders one\ncontrol set over one state model.\n\n`@checkstack/ui` gains what these two surfaces genuinely needed, rather than\neither of them forking again:\n\n- `DataTableFacetControl` splits a facet''s PRESENTATIONAL half from its row\n  accessor. The catalog''s matching cannot be a `value(row): string` - a\n  system belongs to several groups and carries several tags, its health lives\n  in a separate status map, and the same three controls also narrow GROUPS, a\n  different row type. Supplying fake accessors would have been the hack.\n- `disabled`/`disabledReason` keep a control present-but-unavailable (the\n  catalog''s health filter before a health source is installed). A disabled\n  control says the capability exists and what would unlock it, which an\n  absent one cannot, and it keeps the parameter declared so a selection\n  arriving on a link still constrains.\n- A facet option may carry a `tone`, applied while selected. Reserved for a\n  dimension that genuinely IS a status: the run filter''s green Healthy / red\n  Failing is the product''s vocabulary, and the hand-rolled pills being\n  replaced carried exactly that colour. A shared control that could not\n  express it would be a downgrade on the surface where colour means most.\n  Tone never affects matching (guarded by a test).\n\nMigrated:\n\n- Catalog: `CatalogBrowseToolbar` becomes a thin wrapper over the shared bar;\n  the density toggle rides in `children`, since it narrows nothing and as a\n  facet would sit behind Clear, where \"clear filters\" would silently reset\n  row height. One toolbar still drives the browse grid and all three manage\n  tabs; GroupsTab''s reorder arrows stay gated on the filtered state.\n  Environments were filtered by an ad-hoc substring match in the page and now\n  go through the shared logic, matching descriptions too.\n- Health checks: the list toolbar (a self-declared copy of the catalog''s) and\n  both hand-rolled pill groups are deleted. Run-history filters gain URL\n  persistence, so a filtered run view is shareable, and both pill groups gain\n  the `aria-pressed` and labelled group they lacked.\n\nEvery existing URL parameter is preserved and guarded by tests, so links\nshared before the migration still reopen the same view - including the\ncatalog''s per-system \"view health checks\" link, whose server-side\nauthorization path is deliberately kept as a control with no row accessor.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* feat(ui): make filtering part of the column contract\n\nFiltering joins sorting and searching on `DataTableColumn`: providing\n`filterValue` is what makes a column filterable, with no separate boolean\nflag, exactly as `sortValue` makes it sortable.\n\nA status column already reads the row for `sortValue` and renders it in\n`cell`. Declaring the filter there too states the value ONCE, so the badge,\nthe sort and the filter cannot drift apart - previously the same value had to\nbe repeated in a standalone facet.\n\n`filterOptions` is optional. Omitted, the options are DERIVED from the\ndistinct values present in the data, sorted and labelled by the raw value.\nDeclare them when the raw values are not what a person should read\n(`authenticated` -> \"Authenticated only\"), when the order carries meaning\n(severity by impact, which deriving would sort alphabetically into critical /\ninfo / warning), or when an option must stay on offer though no row has it.\n\nOptions come from the FULL row set, never from what is currently visible.\nThat is also why a cell cannot simply publish its value upward for the table\nto collect: rows excluded by a filter never render, so reading options off\nthe rendered pills would let selecting one option delete every other option\nand strand the user with no way back. Data has to exist before the render\nthat consumes it. Guarded by a test.\n\nThe standalone `facets` prop keeps its place for a dimension no single column\nowns - the catalog''s group and tag filters match several values per row and\nnarrow two different row types. Column-derived facets render first, in column\norder, then those.\n\nAnnouncements is converted: severity, status and visibility now filter from\nthe columns that display them, and its facet module shrinks to the option\nlists whose labels and ordering cannot be derived.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* refactor(ui): move every table''s filters into the table itself\n\nThe earlier migration unified how filter controls are BUILT but left several\nrendering above their table as a detached bar, which I justified by the\nfiltering running server-side. That justification was wrong: where the\nnarrowing runs says nothing about where the control belongs, and a bar\nfloating above a card reads as unrelated to the list beneath it.\n\nAudited all 43 DataTable surfaces. Six had a bar that did not need to be\noutside; all six are now in the table''s own bar:\n\n- Incidents, maintenances and automation run history: the Status column\n  declares `filterValue`, so the control sits with the column it filters. The\n  selection still narrows the list query - that is what reduces the fetch -\n  and the column filter re-applying it over already-scoped rows is a no-op.\n- Health-check list: search, strategy and status move onto their columns; the\n  assigned-system control rides in as a control-only facet.\n- Health-check drawer: the run-status control moves into the runs table.\n\n`DataTable`''s `facets` now accepts a control WITHOUT a row accessor, rendered\nbut not applied. That is what lets a server-applied dimension - one with\nnothing on the row to match, like the health-check system, whose selection\nswaps the data source - stay in the table''s bar instead of forcing a second\nbar onto the page.\n\nFixes a trap the move exposed. With server-side filtering an empty `data`\nmeans either \"none exist\" or \"none match\", and three of these pages showed\ntheir onboarding empty state either way. Automation''s run history was worse:\nit replaced the whole table when the server returned nothing, taking the\nfilter controls with it, so a filter matching nothing could not be cleared.\nEach now suppresses `emptyState` while a filter is active and offers a \"no\nmatches, clear filters\" state instead.\n\nThree surfaces keep an external bar deliberately, each narrowing more than\none list: the catalog toolbar (browse grid + three manage tabs), the\nautomation list (one table per accordion group), and the drawer''s source\ncontrol (it scopes the charts as well as the runs, like the date range beside\nit). The history detail page''s list is not a DataTable at all.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* fix(ui): keep a filter-adjacent control in the filter row, not at the page edge\n\nIncidents and maintenances put their \"Show resolved\" / \"Show completed\"\ntoggle in `toolbar`, which DataTable right-aligns because that slot is for\nACTIONS. The result was the status dropdown pinned to the far left and its\ncompanion checkbox to the far right, reading as two unrelated controls.\n\nAdds `filterExtras`, which renders inside the filter row beside the facets -\nthe right home for a control that belongs with the filters but is not a facet:\na date range, a density toggle, or a boolean that WIDENS the list. Both pages\nmove onto it, and `toolbar` is documented as actions-only.\n\nAlso corrects the documentation of what `filterValue` on a column does. It\ncolocates the DECLARATION - the filter sits beside that column''s `cell` and\n`sortValue`, so the three cannot drift - and does NOT move the control to the\ncolumn''s header. Every filter renders in one shared bar above the table, in\ncolumn order. A previous commit message described this as the control\n\"sitting with the column it filters\", which was wrong.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* fix(incident): give the row one coloured dimension, not two\n\nAn incident row showed a coloured severity AND a coloured status, so two\ncompeting colour scales sat on one line: a red \"Investigating\" beside an\namber \"Major\" reads as a contradiction rather than as two independent facts.\nThe lifecycle is now stated in words on a neutral pill, leaving severity to\ncarry the row''s hue - the pill, the leading dot and the card accent stripe.\n\nThe public status page has always rendered the same incident this way\n(severity tinted, status on a muted chip), so the manage, detail, overview\nand system-history views now agree with what a customer sees.\n\n`presentIncidentStatus` no longer returns a tone. Nothing consumed it once\nthe badge went neutral, and a returned-but-unused tone is precisely how the\nstatus gets re-coloured later; a test pins its absence.\n\nAudited every surface that maps a domain value to a status tone. Maintenance\nis NOT the same bug: it has no severity field at all, so its lifecycle is its\nonly dimension and nothing competes with it - as for health checks, SLOs,\ngitops syncs, notifications, secrets, integrations and dependencies. The rule\nthat explains all of them is now written on the shared tone module: at most\nONE coloured dimension per row; urgency wins where both exist; an aggregate\nbucket card is the deliberate exception.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* refactor(ui): consolidate eight status pills into one\n\n`StatusPill` moves into `@checkstack/ui`, replacing six near-identical local\ncomponents (announcements, incidents, maintenance, health checks,\nnotifications, script packages) and three hand-rolled inline chips (the\npublic status page''s event card and detail page, the announcements widget).\nThey differed only in `label` vs `children`, whether they forwarded\n`className`, and whether they set `shrink-0` - they agreed on everything that\nmattered.\n\nThe shared pill absorbs the variations rather than flattening them:\n\n- `tone=\"neutral\"`, which had been hand-rolled in three places since the \"at\n  most one coloured dimension per row\" rule landed. Drops the dot: with no\n  hue to encode, a grey dot adds nothing.\n- `size=\"sm\"` for dense contexts, previously inline `text-[11px]` chips.\n- `shrink-0` unconditionally - a pill squashed by a greedy sibling is\n  unreadable, and its text is the accessible encoding of the status.\n\nDomain plugins keep their thin wrappers (`HealthStatusPill`,\n`getIncidentSeverityBadge`, ...): mapping a domain value to a tone and label\nIS domain knowledge, and only the chip moved.\n\nTwo related duplications found in the same sweep also go: the dependency\nplugin hand-wrote the pill''s classes inline in a `getImpactBadge` switch\nduplicated across its alert banner AND its editor - now one `ImpactBadge`\nover the tone mapping its own logic module already owned - and its private\ntone table now sources the triad from the shared one.\n\n`status-page-frontend`''s local `StatusPill` becomes `PublicStatusPill`: it is\nkeyed by the public status enum and draws from that enum''s own visual tokens,\nso it is a different component and the name now says so.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* feat(incident,maintenance,automation): let people search these lists\n\nAll three management lists had their search box switched off on my assumption\nthat you find a record by status rather than by typing its name. Every one of\nthem shows a title or name column, and none of the underlying queries\npaginate - the full set is already in the browser - so there was nothing to\ngain by withholding search.\n\n- Incidents gain search AND a severity filter. Severity had a column and a\n  sort but no filter, so \"show me the criticals\" meant reading the table.\n  Options are ordered by impact to match the column''s sort; deriving them\n  would have sorted alphabetically as critical / major / minor.\n- Maintenances gain search.\n- Automations gain search, page-level rather than table-level: that list\n  renders one table per group, so a table-owned box would search only its own\n  group and a match inside a collapsed group could never surface. Filtering\n  ahead of the grouping also makes empty groups disappear instead of leaving\n  a wall of empty accordions.\n\nEach search matches the title/name AND the second line the row renders (a\ndescription, or the group label), so it matches the words you can see.\n\nChecked the remaining `searchable={false}` tables: run-history and\ndelivery-attempt tables have no name to type, the catalog manage tabs are\nsearched by their shared toolbar, and the API-docs table lists one endpoint''s\nparameters. Those stay off deliberately.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* refactor(ui): source every status tone from the one shared table\n\nNineteen plugin modules each re-declared the tone-to-class table verbatim,\nsome reproducing every field of the shared one. They now take their classes\nfrom `pillToneStyles` while keeping their own domain mapping - which value\nmeans which tone - since that is real domain knowledge and is unit tested. A\nrepo-wide search for a hand-written triad row now returns only\n`core/ui/src/components/status-tone.ts`.\n\nSeveral hand-rolled pills went with them onto the shared `StatusPill`: the\nautomation run pill, the satellite status badge, the notification channel\npill, the SLO objective pill and both AI tool-card pills.\n\nFour rows stay local ON PURPOSE, each commented, because they are not the\nshared tone despite looking like it:\n\n- The dashboard''s `info` uses the `--info` token, a genuinely different hue\n  from `--status-info` (light: 217 91% 60% vs 214 90% 45%).\n- Integrations'' and notifications'' `unknown`/`neutral` are the muted\n  treatment - the ABSENCE of a tone - not the shared grey.\n- The queue''s \"processing\" uses opacity-softened muted classes matching\n  neither the shared table nor the pill''s neutral.\n\nOne real divergence was found and deliberately NOT normalised: the system\nincident panel borders at `/30` where the shared table uses `/20`. It is now\none documented map rather than a whole private table. Pills whose geometry\nhas no shared equivalent (the dependency canvas node''s animated halo, the\nincident panel''s compact chips, the dashboard''s non-triad signal tone) keep\ntheir markup and share only the classes.\n\nVerified the repo-wide suite is unchanged: 97 fail / 36 errors both with and\nwithout these edits (pre-existing backend HTTP/CORS and core/ui Tabs\nsuites). Scoped to the 18 affected packages: 1341 pass, 0 fail.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* refactor(ui): converge the tone exceptions that were drift, not design\n\nReviewed the four \"deliberate exceptions\" the tone de-duplication left. Three\nwere drift wearing an explanatory comment; one was genuine.\n\n- `neutralToneStyle` now ships from `@checkstack/ui`. Three plugins had each\n  written the same three muted strings by hand. It sits BESIDE\n  `pillToneStyles`, not in it - the absence of a tone is not a tone - and\n  `StatusPill`''s `tone=\"neutral\"` renders it.\n- Dashboard signals take the status ladder''s blue. In a single record `error`\n  and `warn` came from the ladder while `info` reached for the general-purpose\n  `--info` accent, so the same \"Watch\" signal appeared in two different blues\n  across the problem card, its chip and the fleet-header bar. `--status-info`\n  is also the darker L45 blue chosen so its text stays readable on exactly the\n  light card these render on. Its test is updated to the new expectation, with\n  a comment on why.\n- The system incident panel borders at `/20` like every other tinted border.\n  That was the last class-string divergence in the tone system; the one-off\n  map documenting it is gone.\n- The queue''s KPI tile and job-state pill use the shared neutral instead of\n  each carrying a slightly softer private variant, which made \"carries no\n  signal\" look like two different things on one page.\n\nKept, because it is real: `--info` and `--status-info` stay separate tokens.\nThe first is the general semantic palette (beside `--success`/`--warning`),\nthe second the colourblind-safe status ladder with its own contrast\nrationale. Non-status surfaces - `Alert`, plugin-type chips - keep the\ngeneral accent.\n\nFull suite unchanged from baseline: 97 fail / 36 errors with and without\n(pre-existing backend HTTP/CORS and core/ui Tabs suites).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* fix(healthcheck): stop labelling live satellite checks as \"Old checks\"\n\nOn a system with environments, a check assigned to BOTH the local core and a\nsatellite showed the satellite''s results under \"Old checks\" from the moment\nthe satellite first reported - while being the freshest data on the page.\n\nRoot cause: satellites are handed no environment information.\n`getAssignmentsForSatellite` returns no environments and\n`ingestSatelliteResult` has no `environmentId` field, so its insert\n(service.ts) omits the column and EVERY satellite result is written\nenv-less. The local executor meanwhile resolves the effective environments\nand writes a concrete id per run.\n\nThe overview then decided orphanhood structurally: an env-less slice must be\nhistorical, because a check that fans out per environment cannot still be\nwriting env-less runs. True while only the local executor wrote runs; false\nthe moment a satellite does.\n\nThe rule now means what its name says. An env-less slice is old only when it\nhas actually gone quiet - judged from its own run timestamps against the\ncheck interval, with five missed intervals of slack and a ten-minute floor,\nso a slow or backing-off probe is never mistaken for a dead one. A slice that\nhas never run is pending, not old. A concrete environment that left the\nsystem, or was disabled for the assignment, is still called old immediately:\nthat verdict is certain and making it wait would only delay a correct label.\n\nThe new condition is purely additive, so it can only REDUCE false \"old\"\nlabels - it can never newly mark a slice old.\n\nFixes the mislabelling only. The underlying gap - satellites getting no\nenvironment context, so they contribute no per-environment health - is the\nseparate change agreed with the reporter.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* feat(healthcheck,satellite): run satellite checks per environment, scopably\n\nSatellites were handed no environment information, so every result they\nreported was stored env-less. On a system with environments, satellite checks\ncontributed nothing to per-environment health - and were labelled \"Old\nchecks\" for it until the preceding fix.\n\nA satellite now fans out exactly as the local executor does:\n\n- `getAssignmentsForSatellite` resolves each assignment''s effective\n  environments and ships them with the assignment.\n- The agent schedules ONE run per environment (`expandRuns`) and reports each\n  result with its `environmentId`, so per-environment history, charts and\n  rollups finally include satellite results.\n- Satellite collectors now receive the `environment` run-context block, so\n  `{{ environment.<key> }}` resolves there exactly as it does locally.\n\nCrucially a satellite can be SCOPED to specific environments. Without that,\nfan-out alone would make every satellite probe every environment: a\nstaging-network satellite would start failing prod checks it cannot route to,\nand a single per-environment slice would merge results from satellites in\ndifferent networks. The new `satelliteEnvironmentIds` map scopes each\nsatellite - absent key = all environments (so every existing assignment is\nunchanged), `[]` = one env-less run, a list = those ids. Narrowing is\none-way: a satellite can never widen the assignment''s own selector, which is\nenforced by resolving the assignment''s set first and intersecting after.\n\nThe migration adds the column with NO backfill: \"key absent\" already means\n\"all environments\", so NULL reproduces today''s behaviour exactly.\n\nBoth protocol fields are optional, for skew in either direction. An older\nsatellite sends no `environmentId` and its runs store env-less as before; an\nolder core sends no environments and the agent falls back to one env-less\nrun. Covered by protocol and fan-out tests.\n\nUI for the per-satellite picker follows in the next commit.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* feat(healthcheck): let each satellite be scoped to specific environments\n\nCompletes per-environment satellite execution with the UI to configure it.\n\nThe Execution panel now shows, under each ASSIGNED satellite, an\n\"All environments / Specific environments\" choice plus a per-environment\ntick list - but only when the system actually has environments, so the\ncontrol never appears with nothing to decide.\n\nChoosing \"All\" DELETES that satellite''s key rather than writing out every\ncurrent environment id: absent means \"all\", so the satellite keeps following\nenvironments added later. Switching to \"Specific\" seeds from the environments\nthe assignment itself resolves to, so the first click narrows nothing until\nthe operator unticks something - the safe direction. Ticking none is a\ndeliberate opt-out and says so in the panel: that satellite runs the check\nonce, with no environment in context.\n\nAlso documents the model on the satellites concept page, including that a\nsatellite can only narrow the assignment''s selector, never widen it, and that\nan older satellite keeps reporting env-less until it is upgraded.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* fix(healthcheck): stop reporting unmeasured systems as healthy\n\nA system whose check had never produced a run reported `healthy`, so it\nshowed green in the catalog, kept its GROUP green, and read \"operational\" on\nthe public status page. A system with no checks at all did the same.\n\n`getSystemHealthStatus` started each check at `healthy` and each system''s\naggregate at `healthy`, then only ever downgraded - and with no runs to\nexamine, nothing downgraded them. `HealthCheckStatus` had no way to express\n\"not measured\", so absence of evidence became evidence of health. For a\nmonitoring product that is the worst possible default: the one state you must\nnever invent is the reassuring one.\n\nAdds `SystemHealthStatus` with `unknown`, for systems and their checks. It is\ndeliberately NOT a run status - a run that happened is always one of the\nthree, and the database enum stays three-valued.\n\n- A check with no runs is `unknown`. A system is `unknown` when no check\n  contributed a signal; one healthy check still makes it healthy, and the\n  unmeasured sibling stays visible on the system''s own page.\n- The catalog reports `unknown` by OMISSION, which its group rollup already\n  treats as \"no signal\" - so a group with an unmeasured member stops claiming\n  health. The rollup itself was never wrong; it was being told green.\n- The status page maps it to its existing `unknown`, ignored for the overall\n  banner unless everything is unknown.\n- A first measurement records a NULL `fromStatus` - the column was already\n  nullable for exactly this case - rather than pretending health beforehand.\n- Automations matching `unhealthy` do not fire for an unmeasured system.\n- Dependency warnings keep today''s behaviour on purpose: an unmeasured\n  upstream raises no warning, and a never-run check is dropped from the\n  evaluation instead of counting as a pass.\n\nThree tests asserted the old behaviour and are updated with the reasoning,\nincluding that pausing a system''s only check now leaves it `unknown`. Paused\nfailures still do not keep a system degraded - unchanged - but a system with\nnothing running is genuinely unmeasured.\n\nFull suite unchanged from baseline: 97 fail / 36 errors with and without.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* fix(healthcheck): stop the history page calling live environments \"Removed\"\n\nThe history page showed \"Removed environment\" beside runs whose environment\nwas perfectly healthy, while opening the same run showed it correctly.\n\nIt rendered `HealthCheckRunsTable` without passing `environmentLabels` at\nall. The prop was optional, so the table had no names to resolve a run''s\nenvironment id against and fell back to its \"this environment no longer\nexists\" label. Every other caller passed it - the history page was the only\none that did not, which is exactly why the bug appeared on one screen and\nnowhere else.\n\nThe page now resolves names from every environment in the instance, like the\nother screens, and holds its rows until they load so a still-loading\nenvironment cannot flash as removed either.\n\nHardened so it cannot recur: `environmentLabels` is now REQUIRED on both run\nlists. \"Removed environment\" asserts that an id is absent from the COMPLETE\nlist, which is only sound if the complete list was supplied - and while the\nprop was optional, forgetting it produced a confident lie rather than a\nvisible gap. The three cases (env-less, named, genuinely removed) now go\nthrough one tested helper, `resolveRunEnvironmentLabel`, instead of a lookup\nthat could not distinguish \"no list\" from \"not in the list\".\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ySLteubwZvRysmosv23NP\n\n* fix(healthcheck): evaluate health per probe location, not per environment alone\n\nA check''s runs were sliced by environment only, so local and satellite runs\nfor the same environment landed in one list and reached the threshold\nevaluator as an interleaved stream. In the default `consecutive` mode the\nstreak breaks on every alternation, no threshold is ever reached, and\nevaluation falls through to its healthy default - so a satellite failing\n100% of the time read as healthy for as long as a local check succeeded\nbetween its runs.\n\nA slice is now an (environment, source) pair, evaluated on its own window\nwith worst-wins across slices. This is the rule environments already\nfollowed; the source dimension was simply never considered. Both the system\nrollup and the system overview had the bug; both are fixed.\n\nKeying slices by source also retires a location''s verdict with it: a\nde-assigned satellite (or the core after `includeLocal` is off) stops\ncounting immediately instead of dragging the rollup until its failures age\nout of the window, per-satellite environment scoping is honoured when\nresolving slices, and \"has a live environment slice\" is answered per\nlocation so a satellite scoped to run env-less keeps its slice while the\ncore fans out.\n\nThe overview shows one row per slice and names the location as soon as a\ncheck runs from more than one place. `sliceCount` counts locations too, so\na check probed from the core and one satellite contributes 2 to the\ndashboard denominator rather than 1.\n\nThe service tests cannot reproduce the original masking (their mock resolves\nruns per slice, so it never interleaves); the guard for the mechanism lives\nin state-evaluator.test.ts, which asserts that an interleaved stream\nevaluates to healthy and fails loudly if the slicing is ever re-collapsed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KiVAPXAuVtVemtYvvyPXzp\n\n* fix(maintenance): make status colours agree across every surface\n\nA \"Scheduled\" maintenance was blue on the manage/list/detail pages, amber on\nthe catalog system card, and grey on the status page block and public detail\npage. The manage surfaces went through the canonical presentMaintenanceStatus\nmapping; the other three hardcoded Tailwind classes and never consulted it -\ntwo painting grey for EVERY status (so in-progress read grey too, with the raw\n\"in progress\" enum instead of the \"In Progress\" label), the catalog card\npainting amber for every status.\n\nAll three now derive tone and label from the canonical mapping. status-page-\nfrontend gains maintenanceStatusTone/maintenanceStatusLabel, replicated against\nthe shared pillToneStyles tones - the same platform-layer arrangement\nseverityTone.ts uses, since status-page-frontend must not import the domain\nmaintenance-* packages. A system under planned maintenance now reads blue\n(informational), not grey (inert/unknown). The catalog card takes the tone of\nwhichever window leads: amber in-progress, else blue scheduled.\n\nSeparately, StatusBadge now draws from the shared status tokens (--status-*)\ninstead of the generic --success/--warning/--info palette. Those palettes\ndiffer (e.g. --info 217 91% 60% vs --status-info 214 90% 45%), so a badge and a\npill of the same tone rendered two different blues for the same meaning. Health,\nincident, SLO, maintenance, anomaly and dependency badges now share one hue per\ntone with their pills.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KiVAPXAuVtVemtYvvyPXzp\n\n* fix(notifications): render markdown in incident/maintenance update messages\n\n@stuajnht reported an email showing the raw markdown of a link instead of a\nclickable link. The email renderer was fine; the shared sanitizeUpdateMessage\n(used by both incident and maintenance backends) backslash-escaped every\nmarkdown control character and forced the message onto one blockquoted line, so\na link arrived as \\[label\\]\\(href\\) and rendered as literal text everywhere.\n\nUpdate messages are authored as markdown and render as markdown on the web, so\nthey now do the same in notifications. sanitizeUpdateMessage still normalizes\nthe text (strips non-whitespace control characters, normalizes line endings,\ncollapses blank-line runs, bounds length) but no longer escapes markdown, and\nbuildUpdateMessageSuffix appends it as its own multi-line block. Safety is\nunchanged: only SMTP emits HTML, via markdownToHtml''s allow-list; every other\nstrategy renders markdown/mrkdwn/adaptive-card or plain text, none execute HTML.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KiVAPXAuVtVemtYvvyPXzp\n\n* refactor(healthcheck): share one execution engine between core and satellite\n\nThe satellite''s health-check execution loop was a hand-maintained copy of the\ncore queue executor''s. When the core grew a per-run templating pass, the copy\nwas never updated, so system/environment custom fields ({{ system.metadata.* }},\n{{ environment.* }}) silently failed to expand on satellite runs (reported by\n@stuajnht).\n\nIntroduce @checkstack/healthcheck-execution, a lean backend-only package holding\nthe shared engine (runHealthCheckCollection): render the strategy + collector\nx-templatable fields against the run''s env/system context, build the transport\nclient, run collectors, close the client, and assemble a sink-agnostic outcome.\nBoth the core queue executor and the satellite now run through it; each keeps\nonly its genuine edges as injected hooks (DB secret resolution + migrate-on-read\non the core; JIT socket secrets on the satellite; assertion/strip vs raw result\nmapping). Templating, secret-then-template ordering, and the per-collector\nfan-out can no longer drift between the two.\n\nThe engine lives outside @checkstack/backend-api deliberately, so the platform\nfoundation does not grow more health-check-specific execution logic. Evacuating\nthe pre-existing health-check contracts and registries from backend-api (they\nare wired into core-services and the RPC context) is a separate platform change.\n\nAlso: transport sub-phase timings are now measured at the probe and surfaced by\nsatellites, so a satellite run''s metadata.timings matches a local run''s - the\ncore cannot time a probe it did not run.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KiVAPXAuVtVemtYvvyPXzp\n\n* fix(healthcheck): react to satellite results through the same core path as local runs\n\ningestSatelliteResult only inserted the run row and invalidated the cache, so a\nsatellite-detected health change fired no notifications, no automations, no\ntransition record, and no realtime signal - the local executor does all of that\nafter every run. Satellite monitoring was effectively silent.\n\nExtract the local post-run path into one shared `persistRunAndReact` (reactive\n`health` entity write, cache reconcile, realtime signal, checkCompleted/\ncheckFailed hooks, transition record, subscriber notification) and route BOTH\nthe local executor and satellite ingest through it. The host binds the service\ndeps once and passes the router a narrowed reactor, so the two callers cannot\npass different deps and drift - which is how ingest had diverged into a\nsilent, duplicated copy in the first place. Ingest now splits into\n`processSatelliteResult` (assertions + ephemeral strip + name resolution) plus\nthe shared reactive path.\n\nAlso annotate a satellite collector''s transport error as `_collectorError` on\nthe stored result, matching local runs.\n\nTests: pin that ingest routes through the shared reactor (guarding against a\nregression back to insert-only), and extract the satellite executeAssignment\ninto a testable module with tests for custom-field template expansion,\nprobe-measured timings, the _collectorError annotation, and strategy-not-loaded.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KiVAPXAuVtVemtYvvyPXzp\n\n* fix(satellite): stop ENOENT crash-loop by keeping k8s-events-common in the image\n\n@stuajnht reported satellite releases 134/135 crash-loop at startup with\n`ENOENT reading .../@checkstack/k8s-events-common` (133 works). The k8s-events\ntelemetry pull executor imports that package eagerly at module load, but the\nsatellite image pruned it away, so the agent died before running any check.\n\nTwo root causes, both fixed:\n\n- k8s-events-common lived under plugins/, unlike its sibling telemetry contracts\n  (metricstream/logstream/tracestream-common) which are in core/. A core package\n  (the satellite) importing a plugins/ package is a dependency-direction\n  violation; it now lives in core/ alongside its siblings.\n- The image prune deleted every plugin except healthcheck-*/collector-* backends\n  by name pattern, silently dropping anything else the satellite needs. It is now\n  driven by the dependency graph: it keeps the transitive runtime-dependency\n  closure of the satellite plus every plugin it loads dynamically (those backends\n  as extra roots, so they are never pruned by accident). The \"which plugins does\n  the satellite load\" rule is a single shared predicate consumed by both the\n  runtime loader and the build-time prune, so they cannot drift. The workspace\n  discovery is likewise shared with the tsconfig-reference generator.\n\nVerified by building Dockerfile.satellite and starting the image: it loads all\n15 strategies + 28 collectors, runs the k8s-events executor registration without\nENOENT, and reaches normal core-connection retries instead of crash-looping.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KiVAPXAuVtVemtYvvyPXzp\n\n* fix(status-page): custom-domain serving, event rendering, announcement public remote\n\nResolves the reported Status Pages feedback:\n\n#1 Custom domains\n  - Route on X-Forwarded-Host (first hop) then Host, via a shared\n    resolveRequestHost, so a page behind a Host-rewriting edge proxy resolves\n    (routing previously read the raw Host and served the admin app).\n  - Emit public.html in the frontend build: the backend serves it to a\n    custom-domain host but it was never produced (since #341), so the SPA\n    fallback 404''d. Verified over real HTTP end to end.\n\n#2 Announcements block rendered blank on the public bundle\n  - Ship announcement-frontend as a public Module Federation remote\n    (checkstack.publicRemote) exposing a lean renderer-only entry, so the lean\n    public bundle (custom domain AND same-origin /statuspage/view) can loadRemote\n    it. Backend discovery syncs a plugins row for it; `bun run build:public-remotes`\n    builds every publicRemote plugin, wired into the Dockerfile and e2e pretest.\n  - Verified in a real browser: the remote loads over MF and the block renders.\n\n#3-#6 Detail pages\n  - Colour the incident/maintenance lifecycle status on its own line\n    (new incidentStatusTone, mirroring maintenanceStatusTone) on block + detail.\n  - Render update-message and description markdown (was raw source).\n  - Widget resolveDetail returns the item with ALL public updates (no maxUpdates\n    cap) + its description; the status-page service calls it, gated by the same\n    anti-enumeration boundary as the block.\n\nDocs: document the edge Host / X-Forwarded-Host requirement with reverse-proxy\nexamples on the custom-domain guide + docker-compose page; status-pages\narchitecture page covers resolveDetail and publicRemote.\n\nTests: live-behaviour widget + service tests, a public.html build-emission guard,\nand Playwright specs that render the REAL public bundle for #2-#6 (the published\npage is seeded via the API; the builder add-block UI is covered by\nstatus-page.spec). Also de-flake the infrastructure.empty queue sub-tab test\n(it asserted a fresh boot has no active/failed system jobs, which races).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KiVAPXAuVtVemtYvvyPXzp\n\n* fix(docker): copy top-level scripts into the builder stage\n\nThe builder stage runs `bun run build:public-remotes` (added for the\nannouncement public MF remote) but never COPYd the top-level `scripts/` dir, so\nthe Docker image build failed with `Module not found scripts/build-public-remotes.ts`.\nCopy `scripts/` into the builder (the runtime stage does not need it).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KiVAPXAuVtVemtYvvyPXzp\n\n* chore(deps): upgrade astro to v7 and starlight to 0.41\n\nClears the astro security advisories (GHSA-4g3v-8h47-v7g6, CVE-2026-59729;\nfixed in astro 7.1.0) that were gating the dependency-graph security check.\nastro 6 -> 7.1.3, @astrojs/starlight 0.38 -> 0.41.3 (peer astro ^7.0.2), sharp\n0.33 -> 0.35 (matches the root override). Migrates the docs sidebar to the\nStarlight v0.39+ schema (an autogenerated group''s `autogenerate` config must be\nwrapped in an `items` array). Docs build verified: 163 pages built under Astro 7.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KiVAPXAuVtVemtYvvyPXzp\n\n* fix(announcement): don''t restrict the published package to dist\n\n`core/announcement-frontend` is a CORE plugin consumed from SOURCE (`main`:\nsrc/index.tsx) - the admin app imports it directly - so `\"files\": [\"dist\"]`\n(copied from the third-party remote template) excluded `src/` from the published\npackage and broke the integration dev-server boot (\"could not resolve\n@checkstack/announcement-frontend\"). The public MF remote is served from the\nworkspace `dist/` on disk, not the npm package, so the field was never needed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KiVAPXAuVtVemtYvvyPXzp\n\n* fix(scripts): make tsconfig-references cycle pruning deterministic\n\nThe dependency-cycle back-edge pruning in `generate-tsconfig-references`\ndepended on package DISCOVERY order (both the DFS start-node order and child\norder), so a cycle (satellite-backend <-> healthcheck-backend) could prune a\nDIFFERENT edge in CI than the committed tsconfigs reflect - failing\n`typecheck:references:check` non-deterministically. Sort both orders\nlexicographically so the pruned set is identical in every environment.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KiVAPXAuVtVemtYvvyPXzp\n\n* fix(scripts): use toSorted() in cycle pruning (lint)\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KiVAPXAuVtVemtYvvyPXzp\n\n* fix(e2e): ship the announcement remote to e2e + fix health/empty-state assumptions\n\nFour E2E failures, all fixed:\n\n- Announcement spec: the `e2e_build` CI job built only frontend + docs and\n  uploaded only those dists, so the shards never had the announcement MF remote\n  and the public bundle''s loadRemote 404''d. It now runs `build:public-remotes`\n  and uploads `core/announcement-frontend/dist`.\n\n- catalog + incident `expandBrowseSections`: waited on the catalog browse health\n  rollup PILL, but this branch made an unmeasured system read \"unknown\", which\n  renders NO rollup pill - so the wait timed out. Replaced with a poll that\n  opens every collapsed section until stable (a manual open overrides the\n  all-healthy auto-collapse), independent of any pill.\n\n- automation \"runs page renders an empty state\": asserted the FILTERED empty\n  state (\"No runs match this filter\"), but a fresh automation with no active\n  filter shows \"No runs yet\". Accept either, and allow the runs query to load.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KiVAPXAuVtVemtYvvyPXzp\n\n* fix(auth): let team members and managers see and run their own team\n\nTeam read procedures were gated on the GLOBAL auth.teams.read rule, so a\nteam-scoped user (a per-team ReBAC grant, no global rule) was 403''d on\ngetTeams and denied listObjectRelations - breaking the \"Who can change this\"\nmodal for a team manager and locking managers out of their own team.\n\nBackend: the read/metadata procedures (getTeams, getTeam, listObjectRelations,\nlistObjectRelationsBulk, listSubjectRelations, listTeamCreateGrants,\nsearchUsers, resolveResourceNames, getResourceKinds) drop the global gate and\nscope in the handler - a global auth.teams.read holder or service sees every\nteam; everyone else sees only the team(s) they are a member or manager of.\ngetTeam returns undefined for a team with no stake (no existence leak) and\nlistObjectRelations hides grants from a caller with no stake while still\nreturning the public flag (a successful empty response, never a 403). Write\nprocedures keep enforcing assertTeamManagementAccess, so a team manager can\nmanage their own team; create/delete team stay admin-only. hasGlobalTeamsRead\nnow uses isAccessRuleSatisfied (so * and teams.manage both count).\n\nFrontend: the Teams page and nav are shown to a global teams.read holder OR any\nmember/manager of at least one team (not unconditionally). A new isInAnyTeam\nflag on the app-wide accessRules query (member OR manager, so manager-only\nteams count) is threaded through the sidebar''s nav model and exposed to every\nroute''s isVisible predicate via a new isInAnyTeam context field.\n\nDocs and the AI docs index updated to match.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KiVAPXAuVtVemtYvvyPXzp\n\n* chore(changeset): cover @checkstack/k8s-events-common relocation\n\nThe satellite ENOENT fix moved k8s-events-common from plugins/ to core/,\nrenaming its src files, but the changeset only bumped @checkstack/satellite.\nAdd k8s-events-common (patch) to the same changeset so changeset coverage is\ncomplete.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KiVAPXAuVtVemtYvvyPXzp\n\n* fix(healthcheck): stop unmeasured (unknown) systems reading as \"Degraded\"\n\nA system with no checks (or none run yet) has health status \"unknown\", but two\ndisplay paths only excluded \"healthy\" and let every other status fall through to\nthe amber \"Degraded\" label - so a check-less system falsely showed \"Degraded\" on\nits detail page/catalog rows and as a dashboard problem card, with no incident,\nfailing check, or failing dependency behind it.\n\n- deriveHealthcheckSignals (healthcheck-common): omit \"unknown\" alongside\n  \"healthy\" so the dashboard raises no signal for an unmeasured system. Its doc\n  already claimed both were omitted; the code only skipped healthy.\n- SystemHealthBadge (healthcheck-frontend): return no badge for \"unknown\". The\n  decision is extracted into a pure resolveHealthBadge helper with unit tests.\n\nThe dependency \"Degrading impact\" chips are unrelated (edge config, not live\nstate); the warning engine already maps an unmeasured upstream to operational.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KiVAPXAuVtVemtYvvyPXzp\n\n* feat(auth): delegate resource team-access editing to resource managers\n\nEditing a resource''s team access (\"Who can change this\": add/remove teams,\ntoggle Manage/Private) was gated on the GLOBAL auth.teams.manage rule on both\nfrontend and backend, so only a platform admin could do it - even a member of a\nteam that already manages the resource saw the editor read-only.\n\nwriteRelation/removeRelation/setObjectPublic now authorize per-object: the caller\nmay edit a resource''s team access when they can MANAGE that specific resource - a\nglobal auth.teams.manage admin, a holder of the resource''s own <type>.manage rule\n(e.g. catalog.system.manage), or a member of a team with an editor/owner grant on\nit. A viewer-only team (or no grant, no global rule) is read-only and CANNOT\nelevate its own or another team''s access. The check reuses the ReBAC engine\n(tupleStore.check action=manage), so team-private resources are respected: a\nglobal resource-manager not on a granted team cannot reach a private resource -\nonly a teams.manage admin or a granted team can. The first grant on an unscoped\nresource still needs a global rule.\n\nA new canManageObjectAccess query runs the SAME authorization; TeamAccessEditor\ngates its write controls on it instead of the global rule, so the UI matches what\na write would accept (no drift). The backend re-checks every write.\n\nBREAKING CHANGE: granting/revoking a team''s access to a resource is no longer\nadmin-only - it is delegated to whoever can manage that resource.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KiVAPXAuVtVemtYvvyPXzp\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.co