flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/enyineer/checkstack/commit/7ff1ff5e74c5c0e4ea94ec9db5f5d635bc871a9a","node_id":"C_kwDOQrp5TdoAKDdmZjFmZjVlNzRjNWMwZTRlYTk0ZWM5ZGI1ZjVkNjM1YmM4NzFhOWE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/enyineer/checkstack/commit/6c8b36bf4334ed92eb7f77738cc0754c536bf3b6","sha":"6c8b36bf4334ed92eb7f77738cc0754c536bf3b6","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/enyineer/checkstack/commits/6c8b36bf4334ed92eb7f77738cc0754c536bf3b6"}],"sha":"7ff1ff5e74c5c0e4ea94ec9db5f5d635bc871a9a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/enyineer/checkstack/commits/7ff1ff5e74c5c0e4ea94ec9db5f5d635bc871a9a"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/27201677?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/enyineer","id":27201677,"login":"enyineer","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI3MjAxNjc3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/enyineer/checkstack/commits/6c8b36bf4334ed92eb7f77738cc0754c536bf3b6/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-07-15T23:53:35Z","email":"nico.enking@gmail.com","name":"Nico"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-07-15T23:53:35Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat: telemetry platform (logs, metrics, traces) + team-scoped catalog (#473)\n\n* feat(backend-api,queue-api): shared guarded fetch and recurring-job reconcile helper\n\n- backend-api: createGuardedFetch/GuardedFetchError - SSRF-guarded fetch that\n  re-validates the host on every redirect hop, downgrades 301/302/303 to GET,\n  refuses non-replayable 307/308 bodies, strips credential headers on\n  cross-origin redirects, and supports maxRedirects: 0 (return 3xx as-is).\n- queue-api: reconcileRecurringJobs - desired-vs-actual convergence for\n  recurring jobs (idempotent schedule + ownsJobId-scoped orphan cancel,\n  parallel round-trips).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* feat(telemetry): platform source/sink abstraction for pluggable telemetry ingestion\n\nNew platform packages telemetry-common/backend/frontend:\n\n- Signal model (logs/metrics/traces) with OTel-shaped normalized records as\n  the source->sink lingua franca; signal coverage satisfies-enforced.\n- telemetrySinkExtensionPoint (stream owners contribute one sink per signal:\n  write, bind-time authorization, bindable-stream listing, batched stream\n  name resolution) and telemetrySourceExtensionPoint (pull, webhook and\n  listener source types; boot-time validation incl. a hard x-secret probe).\n- Team-scoped source instances (telemetry.source) with per-signal stream\n  bindings, encrypted-at-rest secret config fields on the shared\n  config-secret marker primitives, and SECRET_CLEAR_SENTINEL semantics.\n- Pull reconciler (on queue-api reconcileRecurringJobs) + runner with\n  SSRF-guarded fetch; per-pod listener lifecycle manager with event-bus\n  cross-pod convergence; rate-limited webhook endpoints with hash-only\n  rotatable secrets AND platform-verified vendor HMAC signatures\n  (GitHub/Slack descriptors, capped pre-auth body buffering).\n- telemetry-pull satellite capability: config push without secrets, JIT\n  per-field secret resolution, binding-authorized batch re-ingestion,\n  status mirroring, satellite authorship guard on binding.\n- Frontend: self-hiding StreamSourcesSection embed, source catalog with\n  schema-driven config dialogs, multi-signal binding editor, global\n  Sources page (manageCapability-gated), health hints, satellite picker,\n  webhook secret shown-once, connection testing, metadata-filtered\n  SourceConfigSlot.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* feat(logstream,metricstream): telemetry sinks, sources UI embeds and shared-helper migrations\n\n- Both backends contribute telemetry sinks reusing the exact push-endpoint\n  normalization (parity regression tests), with bind authorization,\n  bindable-stream listing and batched name resolution through the shared\n  sink-guards factory.\n- Frontends embed StreamSourcesSection (metricstream Sources tab, logstream\n  Settings tab).\n- metricstream migrations onto the new shared helpers: the Prometheus scrape\n  executor now uses the guarded fetch (every redirect hop re-validated) and\n  the scrape reconciler uses queue-api reconcileRecurringJobs.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* feat(satellite): telemetry-pull capability and shared interval scheduler\n\n- Generic CapabilityIntervalScheduler base extracted from the scrape\n  scheduler (timer reconcile, stable-id in-flight guard, concurrency cap,\n  JIT secret cache flushed on config push); scrape + telemetry-pull\n  schedulers compose it.\n- telemetry-pull agent side: executor registry (statically-linked\n  SatellitePullExecutor seam), per-instance scheduling, unbound-signal\n  drop, absolute-counter status; advertised via\n  CHECKSTACK_SATELLITE_TELEMETRY_PULL.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* docs(telemetry): developer guide for sources/sinks and regenerated docs index\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* feat(tracestream): distributed tracing streams with tail-based sampling\n\nAdd the tracestream service as the third stream type alongside logstream\nand metricstream:\n\n- core/tracestream-common: cktr_ token format, stream RLAC (team-scoped\n  tracestream.stream), config schema (hot 6h / retained 7d / summaries 30d,\n  keep-errors + slow>threshold + 1% deterministic baseline sampling), oRPC\n  contract (stream CRUD, keyset searchTraces, getTrace, RED op buckets,\n  cross-stream findTraceById), OTLP + native ingest parsing.\n- core/tracestream-backend: port-based storage (spans, summaries, service\n  ops, minute/hourly t-digest buckets, activity + important events) with\n  span idempotency via a (streamId,traceId,spanId) unique index, tail\n  sampling decision/sweep/rollup/cleanup jobs, OTLP-HTTP protobuf+JSON and\n  native JSON ingest endpoints, traces telemetry sink so telemetry sources\n  can bind trace streams.\n- core/tracestream-frontend: stream list + detail (Overview/Traces/\n  Services/Settings with URL state), trace waterfall viewer, span detail\n  panel, token management, ship-traces instructions, telemetry sources\n  embed.\n- @checkstack/ui: virtualized, isLowPower-aware TraceWaterfall chart with\n  Storybook story.\n- @checkstack/backend-api: promote the t-digest helpers out of\n  healthcheck-backend so tracestream''s RED rollups share one impl.\n- Developer-guide page + regenerated AI docs index.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* fix(otlp-wire,logstream,metricstream): signed int64 decoding and tuple-keyset event pagination\n\n- otlp-wire: decode OTLP AnyValue int64 as SIGNED two''s complement (a\n  negative attribute previously surfaced as a huge positive float) and keep\n  magnitudes beyond the JS safe-integer range as exact decimal strings\n  instead of silently rounding, matching proto3-JSON semantics.\n- logstream/metricstream: paginate important events on a (ts,id) tuple\n  keyset cursor instead of a bare timestamp, so events sharing a timestamp\n  can no longer be skipped or duplicated across pages.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* test: root-cause load-flaky tests across the suite\n\nEleven tests failed only under full-suite machine load. Each got a\nroot-cause fix, never a skip or weakened assertion:\n\n- Shared beforeAll fixtures where per-test I/O (script-package installs,\n  container spins) was being starved into timeouts.\n- Terminal-signal polling instead of wall-clock windows for retry/queue\n  tests; the memory-queue retry tests additionally size their poll safety\n  net at the suite''s starvation ceiling (an 8s net expired at 8.3s while\n  nominal ~10ms retry timers were pending).\n- Fake timers where real ones only added scheduler jitter (Tabs).\n- Deliberate commented starvation ceilings (~2x worst observed) for tests\n  whose work is genuinely heavy under contention: real bun subprocess\n  cold-starts (generate-sdk --check, scaffold standalone, shell scripts)\n  and a real Bun worker module-graph load (logstream worker round trip).\n- test-utils mock queue now logs caught handler errors as a message string\n  instead of the raw Error, so an expected failure in a passing test no\n  longer renders as a red uncaught-error block that counts as a suite\n  error.\n\nAll fixes verified in isolation and under a 16-worker CPU/FS/cold-start\ncontention harness.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* feat(correlation): cross-signal trace correlation for logs, traces and health checks\n\nLogs, traces, and health-check runs now link to each other through W3C\ntrace context:\n\n- logstream: searchEvents accepts an exact traceId filter (Explore gets a\n  matching, deep-linkable input) backed by a new partial (trace_id, ts)\n  index; the new cross-stream findEventsByTraceId returns per-stream match\n  groups post-filtered by the caller''s read grants, with a required time\n  window, a 50-stream group cap and a per-stream row cap enforced in SQL.\n- Per-stream config.traceExtraction rules (attribute paths + capture-group\n  body regex) populate trace/span ids for non-OTLP sources at the ingest\n  flush seam. Rules are validated at save by a conservative\n  backtracking-safety analyzer (rejects backreferences, quantified groups\n  containing quantifiers/alternation, and >2 unbounded quantifiers) so a\n  user-authored pattern can never stall the shared ingest worker. Carried\n  ids are normalized (trim/dash-strip/lowercase) at the same seam so\n  storage and the exact-match queries share one canonical form; query\n  inputs normalize identically.\n- Correlation slots: LogEventDetailSlot (logstream-common),\n  TraceCorrelationsSlot (tracestream-common), RunDetailExtrasSlot\n  (healthcheck-common, with extractRunTraceIds owning the run-result\n  shape). The trace view shows correlated log events grouped per readable\n  stream with windowed Explore deep links; log events and health-check\n  runs with a known trace id get a \"View trace\" jump resolved through\n  findTraceById, gated behind a cached any-trace-streams probe.\n- Docs: new trace-correlation developer-guide page + cross-links;\n  regenerated AI docs index.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* feat(healthcheck-http): emit a W3C traceparent header on every probe\n\nThe HTTP request collector stamps each probe with a traceparent header,\nDEFAULT-ON with a per-collector opt-out (emitTraceparent: false). Ids are\nfreshly generated per run (CSPRNG); a user-configured traceparent header\n(any casing) wins and is passed through verbatim with no id recorded. The\nsent trace id persists on the collector result as a text-annotated,\nnon-anomaly traceId field, powering the run detail panel''s \"View trace\"\njump when the probed application exports spans to a trace stream.\n\nBEHAVIOR CHANGE for existing checks: stored configs parse the new field as\ntrue, so pre-existing HTTP checks start sending the header (sampled flag\n01) on deploy - documented in the changeset with the opt-out.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* feat(tracestream): health-check strategy, collectors and error-spike fast path\n\nReader-only OBSERVABILITY strategy for trace streams:\n\n- trace-window collector: windowed span/trace totals, error counts,\n  errorRatePerMinute and secondsSinceLastSpan, read via a new SQL-SUM\n  op-bucket port (never digest-bearing rows on count paths).\n- operation-latency collector (repeatable): per service/operation p95Ms,\n  avgMs, maxMs and error rate; the window p95 merges every minute\n  bucket''s t-digest state into ONE digest before computing the\n  percentile (new queryWindowLatency port; an average of per-bucket\n  p95s is provably wrong and rejected by an integration test).\n- Fast-path re-evaluation: a flush that persists error spans enqueues\n  affected checks ahead of schedule (pod-local 15s debounce with\n  unmark-on-enqueue-failure, deterministic cluster-wide job id).\n- error_spike important event: post-commit trailing-average detection\n  evaluated at the freshest SPAN-TIME minute the flush touched (wall\n  clock misses bursts under normal OTLP export lag), deduped 10 min\n  cluster-wide via the shared events table with a pod-local gate.\n- Check-editor stream/service/operation dropdowns via shared resolver\n  constants in tracestream-common, filled by tracestream-frontend;\n  error_spike event visual.\n\nThe storage-port additions in this commit (sumWindowCounts,\nqueryWindowLatency, lastEventAt) also carry the satellite drop\naccounting seam introduced by the follow-up satellite commit.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* feat(satellite,tracestream): satellite trace forwarding with durable drop accounting\n\nSatellites with CHECKSTACK_SATELLITE_TRACE_RECEIVERS=1 expose local\n/v1/traces (OTLP protobuf + JSON) and /ingest/traces (native) receivers\non the shared receiver port, parsing with the same browser-safe parsers\nthe core uses and forwarding over the new \"tracestream\" telemetry\nchannel. The wire schema (satellite-relay.ts) carries ISO dates and\ndecimal-string nanosecond timestamps, enforces canonical lowercase-hex\nspan identities, and caps attributes/events/links at exactly the\nparsers'' limits (shared caps module), so a forwarded span can never be\nmore permissive or differently-identified than a direct push.\n\nThe core capability handler verifies each forwarded cktr_ token with\nthe same authenticator as direct pushes, re-clamps span times against\nthe CORE clock through the identical ingest pipeline, and mirrors\nlogstream''s ack semantics (retryable only on pure saturation; drop\nattribution is best-effort and can never flip an accepted batch''s\nack). Satellite in-transit drops are persisted per stream\n(dropped_in_transit_count on the activity table, additive migration\n0002) at parity with logstream/metricstream.\n\nIncludes the satellite-frontend capability badge, an env-guarded e2e\ntraces case, and satellite/tracestream documentation.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* refactor(healthcheck,ingest-utils): shared run-queue contract, window math and receiver chunking\n\nThree duplications, each now single-copy with the superseded local\ndeclarations deleted:\n\n- The health-check run-queue contract (HEALTH_CHECK_QUEUE,\n  HealthCheckJobPayload, fastPathJobId with a per-plugin prefix,\n  FAST_PATH_DEBOUNCE_MS) moves to @checkstack/healthcheck-common; the\n  queue owner (healthcheck-backend) and both fast-path enqueuers\n  (logstream, tracestream) import the same definitions, so the shape\n  can no longer drift. Enqueued job-id strings are byte-identical.\n- The observability window math (computeWindowBounds with its\n  complete-minute/readTo semantics, computeSecondsSinceLast,\n  MIN_WINDOW_SECONDS) moves to healthcheck-common; the logstream,\n  metricstream and tracestream copies (verified logic-identical by\n  function-body diff before extraction) now import it.\n- The satellite receivers'' batch chunking + byte-budget estimation\n  moves to @checkstack/ingest-utils (chunkTelemetryBatchItems,\n  estimateTelemetryItemBytes); the log/metric/trace receivers keep\n  only per-signal item shapes and caps, with behavior pinned by their\n  unchanged tests. The core-side handlers stay per-signal on purpose:\n  they differ structurally in re-normalization and ack accounting,\n  and parameterizing them would obscure the trust logic.\n\nAlso fixes logstream''s fast-path debounce to unmark a slice when its\nenqueue fails, matching the tracestream fix (a Redis blip no longer\nsilently drops a burst''s evaluation for the rest of the bucket).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* fix(ui,healthcheck-frontend): external resolver dependency refetch for cross-form pickers\n\nDynamicOptionsField refetched options only when the resolver name or\nthe field''s own x-depends-on values changed, so a resolver reading\nEXTERNAL state (the health-check editor''s strategyConfig - e.g. the\nselected stream backing logstream''s pattern picker and tracestream''s\nservice/operation pickers) could not express that dependency and only\nstayed fresh because the current editor happens to remount collector\npanels on navigation. DynamicForm now accepts an optional\nresolversDependencyKey included in the refetch effect''s deps; the\nhealth-check editor passes a memoized strategyConfig fingerprint to\ncollector forms only. Defense-in-depth: no reproducing symptom in\ntoday''s mutually-exclusive panel layout, but the mount-time-only\nfreshness guarantee is gone the first time a layout co-mounts the\nforms. Tested without remount, including a no-churn guard for\nunrelated in-form edits.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* test: explicit load-testing lane (bun run test:load)\n\nThe logstream and metricstream ingest load guards saturate the machine\nby design (sustained multi-thousand-rows/s ingest plus event-loop\npressure probes) and exist to diagnose performance regressions, not to\ngate every run. They now require CHECKSTACK_LOAD_TESTS=1 on top of the\nintegration lane (new isLoadTestEnabled helper in test-utils-backend),\nso package-level CHECKSTACK_IT=1 runs and CI''s integration job skip\nthem. Run them deliberately via the new root script:\n\n  bun run test:load\n  CHECKSTACK_IT_LOAD_MS=3000 bun run test:load   # quicker window\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* refactor(backend-api): one user-scoped RPC client for caller-identity re-entry\n\nPromote createUserScopedRpcClient + forwardableAuthHeadersFrom into\n@checkstack/backend-api as the single implementation of \"re-enter the\nlive router AS the calling user\". Only the session cookie and bearer\nAuthorization are ever forwarded; a request without them re-enters\nanonymous, so the gates built on it fail closed. ai-backend (deferred\ntool routing), status-page-backend (publish gate) and telemetry-backend\n(satellite binding auth) migrate to the shared export and their local\nnear-verbatim copies are deleted; the canonical unit test moves to\nbackend-api. Behavior is byte-identical - this removes six drift-prone\ncopies of security-sensitive header-forwarding code.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* feat(catalog,logstream,metricstream,tracestream): stream-to-system links and AI tool projections\n\nExplicit links between observability streams and catalog systems, plus\nmodel-facing read tools for all three stream plugins:\n\n- Each stream plugin declares the same four link procedures over its own\n  bare-systemId junction table (shared schemas + chunk/merge helpers in\n  telemetry-common). The replace-all write verifies the caller can READ\n  every NEWLY ADDED system - one user-scoped catalog getSystems\n  membership pass through an injected, test-substitutable authorizer,\n  existence-checked first - so a manager can never expose a system they\n  cannot see, and is never dead-locked by a link a broader-privileged\n  user authorized. Reverse lookups are grant-filtered (listKey).\n- catalog-frontend ships the shared StreamSystemLinksEditor (system\n  picker + explicitly-applied \"suggested from observed service names\"\n  chips with a loading affordance), StreamSystemLinksSettingsCard (one\n  draft/dirty/save implementation for all three Settings tabs) and\n  LinkedStreamsCard (one detail-page card chrome). Suggestion sources:\n  tracestream''s service catalog, metricstream label values, logstream''s\n  new bounded listServiceNames scan.\n- The catalog system page gains self-hiding Logs/Metrics/Traces cards\n  (SystemDetailsSlot) and the dashboard gains conservative per-stream\n  signals (SystemSignalsSlot): one bulk, client-chunked\n  listLinkedStreamStatuses per plugin, with the newest-event lookup\n  constrained to each plugin''s signal-worthy event types (a benign newer\n  event can no longer mask an active spike) and deterministic\n  tiebreaks; deleted-stream phantoms are never surfaced.\n- AI tool projections (read-only, RLAC via routed re-entry):\n  logstream.searchLogs (slimmed) / severityStats / listStreams;\n  metricstream.listStreams / listMetricNames / metricBuckets (the\n  unbounded raw-series read is deliberately not projected);\n  tracestream.searchTraces / getTraceSummary (spans reduced to seven\n  scalar fields) / serviceStats / listServices. The tool-registry guide\n  now documents the projectResult output-slimming convention.\n- New developer-guide page for the link contract; regenerated docs\n  index; per-plugin migrations (generated, additive).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* feat(telemetry): derive, push, and pull-health source-platform expansion\n\nThe platform gains three seams and the lifecycle machinery around them:\n\n- DERIVE sources (log-to-metric, log-to-trace built-ins): sink-owning\n  plugins tap their post-flush batches into a generation-guarded,\n  hook-invalidated dispatcher; lazy record thunks keep streams without\n  derive sources at zero cost; per-batch label-tuple cap bounds series\n  cardinality.\n- PUSH sources: per-instance bearer tokens minted by the platform\n  (hash-only, shown once, rotatable), verified by endpoint-owning\n  plugins through a type-scoped verifier service + createPushTokenLookup\n  over the shared ingest authenticator; cross-pod verdict eviction on\n  mint/rotate/disable/delete AND binding changes; migration 0002\n  promotes every non-revoked legacy stream token in place (same id,\n  hash, prefix) so existing shippers keep working; a marker-gated\n  one-shot backfills bound-stream team grants onto promoted sources.\n- Pull health hooks (onRunFailure/onRunRecovery) with stored\n  consecutive-failure counts, invoked on core and satellite-reported\n  runs.\n- Stream-deletion lifecycle (handleStreamDeleted): bound sources lose\n  the binding; binding-less sources are fully purged (secrets, schedule,\n  grants, token revoked) so deleted streams'' shippers get 401s instead\n  of black-holing data.\n- Listener manager only binds on the default instance (PR-preview port\n  isolation); satellite pull config resolves ${{ secrets.NAME }}\n  references just-in-time.\n\nCross-plugin migration ordering (promotion before owner drops) is\nguaranteed by topological migration order, now documented in the\nmigrations rule. The satellite e2e seed follows the promoted schema.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* feat(logstream): syslog listener, derive tap, and push sources on the platform\n\nLogstream''s ingest management moves fully onto the telemetry platform:\n\n- Syslog becomes the first LISTENER source type: a source instance with\n  port/TLS config bound to one stream replaces the env-var listener; the\n  binding is the authorization (no in-message tokens on the core path;\n  the satellite edge receiver keeps its token protocol). TLS cert/key\n  paths are validated at start so a broken intake surfaces as lastError.\n- The post-flush derive tap feeds the platform dispatcher detached and\n  lazily - a deriver can never slow or fail ingest, and streams without\n  derive sources pay no conversion cost.\n- Push tokens migrate to platform push sources: the ingest authenticator\n  resolves bearers through the platform verifier (logstream.push), the\n  token CRUD procs, token table (dropped), TokensSection UI, and\n  TokenUseTracker are removed outright, and stream deletion cascades\n  through the platform lifecycle. The stream detail page gains a\n  dedicated Sources tab.\n- Boot warns when the removed CHECKSTACK_LOGSTREAM_SYSLOG_PORT is set.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* feat(metricstream): scrape, push, and exemplars on the telemetry platform\n\n- Prometheus scraping becomes the pull source type\n  metricstream.prometheus-scrape; the private scrape extension point,\n  reconciler, CRUD, and UI are removed. A boot one-shot re-keys migrated\n  inline bearers into the platform''s secret channel and deletes each\n  internal secret after re-keying; scrape_failing important events are\n  restored through the platform''s pull health hooks (threshold 3, once\n  per outage episode).\n- Push tokens migrate to platform push sources (metricstream.push):\n  lookup swapped to the platform verifier, token CRUD/UI removed, and\n  BOTH legacy tables (metric_stream_tokens, metric_scrape_targets) are\n  dropped - safe because the platform''s promotion migrations run first\n  in topological order.\n- Metric points carry OTLP/OpenMetrics exemplars end to end: decoded,\n  folded per series, persisted by MERGING with stored exemplars in one\n  batched update per flush, returned windowed on chart reads, and\n  rendered as a click-through-to-trace lane; the telemetry sink and\n  satellite wire preserve them with full fidelity.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* feat(tracestream): push sources, drop-counter tiles, and shared trace links\n\n- Push tokens migrate to platform push sources (tracestream.push):\n  lookup swapped to the platform verifier, token CRUD/UI and the\n  TokenStore port removed, trace_stream_tokens dropped, and the new\n  cross-pod invalidation subscription closes tracestream''s previous\n  mint-vs-negative-cache race. Stream deletion cascades through the\n  platform lifecycle, and the stream detail page gains a dedicated\n  Sources tab.\n- The Overview tab surfaces the three drop counters (spans, traces,\n  in transit) as warn-toned stat tiles.\n- buildViewTraceHref is exported once from tracestream-common (consumed\n  by the metric chart''s exemplar lane); the token extractor aligns with\n  the ReDoS-hardened slice form.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* feat(satellite): statically-linked telemetry pull executors\n\nThe agent executes platform pull sources at the edge through a\nstatically-linked executor registry: the Prometheus scrape executor\n(honoring each instance''s own timeoutMs, sharing the size/series-capped\nresponse reader with core) and the Kubernetes events executor (wrapping\nthe SSRF guard around the shared pull driver, surfacing scan\ntruncation). Just-in-time secrets resolve fresh per run, so rotating a\nreferenced secret takes effect on the next pull; the legacy scrape\ncapability and CHECKSTACK_SATELLITE_SCRAPE flag are removed (with a\nboot warning when still set), and the satellite UI shows the\ntelemetry-pull capability badge.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* feat(k8s-events): Kubernetes events source plugin\n\nNew plugin pair contributing the first external pull source type:\ninterval-LISTs a cluster''s events.k8s.io/v1 events (request shapes\nverified against the official Kubernetes API reference) and ingests\nthem as log records - Warning events as warnings, with the regarding\nobject, reporting controller, and stable event uid as attributes.\nService-account bearer auth (encrypted at rest, JIT on satellites);\nnamespace/fieldSelector/labelSelector scoping. maxEventsPerPull caps\nEMITTED in-window records so an event backlog cannot starve recent\nevents; the scan is bounded by a 40-page budget with a truncation\nwarning for busy clusters and a transport error for servers that page\nforever without items.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* feat(telemetry-frontend): push token reveal, rotation, and shipper snippets\n\nThe sources UI absorbs everything the per-plugin token sections did:\npush types skip the empty config form and render descriptor-driven\nsetup snippets instead (OTel Collector YAML with the per-signal\nendpoint key, curl for native ingest, origin-prefixed, token\ninterpolated when present); creating a push source reveals the\nshown-once token through the generalized secret-reveal panel; push\nrows get a rotate action and last-received liveness from lastRunAt.\nEmpty-state and section copy now cover push, pull, webhook, listener,\nand derive modes.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* fix(healthcheck): run the config-secrets backfill after all plugins initialize\n\nStrategies contributed by other plugins register during THEIR init, and\nplugin init order follows the service-ref graph - so running the\nbackfill during healthcheck''s own init could scan configurations before\na contributor (e.g. logstream''s health strategy) had registered,\nskipping that strategy''s config with a boot warning. afterPluginsReady\nis the only phase that guarantees a complete registry; the backfill is\nidempotent, so previously-skipped configs converge on the next boot.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* docs(ai-backend): source-ecosystem documentation pass and regenerated index\n\nRewrites the shipping guides and concepts for the platform source\nmodel: push sources with shown-once rotatable tokens on every stream''s\nSources tab, the syslog listener source (multi-pod bind semantics\nstated per topology), the migrated Prometheus scrape flow, satellite\npull execution, and the developer-guide sections for the listener,\nderive, and push seams (including the push verifier composition for\nthird-party plugin authors). Regenerates the AI docs index over the\nnew content.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* test: consistent budgets for two contention-flaky tests\n\nThe template scaffolding tests granted their typecheck/eslint\nsubprocesses 60s but declared a 30s bun-test timeout, so under\nfull-suite load the test killed itself while its child was still\ninside its own budget (48.5s observed); the test ceiling now sits\nabove the subprocess budget. The script-packages coalescing test gets\na documented starvation ceiling (~2x worst observed 71.9s) - its\nassertions, not the clock, carry the correctness.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* fix(frontend): keep edit forms stable during background refetches\n\nEdit dialogs and settings pages re-seeded their local form state from the\nunderlying query inside a useEffect, so a webhook/realtime signal refetch (or\nany parent re-render passing a fresh initialData object) re-fired the effect\nand wiped the user''s in-progress edits mid-typing - most visibly when editing\na system''s description in the catalog.\n\nSeed once instead: on the dialog open transition (new useSeedFormOnOpen hook)\nor once per record (existing useInitOnceForKey), with gcTime:0 on one-shot\nloader queries so a warm cache cannot race the seed.\n\nAdds the shared useSeedFormOnOpen hook to @checkstack/ui and a warn-level\nESLint rule checkstack/no-state-seed-in-effect that flags the antipattern,\nnames the correct fix variant for each shape, and documents the\neslint-disable-next-line escape hatch for genuine one-way syncs (5 benign\nsites annotated with reasons).\n\nFixed surfaces: catalog system/environment/group editors, healthcheck\nplatform-defaults dialog, SLO / gitops-provider / telemetry-source /\nsatellite / announcement / role dialogs, and the cache / queue / notification\n/ secrets / anomaly / profile / strategies / ai settings.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* perf(scripts): run the test suite in a shared process, isolate only polluters\n\nReplaces `bun test --parallel` (a fresh worker + full module-graph reparse\nper file, ~11min pinning every core and thermally throttling laptops) with a\ntwo-pass partition: the bulk of the suite runs in ONE shared process, and only\nthe greppable set that leaks process globals runs `--isolate`. ~671s -> ~32s,\nmostly single-core, ~0.9GB peak, identical pass/fail.\n\nAdds the `checkstack/no-shared-process-test-pollution` ESLint rule (error): the\nrunner detects the isolate-set by grepping each TEST file, so a `mock.module` /\nglobal assignment / `spyOn(global*)` hidden in an importable non-test module\nwould defeat the partition and leak into unrelated suites. The rule forbids\nthose calls outside `*.test.*` files (the sanctioned test-preload aside).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* fix(frontend): unify system-overview cards on a shared DetailCard surface\n\nThe Logs/Metrics/Traces cards rendered flatter than every other card on the\nsystem overview page: their shared LinkedStreamsCard had drifted to a flat\n`bg-card` with a hairline-only shadow, while the sibling cards (health,\ndependency, SLO, incident, anomaly, maintenance) used the detail-page gradient\nplus a two-layer elevation shadow. That surface was a copy-pasted className (and\na per-file PANEL_SHADOW constant) in every card, with nothing keeping them in\nsync.\n\nExtract the surface into one primitive - `DetailCard` (+ `detailCardSurface` /\n`detailCardSurfaceFlat`) in @checkstack/ui - and render all seven\nsystem-overview cards from it, so they cannot diverge again. The three telemetry\ncards now match their siblings; the other six are pure refactors (same pixels,\ntheir local PANEL_SHADOW consts removed).\n\nAdd the `error`-level ESLint rule `checkstack/no-inline-detail-card-chrome`,\nscoped to that card family, which fails the build if a card re-declares the\nsurface inline (the --d-card-r radius, the from-surface-2 gradient, or the\nelevation shadow) instead of using DetailCard. New cards join the guard by\nadding their file to the rule''s scope in eslint.config.mjs.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* feat(catalog): team-scoped groups & environments with consistent manage tabs\n\nMake catalog Groups and Environments first-class team-manageable (RLAC)\nobjects for writes while keeping reads public, and polish the three manage\ntabs (Systems/Groups/Environments) for consistency and performance.\n\nAuthorization\n- New reusable create-authorization primitive `create.alsoAcceptCreatorOf`:\n  a creator capability on a sibling TYPE also authorizes creating this\n  resource (owning team still resolved normally). System creators may now\n  create team-scoped groups & environments. Extracted a shared\n  `hasCreateCapability` S2S check so the middleware gate and the frontend\n  `canCreate` verdict use one strict-creator implementation.\n- Group/environment write procs are now team-scoped; `deleteGroup` input\n  reshaped to `{ id }`. Reads stay public; `reorderGroups` stays global.\n- New batched `listObjectRelationsBulk` auth proc + store method resolves\n  owning team(s)/privacy for many resources of one type in one query.\n\nFrontend\n- Owner badge per row (batched `useResourcesManagedBy` + `ResourceOwnerBadge`),\n  membership chips collapsed to a count pill + searchable popover, and a\n  reusable per-row/bulk `ScopeToTeamAction` / `BulkScopeToTeamAction`.\n- Groups & Environments tabs gain row selection + a bulk bar (scope to team,\n  add system, delete), matching Systems; env bulk-attach is one desired-set\n  write so it cannot race.\n- Consistency: unified Edit pencil icon across tabs; Groups now edit through\n  the shared dialog editor (no more inline rename); Systems Health badges\n  stay side by side instead of wrapping.\n- Performance: re-keyed the oRPC wrapped-client cache on the stable utils root\n  (fixed the per-row rebuild GC storm), and moved anomaly/health-check per-row\n  data onto the shared CatalogBrowseDataBoundary bulk providers.\n- `@checkstack/ui`: `DataTable` per-column `truncate`; `RowAction` optional\n  `badge`.\n\nDocs updated (teams, systems-and-groups, environments, teams-and-access) and\ndocs index regenerated. Changesets added (minor; BETA, no major bumps).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* feat(frontend): smooth loading states and collapse secondary overview cards\n\nEliminate loading-state flashes and staggered reveals across the observability\nsurfaces, and collapse secondary system-overview cards by default via a shared\ncollapsible card.\n\n- Dashboard no longer flashes \"all systems healthy\": SystemSignalsSlot gains an\n  additive onLoadingChange report and the overview holds its skeleton until all\n  mounted signal sources settle (bounded by a grace period).\n- System detail overview cards reveal together instead of popping in one by one,\n  via an additive onLoadingChange on SystemDetailsSlot.\n- Catalog manage \"Health\" column shows a per-row skeleton until health data\n  settles (additive onLoading on CatalogBrowseHealthSlot); state badges stay on\n  one row.\n- Move the SLO card into the left monitoring column at the same width as\n  dependencies/health; only maintenances and incidents stay full width.\n- Dependencies and Logs/Metrics/Traces cards are collapsed by default via a new\n  shared CollapsibleDetailCard (@checkstack/ui) that single-sources the header\n  layout so every collapsible overview card is vertically centred and behaves\n  identically.\n- Remove dead, unreferenced dashboard-frontend/src/Dashboard.tsx.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* fix(ci): resolve ReDoS finding, align deps, cover flagged packages\n\n- Fix CodeQL js/polynomial-redos in k8s-events-common buildEventsListUrl:\n  replace the quantifier-anchored /\\/+$/ trailing-slash strip (on config-derived\n  apiServerUrl) with a linear codePoint scan + single slice; add a regression\n  test that collapses 100k trailing slashes well under a time bound.\n- Align external dependency ranges flagged by syncpack (deps:check) in the new\n  telemetry/tracestream packages: @orpc/client, @orpc/server, @playwright/test,\n  react, react-dom, react-router-dom to the monorepo-wide versions.\n- Add changesets for packages with real changes that lacked coverage:\n  @checkstack/frontend-api (wrapped-client cache-key perf fix),\n  @checkstack/test-utils-backend (load-lane gate + mock-queue error logging),\n  and @checkstack/automation-frontend + @checkstack/theme-frontend\n  (comment-only lint annotations).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* test(e2e): open the member count-pill on the environments tab\n\nThe catalog \"creates an environment and attaches a system to it\" spec asserted\nthe member system name was visible inline in the Environments table. This branch\nreplaced the inline member chips with the shared MembershipChips count-pill,\nwhich collapses members to a \"N systems\" pill and keeps the names in a\nlazily-mounted popover. Open the env row''s pill before asserting the member name\nso the test reflects the new (deliberate) de-bloated membership UI. Still\nverifies the exact member is present, just via the popover.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"34d869a48715bd6b02f82bbc99575e10c2fe67f9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/enyineer/checkstack/git/trees/34d869a48715bd6b02f82bbc99575e10c2fe67f9"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/enyineer/checkstack/git/commits/6c8b36bf4334ed92eb7f77738cc0754c536bf3b6","verification":{"payload":"tree 34d869a48715bd6b02f82bbc99575e10c2fe67f9\nparent dfbb66831073e255e80e5bcb0de578023c264a12\nauthor Nico <nico.enking@gmail.com> 1784159615 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1784159615 +0200\n\nfeat: telemetry platform (logs, metrics, traces) + team-scoped catalog (#473)\n\n* feat(backend-api,queue-api): shared guarded fetch and recurring-job reconcile helper\n\n- backend-api: createGuardedFetch/GuardedFetchError - SSRF-guarded fetch that\n  re-validates the host on every redirect hop, downgrades 301/302/303 to GET,\n  refuses non-replayable 307/308 bodies, strips credential headers on\n  cross-origin redirects, and supports maxRedirects: 0 (return 3xx as-is).\n- queue-api: reconcileRecurringJobs - desired-vs-actual convergence for\n  recurring jobs (idempotent schedule + ownsJobId-scoped orphan cancel,\n  parallel round-trips).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* feat(telemetry): platform source/sink abstraction for pluggable telemetry ingestion\n\nNew platform packages telemetry-common/backend/frontend:\n\n- Signal model (logs/metrics/traces) with OTel-shaped normalized records as\n  the source->sink lingua franca; signal coverage satisfies-enforced.\n- telemetrySinkExtensionPoint (stream owners contribute one sink per signal:\n  write, bind-time authorization, bindable-stream listing, batched stream\n  name resolution) and telemetrySourceExtensionPoint (pull, webhook and\n  listener source types; boot-time validation incl. a hard x-secret probe).\n- Team-scoped source instances (telemetry.source) with per-signal stream\n  bindings, encrypted-at-rest secret config fields on the shared\n  config-secret marker primitives, and SECRET_CLEAR_SENTINEL semantics.\n- Pull reconciler (on queue-api reconcileRecurringJobs) + runner with\n  SSRF-guarded fetch; per-pod listener lifecycle manager with event-bus\n  cross-pod convergence; rate-limited webhook endpoints with hash-only\n  rotatable secrets AND platform-verified vendor HMAC signatures\n  (GitHub/Slack descriptors, capped pre-auth body buffering).\n- telemetry-pull satellite capability: config push without secrets, JIT\n  per-field secret resolution, binding-authorized batch re-ingestion,\n  status mirroring, satellite authorship guard on binding.\n- Frontend: self-hiding StreamSourcesSection embed, source catalog with\n  schema-driven config dialogs, multi-signal binding editor, global\n  Sources page (manageCapability-gated), health hints, satellite picker,\n  webhook secret shown-once, connection testing, metadata-filtered\n  SourceConfigSlot.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* feat(logstream,metricstream): telemetry sinks, sources UI embeds and shared-helper migrations\n\n- Both backends contribute telemetry sinks reusing the exact push-endpoint\n  normalization (parity regression tests), with bind authorization,\n  bindable-stream listing and batched name resolution through the shared\n  sink-guards factory.\n- Frontends embed StreamSourcesSection (metricstream Sources tab, logstream\n  Settings tab).\n- metricstream migrations onto the new shared helpers: the Prometheus scrape\n  executor now uses the guarded fetch (every redirect hop re-validated) and\n  the scrape reconciler uses queue-api reconcileRecurringJobs.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* feat(satellite): telemetry-pull capability and shared interval scheduler\n\n- Generic CapabilityIntervalScheduler base extracted from the scrape\n  scheduler (timer reconcile, stable-id in-flight guard, concurrency cap,\n  JIT secret cache flushed on config push); scrape + telemetry-pull\n  schedulers compose it.\n- telemetry-pull agent side: executor registry (statically-linked\n  SatellitePullExecutor seam), per-instance scheduling, unbound-signal\n  drop, absolute-counter status; advertised via\n  CHECKSTACK_SATELLITE_TELEMETRY_PULL.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* docs(telemetry): developer guide for sources/sinks and regenerated docs index\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* feat(tracestream): distributed tracing streams with tail-based sampling\n\nAdd the tracestream service as the third stream type alongside logstream\nand metricstream:\n\n- core/tracestream-common: cktr_ token format, stream RLAC (team-scoped\n  tracestream.stream), config schema (hot 6h / retained 7d / summaries 30d,\n  keep-errors + slow>threshold + 1% deterministic baseline sampling), oRPC\n  contract (stream CRUD, keyset searchTraces, getTrace, RED op buckets,\n  cross-stream findTraceById), OTLP + native ingest parsing.\n- core/tracestream-backend: port-based storage (spans, summaries, service\n  ops, minute/hourly t-digest buckets, activity + important events) with\n  span idempotency via a (streamId,traceId,spanId) unique index, tail\n  sampling decision/sweep/rollup/cleanup jobs, OTLP-HTTP protobuf+JSON and\n  native JSON ingest endpoints, traces telemetry sink so telemetry sources\n  can bind trace streams.\n- core/tracestream-frontend: stream list + detail (Overview/Traces/\n  Services/Settings with URL state), trace waterfall viewer, span detail\n  panel, token management, ship-traces instructions, telemetry sources\n  embed.\n- @checkstack/ui: virtualized, isLowPower-aware TraceWaterfall chart with\n  Storybook story.\n- @checkstack/backend-api: promote the t-digest helpers out of\n  healthcheck-backend so tracestream''s RED rollups share one impl.\n- Developer-guide page + regenerated AI docs index.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* fix(otlp-wire,logstream,metricstream): signed int64 decoding and tuple-keyset event pagination\n\n- otlp-wire: decode OTLP AnyValue int64 as SIGNED two''s complement (a\n  negative attribute previously surfaced as a huge positive float) and keep\n  magnitudes beyond the JS safe-integer range as exact decimal strings\n  instead of silently rounding, matching proto3-JSON semantics.\n- logstream/metricstream: paginate important events on a (ts,id) tuple\n  keyset cursor instead of a bare timestamp, so events sharing a timestamp\n  can no longer be skipped or duplicated across pages.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* test: root-cause load-flaky tests across the suite\n\nEleven tests failed only under full-suite machine load. Each got a\nroot-cause fix, never a skip or weakened assertion:\n\n- Shared beforeAll fixtures where per-test I/O (script-package installs,\n  container spins) was being starved into timeouts.\n- Terminal-signal polling instead of wall-clock windows for retry/queue\n  tests; the memory-queue retry tests additionally size their poll safety\n  net at the suite''s starvation ceiling (an 8s net expired at 8.3s while\n  nominal ~10ms retry timers were pending).\n- Fake timers where real ones only added scheduler jitter (Tabs).\n- Deliberate commented starvation ceilings (~2x worst observed) for tests\n  whose work is genuinely heavy under contention: real bun subprocess\n  cold-starts (generate-sdk --check, scaffold standalone, shell scripts)\n  and a real Bun worker module-graph load (logstream worker round trip).\n- test-utils mock queue now logs caught handler errors as a message string\n  instead of the raw Error, so an expected failure in a passing test no\n  longer renders as a red uncaught-error block that counts as a suite\n  error.\n\nAll fixes verified in isolation and under a 16-worker CPU/FS/cold-start\ncontention harness.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* feat(correlation): cross-signal trace correlation for logs, traces and health checks\n\nLogs, traces, and health-check runs now link to each other through W3C\ntrace context:\n\n- logstream: searchEvents accepts an exact traceId filter (Explore gets a\n  matching, deep-linkable input) backed by a new partial (trace_id, ts)\n  index; the new cross-stream findEventsByTraceId returns per-stream match\n  groups post-filtered by the caller''s read grants, with a required time\n  window, a 50-stream group cap and a per-stream row cap enforced in SQL.\n- Per-stream config.traceExtraction rules (attribute paths + capture-group\n  body regex) populate trace/span ids for non-OTLP sources at the ingest\n  flush seam. Rules are validated at save by a conservative\n  backtracking-safety analyzer (rejects backreferences, quantified groups\n  containing quantifiers/alternation, and >2 unbounded quantifiers) so a\n  user-authored pattern can never stall the shared ingest worker. Carried\n  ids are normalized (trim/dash-strip/lowercase) at the same seam so\n  storage and the exact-match queries share one canonical form; query\n  inputs normalize identically.\n- Correlation slots: LogEventDetailSlot (logstream-common),\n  TraceCorrelationsSlot (tracestream-common), RunDetailExtrasSlot\n  (healthcheck-common, with extractRunTraceIds owning the run-result\n  shape). The trace view shows correlated log events grouped per readable\n  stream with windowed Explore deep links; log events and health-check\n  runs with a known trace id get a \"View trace\" jump resolved through\n  findTraceById, gated behind a cached any-trace-streams probe.\n- Docs: new trace-correlation developer-guide page + cross-links;\n  regenerated AI docs index.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* feat(healthcheck-http): emit a W3C traceparent header on every probe\n\nThe HTTP request collector stamps each probe with a traceparent header,\nDEFAULT-ON with a per-collector opt-out (emitTraceparent: false). Ids are\nfreshly generated per run (CSPRNG); a user-configured traceparent header\n(any casing) wins and is passed through verbatim with no id recorded. The\nsent trace id persists on the collector result as a text-annotated,\nnon-anomaly traceId field, powering the run detail panel''s \"View trace\"\njump when the probed application exports spans to a trace stream.\n\nBEHAVIOR CHANGE for existing checks: stored configs parse the new field as\ntrue, so pre-existing HTTP checks start sending the header (sampled flag\n01) on deploy - documented in the changeset with the opt-out.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* feat(tracestream): health-check strategy, collectors and error-spike fast path\n\nReader-only OBSERVABILITY strategy for trace streams:\n\n- trace-window collector: windowed span/trace totals, error counts,\n  errorRatePerMinute and secondsSinceLastSpan, read via a new SQL-SUM\n  op-bucket port (never digest-bearing rows on count paths).\n- operation-latency collector (repeatable): per service/operation p95Ms,\n  avgMs, maxMs and error rate; the window p95 merges every minute\n  bucket''s t-digest state into ONE digest before computing the\n  percentile (new queryWindowLatency port; an average of per-bucket\n  p95s is provably wrong and rejected by an integration test).\n- Fast-path re-evaluation: a flush that persists error spans enqueues\n  affected checks ahead of schedule (pod-local 15s debounce with\n  unmark-on-enqueue-failure, deterministic cluster-wide job id).\n- error_spike important event: post-commit trailing-average detection\n  evaluated at the freshest SPAN-TIME minute the flush touched (wall\n  clock misses bursts under normal OTLP export lag), deduped 10 min\n  cluster-wide via the shared events table with a pod-local gate.\n- Check-editor stream/service/operation dropdowns via shared resolver\n  constants in tracestream-common, filled by tracestream-frontend;\n  error_spike event visual.\n\nThe storage-port additions in this commit (sumWindowCounts,\nqueryWindowLatency, lastEventAt) also carry the satellite drop\naccounting seam introduced by the follow-up satellite commit.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* feat(satellite,tracestream): satellite trace forwarding with durable drop accounting\n\nSatellites with CHECKSTACK_SATELLITE_TRACE_RECEIVERS=1 expose local\n/v1/traces (OTLP protobuf + JSON) and /ingest/traces (native) receivers\non the shared receiver port, parsing with the same browser-safe parsers\nthe core uses and forwarding over the new \"tracestream\" telemetry\nchannel. The wire schema (satellite-relay.ts) carries ISO dates and\ndecimal-string nanosecond timestamps, enforces canonical lowercase-hex\nspan identities, and caps attributes/events/links at exactly the\nparsers'' limits (shared caps module), so a forwarded span can never be\nmore permissive or differently-identified than a direct push.\n\nThe core capability handler verifies each forwarded cktr_ token with\nthe same authenticator as direct pushes, re-clamps span times against\nthe CORE clock through the identical ingest pipeline, and mirrors\nlogstream''s ack semantics (retryable only on pure saturation; drop\nattribution is best-effort and can never flip an accepted batch''s\nack). Satellite in-transit drops are persisted per stream\n(dropped_in_transit_count on the activity table, additive migration\n0002) at parity with logstream/metricstream.\n\nIncludes the satellite-frontend capability badge, an env-guarded e2e\ntraces case, and satellite/tracestream documentation.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* refactor(healthcheck,ingest-utils): shared run-queue contract, window math and receiver chunking\n\nThree duplications, each now single-copy with the superseded local\ndeclarations deleted:\n\n- The health-check run-queue contract (HEALTH_CHECK_QUEUE,\n  HealthCheckJobPayload, fastPathJobId with a per-plugin prefix,\n  FAST_PATH_DEBOUNCE_MS) moves to @checkstack/healthcheck-common; the\n  queue owner (healthcheck-backend) and both fast-path enqueuers\n  (logstream, tracestream) import the same definitions, so the shape\n  can no longer drift. Enqueued job-id strings are byte-identical.\n- The observability window math (computeWindowBounds with its\n  complete-minute/readTo semantics, computeSecondsSinceLast,\n  MIN_WINDOW_SECONDS) moves to healthcheck-common; the logstream,\n  metricstream and tracestream copies (verified logic-identical by\n  function-body diff before extraction) now import it.\n- The satellite receivers'' batch chunking + byte-budget estimation\n  moves to @checkstack/ingest-utils (chunkTelemetryBatchItems,\n  estimateTelemetryItemBytes); the log/metric/trace receivers keep\n  only per-signal item shapes and caps, with behavior pinned by their\n  unchanged tests. The core-side handlers stay per-signal on purpose:\n  they differ structurally in re-normalization and ack accounting,\n  and parameterizing them would obscure the trust logic.\n\nAlso fixes logstream''s fast-path debounce to unmark a slice when its\nenqueue fails, matching the tracestream fix (a Redis blip no longer\nsilently drops a burst''s evaluation for the rest of the bucket).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* fix(ui,healthcheck-frontend): external resolver dependency refetch for cross-form pickers\n\nDynamicOptionsField refetched options only when the resolver name or\nthe field''s own x-depends-on values changed, so a resolver reading\nEXTERNAL state (the health-check editor''s strategyConfig - e.g. the\nselected stream backing logstream''s pattern picker and tracestream''s\nservice/operation pickers) could not express that dependency and only\nstayed fresh because the current editor happens to remount collector\npanels on navigation. DynamicForm now accepts an optional\nresolversDependencyKey included in the refetch effect''s deps; the\nhealth-check editor passes a memoized strategyConfig fingerprint to\ncollector forms only. Defense-in-depth: no reproducing symptom in\ntoday''s mutually-exclusive panel layout, but the mount-time-only\nfreshness guarantee is gone the first time a layout co-mounts the\nforms. Tested without remount, including a no-churn guard for\nunrelated in-form edits.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* test: explicit load-testing lane (bun run test:load)\n\nThe logstream and metricstream ingest load guards saturate the machine\nby design (sustained multi-thousand-rows/s ingest plus event-loop\npressure probes) and exist to diagnose performance regressions, not to\ngate every run. They now require CHECKSTACK_LOAD_TESTS=1 on top of the\nintegration lane (new isLoadTestEnabled helper in test-utils-backend),\nso package-level CHECKSTACK_IT=1 runs and CI''s integration job skip\nthem. Run them deliberately via the new root script:\n\n  bun run test:load\n  CHECKSTACK_IT_LOAD_MS=3000 bun run test:load   # quicker window\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* refactor(backend-api): one user-scoped RPC client for caller-identity re-entry\n\nPromote createUserScopedRpcClient + forwardableAuthHeadersFrom into\n@checkstack/backend-api as the single implementation of \"re-enter the\nlive router AS the calling user\". Only the session cookie and bearer\nAuthorization are ever forwarded; a request without them re-enters\nanonymous, so the gates built on it fail closed. ai-backend (deferred\ntool routing), status-page-backend (publish gate) and telemetry-backend\n(satellite binding auth) migrate to the shared export and their local\nnear-verbatim copies are deleted; the canonical unit test moves to\nbackend-api. Behavior is byte-identical - this removes six drift-prone\ncopies of security-sensitive header-forwarding code.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* feat(catalog,logstream,metricstream,tracestream): stream-to-system links and AI tool projections\n\nExplicit links between observability streams and catalog systems, plus\nmodel-facing read tools for all three stream plugins:\n\n- Each stream plugin declares the same four link procedures over its own\n  bare-systemId junction table (shared schemas + chunk/merge helpers in\n  telemetry-common). The replace-all write verifies the caller can READ\n  every NEWLY ADDED system - one user-scoped catalog getSystems\n  membership pass through an injected, test-substitutable authorizer,\n  existence-checked first - so a manager can never expose a system they\n  cannot see, and is never dead-locked by a link a broader-privileged\n  user authorized. Reverse lookups are grant-filtered (listKey).\n- catalog-frontend ships the shared StreamSystemLinksEditor (system\n  picker + explicitly-applied \"suggested from observed service names\"\n  chips with a loading affordance), StreamSystemLinksSettingsCard (one\n  draft/dirty/save implementation for all three Settings tabs) and\n  LinkedStreamsCard (one detail-page card chrome). Suggestion sources:\n  tracestream''s service catalog, metricstream label values, logstream''s\n  new bounded listServiceNames scan.\n- The catalog system page gains self-hiding Logs/Metrics/Traces cards\n  (SystemDetailsSlot) and the dashboard gains conservative per-stream\n  signals (SystemSignalsSlot): one bulk, client-chunked\n  listLinkedStreamStatuses per plugin, with the newest-event lookup\n  constrained to each plugin''s signal-worthy event types (a benign newer\n  event can no longer mask an active spike) and deterministic\n  tiebreaks; deleted-stream phantoms are never surfaced.\n- AI tool projections (read-only, RLAC via routed re-entry):\n  logstream.searchLogs (slimmed) / severityStats / listStreams;\n  metricstream.listStreams / listMetricNames / metricBuckets (the\n  unbounded raw-series read is deliberately not projected);\n  tracestream.searchTraces / getTraceSummary (spans reduced to seven\n  scalar fields) / serviceStats / listServices. The tool-registry guide\n  now documents the projectResult output-slimming convention.\n- New developer-guide page for the link contract; regenerated docs\n  index; per-plugin migrations (generated, additive).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* feat(telemetry): derive, push, and pull-health source-platform expansion\n\nThe platform gains three seams and the lifecycle machinery around them:\n\n- DERIVE sources (log-to-metric, log-to-trace built-ins): sink-owning\n  plugins tap their post-flush batches into a generation-guarded,\n  hook-invalidated dispatcher; lazy record thunks keep streams without\n  derive sources at zero cost; per-batch label-tuple cap bounds series\n  cardinality.\n- PUSH sources: per-instance bearer tokens minted by the platform\n  (hash-only, shown once, rotatable), verified by endpoint-owning\n  plugins through a type-scoped verifier service + createPushTokenLookup\n  over the shared ingest authenticator; cross-pod verdict eviction on\n  mint/rotate/disable/delete AND binding changes; migration 0002\n  promotes every non-revoked legacy stream token in place (same id,\n  hash, prefix) so existing shippers keep working; a marker-gated\n  one-shot backfills bound-stream team grants onto promoted sources.\n- Pull health hooks (onRunFailure/onRunRecovery) with stored\n  consecutive-failure counts, invoked on core and satellite-reported\n  runs.\n- Stream-deletion lifecycle (handleStreamDeleted): bound sources lose\n  the binding; binding-less sources are fully purged (secrets, schedule,\n  grants, token revoked) so deleted streams'' shippers get 401s instead\n  of black-holing data.\n- Listener manager only binds on the default instance (PR-preview port\n  isolation); satellite pull config resolves ${{ secrets.NAME }}\n  references just-in-time.\n\nCross-plugin migration ordering (promotion before owner drops) is\nguaranteed by topological migration order, now documented in the\nmigrations rule. The satellite e2e seed follows the promoted schema.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* feat(logstream): syslog listener, derive tap, and push sources on the platform\n\nLogstream''s ingest management moves fully onto the telemetry platform:\n\n- Syslog becomes the first LISTENER source type: a source instance with\n  port/TLS config bound to one stream replaces the env-var listener; the\n  binding is the authorization (no in-message tokens on the core path;\n  the satellite edge receiver keeps its token protocol). TLS cert/key\n  paths are validated at start so a broken intake surfaces as lastError.\n- The post-flush derive tap feeds the platform dispatcher detached and\n  lazily - a deriver can never slow or fail ingest, and streams without\n  derive sources pay no conversion cost.\n- Push tokens migrate to platform push sources: the ingest authenticator\n  resolves bearers through the platform verifier (logstream.push), the\n  token CRUD procs, token table (dropped), TokensSection UI, and\n  TokenUseTracker are removed outright, and stream deletion cascades\n  through the platform lifecycle. The stream detail page gains a\n  dedicated Sources tab.\n- Boot warns when the removed CHECKSTACK_LOGSTREAM_SYSLOG_PORT is set.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* feat(metricstream): scrape, push, and exemplars on the telemetry platform\n\n- Prometheus scraping becomes the pull source type\n  metricstream.prometheus-scrape; the private scrape extension point,\n  reconciler, CRUD, and UI are removed. A boot one-shot re-keys migrated\n  inline bearers into the platform''s secret channel and deletes each\n  internal secret after re-keying; scrape_failing important events are\n  restored through the platform''s pull health hooks (threshold 3, once\n  per outage episode).\n- Push tokens migrate to platform push sources (metricstream.push):\n  lookup swapped to the platform verifier, token CRUD/UI removed, and\n  BOTH legacy tables (metric_stream_tokens, metric_scrape_targets) are\n  dropped - safe because the platform''s promotion migrations run first\n  in topological order.\n- Metric points carry OTLP/OpenMetrics exemplars end to end: decoded,\n  folded per series, persisted by MERGING with stored exemplars in one\n  batched update per flush, returned windowed on chart reads, and\n  rendered as a click-through-to-trace lane; the telemetry sink and\n  satellite wire preserve them with full fidelity.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* feat(tracestream): push sources, drop-counter tiles, and shared trace links\n\n- Push tokens migrate to platform push sources (tracestream.push):\n  lookup swapped to the platform verifier, token CRUD/UI and the\n  TokenStore port removed, trace_stream_tokens dropped, and the new\n  cross-pod invalidation subscription closes tracestream''s previous\n  mint-vs-negative-cache race. Stream deletion cascades through the\n  platform lifecycle, and the stream detail page gains a dedicated\n  Sources tab.\n- The Overview tab surfaces the three drop counters (spans, traces,\n  in transit) as warn-toned stat tiles.\n- buildViewTraceHref is exported once from tracestream-common (consumed\n  by the metric chart''s exemplar lane); the token extractor aligns with\n  the ReDoS-hardened slice form.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* feat(satellite): statically-linked telemetry pull executors\n\nThe agent executes platform pull sources at the edge through a\nstatically-linked executor registry: the Prometheus scrape executor\n(honoring each instance''s own timeoutMs, sharing the size/series-capped\nresponse reader with core) and the Kubernetes events executor (wrapping\nthe SSRF guard around the shared pull driver, surfacing scan\ntruncation). Just-in-time secrets resolve fresh per run, so rotating a\nreferenced secret takes effect on the next pull; the legacy scrape\ncapability and CHECKSTACK_SATELLITE_SCRAPE flag are removed (with a\nboot warning when still set), and the satellite UI shows the\ntelemetry-pull capability badge.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* feat(k8s-events): Kubernetes events source plugin\n\nNew plugin pair contributing the first external pull source type:\ninterval-LISTs a cluster''s events.k8s.io/v1 events (request shapes\nverified against the official Kubernetes API reference) and ingests\nthem as log records - Warning events as warnings, with the regarding\nobject, reporting controller, and stable event uid as attributes.\nService-account bearer auth (encrypted at rest, JIT on satellites);\nnamespace/fieldSelector/labelSelector scoping. maxEventsPerPull caps\nEMITTED in-window records so an event backlog cannot starve recent\nevents; the scan is bounded by a 40-page budget with a truncation\nwarning for busy clusters and a transport error for servers that page\nforever without items.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* feat(telemetry-frontend): push token reveal, rotation, and shipper snippets\n\nThe sources UI absorbs everything the per-plugin token sections did:\npush types skip the empty config form and render descriptor-driven\nsetup snippets instead (OTel Collector YAML with the per-signal\nendpoint key, curl for native ingest, origin-prefixed, token\ninterpolated when present); creating a push source reveals the\nshown-once token through the generalized secret-reveal panel; push\nrows get a rotate action and last-received liveness from lastRunAt.\nEmpty-state and section copy now cover push, pull, webhook, listener,\nand derive modes.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* fix(healthcheck): run the config-secrets backfill after all plugins initialize\n\nStrategies contributed by other plugins register during THEIR init, and\nplugin init order follows the service-ref graph - so running the\nbackfill during healthcheck''s own init could scan configurations before\na contributor (e.g. logstream''s health strategy) had registered,\nskipping that strategy''s config with a boot warning. afterPluginsReady\nis the only phase that guarantees a complete registry; the backfill is\nidempotent, so previously-skipped configs converge on the next boot.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* docs(ai-backend): source-ecosystem documentation pass and regenerated index\n\nRewrites the shipping guides and concepts for the platform source\nmodel: push sources with shown-once rotatable tokens on every stream''s\nSources tab, the syslog listener source (multi-pod bind semantics\nstated per topology), the migrated Prometheus scrape flow, satellite\npull execution, and the developer-guide sections for the listener,\nderive, and push seams (including the push verifier composition for\nthird-party plugin authors). Regenerates the AI docs index over the\nnew content.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* test: consistent budgets for two contention-flaky tests\n\nThe template scaffolding tests granted their typecheck/eslint\nsubprocesses 60s but declared a 30s bun-test timeout, so under\nfull-suite load the test killed itself while its child was still\ninside its own budget (48.5s observed); the test ceiling now sits\nabove the subprocess budget. The script-packages coalescing test gets\na documented starvation ceiling (~2x worst observed 71.9s) - its\nassertions, not the clock, carry the correctness.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* fix(frontend): keep edit forms stable during background refetches\n\nEdit dialogs and settings pages re-seeded their local form state from the\nunderlying query inside a useEffect, so a webhook/realtime signal refetch (or\nany parent re-render passing a fresh initialData object) re-fired the effect\nand wiped the user''s in-progress edits mid-typing - most visibly when editing\na system''s description in the catalog.\n\nSeed once instead: on the dialog open transition (new useSeedFormOnOpen hook)\nor once per record (existing useInitOnceForKey), with gcTime:0 on one-shot\nloader queries so a warm cache cannot race the seed.\n\nAdds the shared useSeedFormOnOpen hook to @checkstack/ui and a warn-level\nESLint rule checkstack/no-state-seed-in-effect that flags the antipattern,\nnames the correct fix variant for each shape, and documents the\neslint-disable-next-line escape hatch for genuine one-way syncs (5 benign\nsites annotated with reasons).\n\nFixed surfaces: catalog system/environment/group editors, healthcheck\nplatform-defaults dialog, SLO / gitops-provider / telemetry-source /\nsatellite / announcement / role dialogs, and the cache / queue / notification\n/ secrets / anomaly / profile / strategies / ai settings.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* perf(scripts): run the test suite in a shared process, isolate only polluters\n\nReplaces `bun test --parallel` (a fresh worker + full module-graph reparse\nper file, ~11min pinning every core and thermally throttling laptops) with a\ntwo-pass partition: the bulk of the suite runs in ONE shared process, and only\nthe greppable set that leaks process globals runs `--isolate`. ~671s -> ~32s,\nmostly single-core, ~0.9GB peak, identical pass/fail.\n\nAdds the `checkstack/no-shared-process-test-pollution` ESLint rule (error): the\nrunner detects the isolate-set by grepping each TEST file, so a `mock.module` /\nglobal assignment / `spyOn(global*)` hidden in an importable non-test module\nwould defeat the partition and leak into unrelated suites. The rule forbids\nthose calls outside `*.test.*` files (the sanctioned test-preload aside).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* fix(frontend): unify system-overview cards on a shared DetailCard surface\n\nThe Logs/Metrics/Traces cards rendered flatter than every other card on the\nsystem overview page: their shared LinkedStreamsCard had drifted to a flat\n`bg-card` with a hairline-only shadow, while the sibling cards (health,\ndependency, SLO, incident, anomaly, maintenance) used the detail-page gradient\nplus a two-layer elevation shadow. That surface was a copy-pasted className (and\na per-file PANEL_SHADOW constant) in every card, with nothing keeping them in\nsync.\n\nExtract the surface into one primitive - `DetailCard` (+ `detailCardSurface` /\n`detailCardSurfaceFlat`) in @checkstack/ui - and render all seven\nsystem-overview cards from it, so they cannot diverge again. The three telemetry\ncards now match their siblings; the other six are pure refactors (same pixels,\ntheir local PANEL_SHADOW consts removed).\n\nAdd the `error`-level ESLint rule `checkstack/no-inline-detail-card-chrome`,\nscoped to that card family, which fails the build if a card re-declares the\nsurface inline (the --d-card-r radius, the from-surface-2 gradient, or the\nelevation shadow) instead of using DetailCard. New cards join the guard by\nadding their file to the rule''s scope in eslint.config.mjs.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* feat(catalog): team-scoped groups & environments with consistent manage tabs\n\nMake catalog Groups and Environments first-class team-manageable (RLAC)\nobjects for writes while keeping reads public, and polish the three manage\ntabs (Systems/Groups/Environments) for consistency and performance.\n\nAuthorization\n- New reusable create-authorization primitive `create.alsoAcceptCreatorOf`:\n  a creator capability on a sibling TYPE also authorizes creating this\n  resource (owning team still resolved normally). System creators may now\n  create team-scoped groups & environments. Extracted a shared\n  `hasCreateCapability` S2S check so the middleware gate and the frontend\n  `canCreate` verdict use one strict-creator implementation.\n- Group/environment write procs are now team-scoped; `deleteGroup` input\n  reshaped to `{ id }`. Reads stay public; `reorderGroups` stays global.\n- New batched `listObjectRelationsBulk` auth proc + store method resolves\n  owning team(s)/privacy for many resources of one type in one query.\n\nFrontend\n- Owner badge per row (batched `useResourcesManagedBy` + `ResourceOwnerBadge`),\n  membership chips collapsed to a count pill + searchable popover, and a\n  reusable per-row/bulk `ScopeToTeamAction` / `BulkScopeToTeamAction`.\n- Groups & Environments tabs gain row selection + a bulk bar (scope to team,\n  add system, delete), matching Systems; env bulk-attach is one desired-set\n  write so it cannot race.\n- Consistency: unified Edit pencil icon across tabs; Groups now edit through\n  the shared dialog editor (no more inline rename); Systems Health badges\n  stay side by side instead of wrapping.\n- Performance: re-keyed the oRPC wrapped-client cache on the stable utils root\n  (fixed the per-row rebuild GC storm), and moved anomaly/health-check per-row\n  data onto the shared CatalogBrowseDataBoundary bulk providers.\n- `@checkstack/ui`: `DataTable` per-column `truncate`; `RowAction` optional\n  `badge`.\n\nDocs updated (teams, systems-and-groups, environments, teams-and-access) and\ndocs index regenerated. Changesets added (minor; BETA, no major bumps).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* feat(frontend): smooth loading states and collapse secondary overview cards\n\nEliminate loading-state flashes and staggered reveals across the observability\nsurfaces, and collapse secondary system-overview cards by default via a shared\ncollapsible card.\n\n- Dashboard no longer flashes \"all systems healthy\": SystemSignalsSlot gains an\n  additive onLoadingChange report and the overview holds its skeleton until all\n  mounted signal sources settle (bounded by a grace period).\n- System detail overview cards reveal together instead of popping in one by one,\n  via an additive onLoadingChange on SystemDetailsSlot.\n- Catalog manage \"Health\" column shows a per-row skeleton until health data\n  settles (additive onLoading on CatalogBrowseHealthSlot); state badges stay on\n  one row.\n- Move the SLO card into the left monitoring column at the same width as\n  dependencies/health; only maintenances and incidents stay full width.\n- Dependencies and Logs/Metrics/Traces cards are collapsed by default via a new\n  shared CollapsibleDetailCard (@checkstack/ui) that single-sources the header\n  layout so every collapsible overview card is vertically centred and behaves\n  identically.\n- Remove dead, unreferenced dashboard-frontend/src/Dashboard.tsx.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* fix(ci): resolve ReDoS finding, align deps, cover flagged packages\n\n- Fix CodeQL js/polynomial-redos in k8s-events-common buildEventsListUrl:\n  replace the quantifier-anchored /\\/+$/ trailing-slash strip (on config-derived\n  apiServerUrl) with a linear codePoint scan + single slice; add a regression\n  test that collapses 100k trailing slashes well under a time bound.\n- Align external dependency ranges flagged by syncpack (deps:check) in the new\n  telemetry/tracestream packages: @orpc/client, @orpc/server, @playwright/test,\n  react, react-dom, react-router-dom to the monorepo-wide versions.\n- Add changesets for packages with real changes that lacked coverage:\n  @checkstack/frontend-api (wrapped-client cache-key perf fix),\n  @checkstack/test-utils-backend (load-lane gate + mock-queue error logging),\n  and @checkstack/automation-frontend + @checkstack/theme-frontend\n  (comment-only lint annotations).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n* test(e2e): open the member count-pill on the environments tab\n\nThe catalog \"creates an environment and attaches a system to it\" spec asserted\nthe member system name was visible inline in the Environments table. This branch\nreplaced the inline member chips with the shared MembershipChips count-pill,\nwhich collapses members to a \"N systems\" pill and keeps the names in a\nlazily-mounted popover. Open the env row''s pill before asserting the member name\nso the test reflects the new (deliberate) de-bloated membership UI. Still\nverifies the exact member is present, just via the popover.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LNFMjcLpE8iXUZHffW8voE\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqWB1/CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAe8QQALGneoPW3++k803wPyMS/JTj\nyyQ4MEwHmM5yual2V8ppOt1OSzVxH3SkFjlAYTrZBmnjkqNdINWuZR967ORf7isk\n9e0nilspoIg3Nl4tohH9S8Q4zAPfVT/UxDn+sf1pXc0S1Z7Dqq1n7uZCkdARe2pS\nCEtXg+EJmEtyMpvzGZZ1mPWzGqbwGumEEwbdD7FRNcZGjHFF6ov1AbYsia1eLbt0\nbxj5VdT6hzlu4DLpyXyOVHReO+3qz0+qMxDGkjBBjpKETEG3V+c6m+5u3XDkfUjF\nEKXOOCVI/ZxGHhQy2YPNFyOTrotcEYUWhvq3iSbLVlQQZKwFfYc/iMN65TZh+ns7\nxhuUAp+5yKpmni5/8CgdmgAXpwLVWqzLbuc/dEvBVUM3jj5azUrpKY8n7Us0szcM\nDW+X6x/qytToVkADiiz16bQrFh9MG6HTKIiuTiTME2lwzkZ8Vvwrm/2452HCcuvu\nRXso1+RTOtrG8yU8oxldvv9uu/kqYhh5pSCqjFlc09TMTTG8V3+NHg/q6ieguWyv\nw2g3eepsIwYFJc3xp0sEIK8oAWbwISY2AYo+Hd99hKSc7fe59o9jqytO5l2lGM+z\n8YMRlO79b3eCU6zf51cd8fxpVz2bow2wPhhCbuT2UuplxvMcNqmkdiADzht9QJWr\nuU5wQLH1ZJYwb0pBEXnZ\n=F88X\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","