OQrp5TdoAKDJhMTAxNmM1MDI5YThjMzA0YTZjNWQwMzY1NWJmYzViMjU0ZmZmNGQ","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/enyineer/checkstack/commit/64b3f8ae7a17cb8f06be14e51cddc5bee72a78b1","sha":"64b3f8ae7a17cb8f06be14e51cddc5bee72a78b1","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/enyineer/checkstack/commits/64b3f8ae7a17cb8f06be14e51cddc5bee72a78b1"}],"sha":"2a1016c5029a8c304a6c5d03655bfc5b254fff4d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/enyineer/checkstack/commits/2a1016c5029a8c304a6c5d03655bfc5b254fff4d"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/27201677?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/enyineer","id":27201677,"login":"enyineer","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI3MjAxNjc3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/enyineer/checkstack/commits/64b3f8ae7a17cb8f06be14e51cddc5bee72a78b1/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-07-08T07:32:01Z","email":"nico.enking@gmail.com","name":"Nico"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-07-08T07:32:01Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"test(healthcheck): real-Redis coverage for BullMQ recurring jobs + reconciler (#419)\n\nAdd two `*.it.test.ts` suites (gated behind CHECKSTACK_IT, run by the\nIntegration lane against the Redis service container):\n\n- bullmq-queue.it.test.ts: drives the real BullMQQueue recurring interface\n  against Redis. Pins the upsert subtleties a mocked bullmq cannot: re-scheduling\n  the same jobId REPLACES the template data + interval (no duplicate scheduler)\n  and a produced job carries the upserted payload, plus interval->cron switching,\n  idempotent/unknown cancel, and null-environmentId + priority round-trip.\n- schedule-reconciler.it.test.ts: runs the real reconcileHealthCheckJobs against\n  Redis (mocked db/catalog) so planReconcile decisions are driven by genuine\n  list/getDetails read-back: per-env fan-out, env-less bare job, idempotent\n  no-op, in-place interval reschedule, orphan cancellation, system-scoped runs\n  never sweeping other systems, foreign-prefix jobs untouched, and env-less->\n  per-env transition. Drives raw bullmq via a queue-api shim to keep the\n  dependency direction clean; adds bullmq as a devDependency (as automation\n  already does).\n\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"1a0e16c81fa950de73b4e87a5c40eaeff943b088","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/enyineer/checkstack/git/trees/1a0e16c81fa950de73b4e87a5c40eaeff943b088"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/enyineer/checkstack/git/commits/64b3f8ae7a17cb8f06be14e51cddc5bee72a78b1","verification":{"payload":"tree 1a0e16c81fa950de73b4e87a5c40eaeff943b088\nparent d0eddc9d59b6e01574294b98043375d425d00c85\nauthor Nico <nico.enking@gmail.com> 1783495921 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1783495921 +0200\n\ntest(healthcheck): real-Redis coverage for BullMQ recurring jobs + reconciler (#419)\n\nAdd two `*.it.test.ts` suites (gated behind CHECKSTACK_IT, run by the\nIntegration lane against the Redis service container):\n\n- bullmq-queue.it.test.ts: drives the real BullMQQueue recurring interface\n  against Redis. Pins the upsert subtleties a mocked bullmq cannot: re-scheduling\n  the same jobId REPLACES the template data + interval (no duplicate scheduler)\n  and a produced job carries the upserted payload, plus interval->cron switching,\n  idempotent/unknown cancel, and null-environmentId + priority round-trip.\n- schedule-reconciler.it.test.ts: runs the real reconcileHealthCheckJobs against\n  Redis (mocked db/catalog) so planReconcile decisions are driven by genuine\n  list/getDetails read-back: per-env fan-out, env-less bare job, idempotent\n  no-op, in-place interval reschedule, orphan cancellation, system-scoped runs\n  never sweeping other systems, foreign-prefix jobs untouched, and env-less->\n  per-env transition. Drives raw bullmq via a queue-api shim to keep the\n  dependency direction clean; adds bullmq as a devDependency (as automation\n  already does).\n\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqTfzxCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAVPsQACUwHdAz40DSoj4OnnoMex8E\njGSgwuw6fD2/kkTFs9pUV3zW5bEWNuRD5clp0bMM3DJpihb1dsCoK32tTmgXPnSu\nYKAcDUJOcT9bcr7Ht22svqqb+/eqDlqsS3RbB4aPllgW38DWqaR8mBSn+XTdv4Np\neY8oU2bCVo//tkgBPBMKGU5hZ0HPcJps/40V/gbYekfE6vKdgw6D4NbKZXwL+Py5\nMhZ1YPT4mdsRZw1DioprHpnaM8I4PoxyQDdhXtzMehvA02yRx5OBvJ5kOlzvrs4z\n3bJQweMl+0zCIE5YInDP1bUPGC96mRKS3RFswhmEt8PcD/ZS8xAUu4vhLLqAYElR\nwOMJdq7pV+AeiLIdj9qQdcmOyny2TcPKduZYmJBdiqAb9CBEbBTXdEa2zHSDNF99\ngG+49FZuTjSJONnpPXN1o9LMmYtxNbIqg9LrjY3R42PSY9GAwZFfz6GARgGMVgdz\n0UtzIEXqnZ/RXrPmCrFEUC89eGtCsJ1lysWQgLhDbziGzq2AZWeKXtoXt1UK9TG6\neV4uc9eQBKOB/hnsopp4ERG9G6umLv7GFh2O/xh0zi3hOeaKCrfGW3JzTaf29qZE\nIJdEVhIy+tH26qqJ0fqzRXaSuiLzyXKj7iLu68UAsNF28rs5tHFJb52/59Pi8yO9\nv9L97jwFtnr3uU1P4BCo\n=mkTA\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-07-08T07:32:02Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/enyineer/checkstack/commit/64b3f8ae7a17cb8f06be14e51cddc5bee72a78b1","node_id":"C_kwDOQrp5TdoAKDY0YjNmOGFlN2ExN2NiOGYwNmJlMTRlNTFjZGRjNWJlZTcyYTc4YjE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/enyineer/checkstack/commit/d0eddc9d59b6e01574294b98043375d425d00c85","sha":"d0eddc9d59b6e01574294b98043375d425d00c85","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/enyineer/checkstack/commits/d0eddc9d59b6e01574294b98043375d425d00c85"}],"sha":"64b3f8ae7a17cb8f06be14e51cddc5bee72a78b1","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/enyineer/checkstack/commits/64b3f8ae7a17cb8f06be14e51cddc5bee72a78b1"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/27201677?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/enyineer","id":27201677,"login":"enyineer","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI3MjAxNjc3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/enyineer/checkstack/commits/d0eddc9d59b6e01574294b98043375d425d00c85/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-07-08T06:25:22Z","email":"nico.enking@gmail.com","name":"Nico"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-07-08T06:25:22Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"perf(healthcheck): per-environment jobs + slow-check bulkhead (#418)\n\n* perf(healthcheck): batch scoped-db reads and add opt-in OTel/Prometheus metrics\n\nTwo grounded performance changes plus the instrumentation that grounds them.\n\nScoped-DB batching:\n- New `withScopedTransaction` helper (@checkstack/backend-api) runs several\n  scoped queries under a single `SET LOCAL search_path` transaction instead of\n  paying BEGIN/SET LOCAL/COMMIT per query.\n- `getSystemHealthStatus` (the platform''s hottest read, a 1+N fan-out) and the\n  executor''s run+aggregate writes now run as one transaction each.\n- Fixes a dashboard \"Recent activity\" React-key collision.\n\nOpt-in OpenTelemetry metrics (off by default, no-op until enabled):\n- `instrumentation` module in @checkstack/backend-api exposes lazy instrument\n  accessors any plugin can record through.\n- @checkstack/backend owns the SDK bootstrap: `startMetrics()` registers a\n  Prometheus exporter (127.0.0.1:9464) gated by `CHECKSTACK_METRICS_ENABLED`,\n  plus host-owned pool + event-loop-delay instruments.\n- Scoped-DB proxy, health-check executor (execution + per-phase histograms),\n  and the in-memory queue record through the accessors.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n* perf(healthcheck): reuse connections and de-cluster the scheduling herd\n\nGrounded in the live OTel phase histograms: per-run health-check wall time was\ndominated by TCP/TLS connection setup under a self-inflicted burst, not by slow\ntargets, CPU, or the database.\n\n- fix(queue-memory): honor `startDelay` in `scheduleRecurring`. It was silently\n  dropped, so every recurring job fired at boot and then on a boot-anchored\n  interval grid, keeping equal-interval checks phase-aligned forever. The first\n  fire is now deferred by `startDelay` and the recurrence is anchored to it\n  (contract-compliant; no-delay callers unchanged).\n- feat(healthcheck): jitter each check''s first fire by a stable, deterministic\n  fraction of its interval so a synchronized set spreads across the interval\n  instead of hammering targets at the same instant. The offset persists across\n  all subsequent runs because the queue anchors the recurrence to the first fire.\n- perf(healthcheck-http): cache the TCP/TLS connect-timing probe per origin. Bun\n  `fetch` already reuses pooled connections (verified: warm reuse survives 20s+\n  idle), but the probe opened a fresh handshake every run, mis-reporting latency\n  and doubling connection count under a burst. It now runs at most once per origin\n  per 60s TTL; measured probe handshakes dropped from per-run to per-origin.\n\nBehaviour is otherwise unchanged (health status, assertions, reported phases);\nthere are simply far fewer connections and the herd is spread out.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n* fix(queue-bullmq): honor startDelay in scheduleRecurring to de-cluster the herd\n\nThe BullMQ backend dropped `startDelay` in `scheduleRecurring`. Its `every`\nscheduler captures the grid phase from whenever `upsertJobScheduler` first runs\n(nextMillis = now, then prevMillis + every), so a bootstrap loop scheduling many\nequal-interval jobs at ~the same instant hands them all the same phase - a\nthundering herd, the Redis-backend twin of the in-memory bug.\n\n`scheduleRecurring` now pins the first fire to `now + startDelay` via the\nscheduler''s `startDate`, which BullMQ uses as the initial nextMillis and anchors\nthe whole recurrence to. The same jittered startDelay the health-check scheduler\nalready computes now de-clusters checks on Redis identically to the in-memory\nbackend. Cron schedules (absolute times) are unaffected; no-startDelay callers\nare unchanged (immediate first fire).\n\nVerified against real Redis: 20 equal-interval checks scheduled in one loop land\non a single phase without startDelay, and spread evenly across the full interval\nwith the jittered startDelay.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n* perf(healthcheck-http): make connect probe background-only; label estimate in UI\n\nLive OTel phase histograms showed the remaining latency outliers (e.g. 1300ms\nruns while the median was ~400ms) came from the connect-timing probe: pinned to\none resolved IP it can be far slower than the reused fetch (intermittently\n~1500ms vs ~90ms for the same host, an IPv6 SYN-retry the real request never\npays), and exec still `await`ed it on the once-per-TTL refresh run. That violates\nthe collector rule that best-effort timing must never delay the check.\n\n- The per-origin probe now refreshes the connect/TLS sample fully in the\n  BACKGROUND and is never awaited on a request''s critical path. Same-origin\n  checks dedupe so only one refresh probe runs per origin at a time. Each run\n  reads whatever sample is cached (possibly none on the first run to an origin,\n  until the background probe resolves), so a slow probe can no longer inflate a\n  check''s latency or the reported connect/TLS phase.\n- The run detail UI now labels the estimate: DNS/wait/transfer are measured on\n  the request, while connection and TLS setup are sampled from a periodic\n  per-host probe and cached briefly (about a minute), so the cached value is not\n  misread as a per-run measurement.\n\nAdds a regression test proving exec never awaits the probe (a probe that never\nresolves does not delay or hang the check).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n* docs(observability): show enabling metrics via `bun run dev`\n\nThe metrics enablement section only showed the `--filter start` form; add the\ndev-cockpit form (`CHECKSTACK_METRICS_ENABLED=1 bun run dev`), which is how the\nflag is set locally and propagates to the spawned backend. Regenerate the AI\ndocs index to match.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n* feat(observability): queue-backlog gauge + fix in-memory backlog undercount\n\nAdds the queue depth metric and makes it trustworthy under the exact saturation\nit exists to detect. Surfaced by a scale harness driving the real hot path.\n\n- New `checkstack.queue.jobs` observable gauge (state=pending|processing),\n  registered once the QueueManager exists. `pending` is the scale signal: a\n  backlog that climbs without draining means work arrives faster than the queue\n  concurrency can execute it. No-op unless metrics are enabled.\n- Fix: the in-memory queue undercounted `pending`. `processNext` removed a job\n  from the pending list and only then awaited a concurrency slot in `processJob`,\n  so jobs blocked on a slot were invisible (not pending, not processing). Under\n  saturation the backlog read ~0 while hundreds of jobs were queued. Slot-waiters\n  are now counted in `pending`; `processing` still counts only executing jobs.\n- Docs: observability guide gains the metric + guidance to read `pending`\n  against lock-pool waiting to tell slot-bound from database-bound.\n\nMeasured with the harness: 20% unreachable checks (each pinning a slot for the\nfull timeout) drove the backlog 0 -> 700+ in 35s with lock-pool waiting at 0 -\nthe first scaling ceiling is concurrency-slot saturation by slow checks, not the\ndatabase. A separate lock-pool micro-benchmark shows rollup-write throughput\nplateaus at ~1900/s (lockPool max 10) regardless of queue concurrency.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n* test(healthcheck): add scale harness for slot + advisory-lock ceilings\n\nA developer diagnostic that drives the real health-check hot path so the two\nscaling ceilings can be measured, not guessed. Composes the real components:\nthe concurrency-gated in-memory queue, the real HTTP strategy (real TCP/TLS to\na local echo server), the real advisory-lock rollup on the real lock/admin\npools, and a faithful copy of the scoped-db transaction shape. Enables metrics\nso it also exercises the queue-backlog gauge live.\n\n- MODE=load: N recurring checks at concurrency C with a SLOW_FRAC of\n  unreachable targets (they abort at the timeout, pinning a concurrency slot).\n  Surfaces slot saturation: `pending` backlog climbs while `lockWait` stays 0.\n- MODE=lockpool: M advisory-xact-lock rollup writes at increasing concurrency\n  K. Surfaces the lock-pool ceiling: throughput plateaus once K passes the pool\n  max and extra concurrency only adds lock-wait.\n\nRoot-script constraints honored: workspace code imported via relative source\npaths, the DB layer reuses the backend''s exported pools (no bare pg/drizzle),\nisolated `scale_harness` schema dropped on exit.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n* feat(healthcheck): slow-check bulkhead primitives (adaptive timeout, classifier, lane)\n\nPure, tested building blocks for the per-env slow-check bulkhead + adaptive\ntimeout (wired into the executor in a follow-up):\n\n- adaptive-timeout: shrink a suspect check''s timeout toward its OWN measured\n  healthy latency, never below it; four guardrails make a recovery deadlock\n  impossible (no-baseline => no shrink, healthy-only baseline, periodic\n  full-timeout recovery probe, one-success snap-back).\n- slow-check-classifier: per-env classification from durable recent runs -\n  suspect = last K runs are SLOW transport failures (latency >= timeout x\n  fraction), so a fast connect-refused is not penalised; plus p95 healthy\n  baseline and recovery-probe cadence.\n- suspect-lane: pod-local work-conserving bulkhead - non-blocking tryAdmit with\n  capacity cap + single-flight per key.\n- slow-check-config: env-driven runtime (lane capacity + tuning), kill-switch.\n- checkstack.healthcheck.deferred counter (reason=lane_full|in_flight).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n* chore(rebase): drop getSystemHealthStatus batching test, regen docs-index\n\nRebase onto #417 (fan-out-aware notifications/counts). #417 rewrote\ngetSystemHealthStatus with per-env slice accounting; the earlier\nwithScopedTransaction batching of that method was taken out in favour of #417''s\ntested version, so its batching test is removed. Re-applying read batching on\nthe fan-out-aware method is tracked as a follow-up. docs-index regenerated.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n* wip(healthcheck): single-env executor + convergence reconciler (incomplete)\n\nSAVEPOINT - does not compile yet; callers not wired. Preserves:\n- executor runs exactly one env from payload.environmentId (fan-out loop +\n  inline rollup + anyEnvNotified removed; rollup moves to an event-driven\n  consumer).\n- schedule-reconciler.ts: convergence controller (planReconcile diff +\n  reconcileHealthCheckJobs, full/system-scoped).\n\nRemaining: wire index/router/gitops/run_now to the reconciler, rework executor\ntests for single-env, Phase 2 rollup consumer, bulkhead per-job wiring.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n* feat(healthcheck): per-environment recurring jobs + convergence reconciler\n\nMigrate health-check scheduling from one recurring job per (config, system)\nthat fans out over environments inside a single tick to ONE recurring job per\n(config, system, environment) slice. Each job now runs exactly its own\nenvironment and holds a concurrency slot only for that one probe, so a slow or\nfailing environment can no longer stall its siblings (per-env isolation is now\nstructural, not loop-guarded).\n\n- queue-executor: single-env executor. `HealthCheckJobPayload.environmentId`\n  is required; the executor validates the payload''s env against the CURRENT\n  effective set (assignment `environmentIds` INTERSECT catalog membership) and\n  SKIPS a stale tick (env-less job for a system that gained envs, or an env no\n  longer effective), leaving the reconciler to converge. A catalog-resolution\n  failure keeps an env-scoped probe running with degraded (empty) fields and\n  still broadcasts ENVIRONMENT_RESOLUTION_FAILED. `bootstrapHealthChecks` and\n  the post-loop system-rollup write are removed (rollup moves to a Phase 2\n  event-driven consumer).\n- schedule-reconciler: k8s-controller-style convergence. Desired per-env job\n  set derives entirely from Postgres + catalog membership, so every pod plans\n  the same. Full reconcile (boot/periodic) serializes on the `health:reconcile`\n  advisory lock and cancels orphaned jobs; a system-scoped reconcile only\n  adds/updates. Wired into boot, assignment mutations (router), and GitOps\n  system reconcile.\n- run_now automation: enqueues one one-off job per effective environment slice\n  (env-less assignment => a single `environmentId: null` run).\n- service: `resolveEnqueueEnvironmentIds` shared helper mirrors the executor''s\n  fan-out resolution for run_now.\n\nBoot/GitOps/router now call `reconcileHealthCheckJobs`; the notification\nrollup dedup (#417) becomes structural under per-env jobs (env-scoped runs\nnever notify the bare rollup).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n* test(healthcheck): convergence reconciler unit + integration coverage\n\nplanReconcile pure-diff cases (schedule missing, no-op unchanged, reschedule on\ninterval change, cancel orphans only on full reconcile, never touch foreign\njobs, system-scoped add/update-only) plus reconcileHealthCheckJobs driven\nagainst in-memory queue/db/catalog mocks (per-env fan-out, env-less collapse,\norphan cancellation, health:reconcile advisory lock, scoped no-cancel).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n* feat(healthcheck): event-driven debounced system-rollup consumer (Phase 2)\n\nUnder per-environment jobs each run writes only its own\n`<systemId>::<env>` health entity; the bare `<systemId>` ROLLUP entity (the\nworst-status view badges, SLO rules, dependency maps and system-level\nautomations reference) is no longer recomputed inline. This consumer closes\nthat gap without re-coupling the executor to the rollup:\n\n- Subscribes to per-env `health` ENTITY_CHANGED with work-queue delivery\n  (once per cluster per change) and ignores bare-rollup changes, so the\n  consumer''s own rollup write can never re-trigger it (no feedback loop).\n- Debounces per system into a fixed time-bucket jobId\n  (`healthrollup:<systemId>:<bucket>`), so a burst of env transitions coalesces\n  to one recompute on both queue backends; the fresh bucket id per window means\n  BullMQ completed-job retention never drops a later window''s job.\n- The rollup-queue consumer recomputes the bare `<systemId>` entity via\n  `recomputeSystemRollupHealth`, which diffs prev→next under the\n  `health:<systemId>` advisory lock, emits the rollup ENTITY_CHANGED, and (on a\n  real change) invalidates the cache + broadcasts SYSTEM_STATUS_CHANGED.\n\nNotifications stay owned by env runs (structural #417 dedup): the consumer\nnever notifies, so a fanned-out system''s rollup notification is always\nsuppressed, while an env-less system notifies directly from its run.\n\n`recomputeSystemRollupHealth` gains optional signalService/cache params (the\nprev read + SYSTEM_STATUS_CHANGED broadcast only run when a caller wants the\nsystem-level signal; pause/resume keeps its single-read entity-only recompute).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n* feat(healthcheck): wire slow-check bulkhead + adaptive timeout into executor\n\nEngage the previously-built primitives (suspect lane, per-env classifier,\nadaptive timeout) in the single-env executor so a slot-hogging check can no\nlonger starve healthy ones:\n\n- Before each run, classify the (config, system, env) slice''s recent runs.\n  A slice whose last K runs were SLOW transport failures (held their slot ~the\n  full timeout) is \"suspect\".\n- A suspect slice is admitted to a capped, pod-local lane (single-flight keyed\n  on the slice) and probed with a timeout SHRUNK toward its own healthy-latency\n  baseline (adaptiveTimeout guardrails: no baseline → no shrink, healthy-only\n  baseline, periodic full-timeout recovery probe, absolute floor). When the\n  lane is full or a prior run of the same slice is still in flight, the tick is\n  DEFERRED: records nothing (so it can''t pile up) and increments\n  `checkstack.healthcheck.deferred{reason}`. The recurring job stays scheduled,\n  so the next tick retries once the lane drains.\n- A healthy slice never touches the lane and always runs at the full timeout.\n- The lane slot is released in a finally on every exit path.\n\nThe decision is extracted into `evaluateSlowCheckAdmission` (pure but for the\nlane acquire) for direct unit testing; the executor just reads the slice''s\nrecent runs, applies the verdict, and releases the slot. Resolved once at\nworker startup from env (kill switch: CHECKSTACK_HEALTHCHECK_SLOW_LANE_ENABLED=0);\n`setupHealthCheckWorker` accepts an explicit runtime for tests.\n\nPer-env isolation is now complete: one failing env of a multi-env system is\nbulkheaded and adaptively timed out on its OWN slice while sibling envs (their\nown jobs) run untouched.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n* perf(healthcheck): batch getSystemHealthStatus 1+N reads into one transaction\n\n`getSystemHealthStatus` reads the system''s enabled associations then one\nrun-window per association — a 1+N scoped-db fan-out where each standalone query\nopened its own BEGIN/SET LOCAL/COMMIT and checked a connection out. Wrap the\nwhole read fan-out (associations + every per-check window, plus the pure per-env\nevaluation) in a single `withScopedTransaction`, collapsing 1+N transactions to\none connection held for the batch. Re-applies the batching that was dropped\nresolving the rebase conflict against the #417 fan-out rewrite, now on top of\nthe per-environment worst-wins rollup.\n\nAdds a regression test pinning that exactly one `db.transaction` is opened per\ncall regardless of check count.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n* test(harness): add BULKHEAD mode to the health-check scale harness\n\nThe load harness modelled only the baseline (no slow-check protection). Add\n`BULKHEAD=1` (+ `LANE_CAP`) which engages the real `evaluateSlowCheckAdmission`\n(suspect lane + adaptive timeout) in the consume handler: a suspect system is\nadmitted to a capped lane or DEFERRED (recording nothing), so slot-hogging\nchecks stop starving healthy ones. Slow systems are seeded as already-suspect\n(the \"host already down at pod start\" case; production has this history on tick\none). Reports `deferrals` in the summary for a direct before/after.\n\nMeasured (N=240, 20% unreachable, conc 10, 5s timeout, 35s):\n  BULKHEAD=0: finalBacklog=774 (climbing), completions=288, timeouts=60\n  BULKHEAD=1: finalBacklog=0 (bounded),   completions=862, timeouts=12, deferrals=207\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n* docs(healthcheck): document per-env execution, reconciler, rollup, bulkhead\n\nAdd a health-check execution & scheduling page covering per-environment\nrecurring jobs, the convergence reconciler, the event-driven debounced rollup\nconsumer, and the slow-check bulkhead + adaptive timeout (with harness numbers).\nAdd the checkstack.healthcheck.deferred counter to the observability metrics\nreference and regenerate the AI docs search index. Include the per-environment\njobs + bulkhead changeset.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n* fix(harness): clamp request-controlled echo delay to bound resource use\n\nCodeQL flagged the scale harness''s loopback echo server passing a\nrequest-query-derived duration straight to setTimeout (js/resource-exhaustion).\nClamp the delay to a fixed 120s ceiling and reject non-finite values before\nscheduling the timer.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n* fix(harness): key echo delay on path, not a request-supplied value\n\nThe Math.min clamp still left a request-query -> setTimeout dataflow that CodeQL\nflags as js/resource-exhaustion. Route the echo server on the path (`/slow` vs\n`/fast`) and delay by a fixed server-side constant, so no request-controlled\nvalue ever reaches setTimeout.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n* chore(changeset): cover @checkstack/test-utils-backend withTransactionMock\n\nThe batching regression test relies on a new `withTransactionMock` export in\n@checkstack/test-utils-backend, a versioned (non-private) package, so bump it\npatch and document the helper.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"c272ebae9ae765586e4c392707cc8169175627fd","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/enyineer/checkstack/git/trees/c272ebae9ae765586e4c392707cc8169175627fd"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/enyineer/checkstack/git/commits/d0eddc9d59b6e01574294b98043375d425d00c85","verification":{"payload":"tree c272ebae9ae765586e4c392707cc8169175627fd\nparent 8aae4e2eeb35078b0251726708607f4c547f9338\nauthor Nico <nico.enking@gmail.com> 1783491922 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1783491922 +0200\n\nperf(healthcheck): per-environment jobs + slow-check bulkhead (#418)\n\n* perf(healthcheck): batch scoped-db reads and add opt-in OTel/Prometheus metrics\n\nTwo grounded performance changes plus the instrumentation that grounds them.\n\nScoped-DB batching:\n- New `withScopedTransaction` helper (@checkstack/backend-api) runs several\n  scoped queries under a single `SET LOCAL search_path` transaction instead of\n  paying BEGIN/SET LOCAL/COMMIT per query.\n- `getSystemHealthStatus` (the platform''s hottest read, a 1+N fan-out) and the\n  executor''s run+aggregate writes now run as one transaction each.\n- Fixes a dashboard \"Recent activity\" React-key collision.\n\nOpt-in OpenTelemetry metrics (off by default, no-op until enabled):\n- `instrumentation` module in @checkstack/backend-api exposes lazy instrument\n  accessors any plugin can record through.\n- @checkstack/backend owns the SDK bootstrap: `startMetrics()` registers a\n  Prometheus exporter (127.0.0.1:9464) gated by `CHECKSTACK_METRICS_ENABLED`,\n  plus host-owned pool + event-loop-delay instruments.\n- Scoped-DB proxy, health-check executor (execution + per-phase histograms),\n  and the in-memory queue record through the accessors.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n* perf(healthcheck): reuse connections and de-cluster the scheduling herd\n\nGrounded in the live OTel phase histograms: per-run health-check wall time was\ndominated by TCP/TLS connection setup under a self-inflicted burst, not by slow\ntargets, CPU, or the database.\n\n- fix(queue-memory): honor `startDelay` in `scheduleRecurring`. It was silently\n  dropped, so every recurring job fired at boot and then on a boot-anchored\n  interval grid, keeping equal-interval checks phase-aligned forever. The first\n  fire is now deferred by `startDelay` and the recurrence is anchored to it\n  (contract-compliant; no-delay callers unchanged).\n- feat(healthcheck): jitter each check''s first fire by a stable, deterministic\n  fraction of its interval so a synchronized set spreads across the interval\n  instead of hammering targets at the same instant. The offset persists across\n  all subsequent runs because the queue anchors the recurrence to the first fire.\n- perf(healthcheck-http): cache the TCP/TLS connect-timing probe per origin. Bun\n  `fetch` already reuses pooled connections (verified: warm reuse survives 20s+\n  idle), but the probe opened a fresh handshake every run, mis-reporting latency\n  and doubling connection count under a burst. It now runs at most once per origin\n  per 60s TTL; measured probe handshakes dropped from per-run to per-origin.\n\nBehaviour is otherwise unchanged (health status, assertions, reported phases);\nthere are simply far fewer connections and the herd is spread out.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n* fix(queue-bullmq): honor startDelay in scheduleRecurring to de-cluster the herd\n\nThe BullMQ backend dropped `startDelay` in `scheduleRecurring`. Its `every`\nscheduler captures the grid phase from whenever `upsertJobScheduler` first runs\n(nextMillis = now, then prevMillis + every), so a bootstrap loop scheduling many\nequal-interval jobs at ~the same instant hands them all the same phase - a\nthundering herd, the Redis-backend twin of the in-memory bug.\n\n`scheduleRecurring` now pins the first fire to `now + startDelay` via the\nscheduler''s `startDate`, which BullMQ uses as the initial nextMillis and anchors\nthe whole recurrence to. The same jittered startDelay the health-check scheduler\nalready computes now de-clusters checks on Redis identically to the in-memory\nbackend. Cron schedules (absolute times) are unaffected; no-startDelay callers\nare unchanged (immediate first fire).\n\nVerified against real Redis: 20 equal-interval checks scheduled in one loop land\non a single phase without startDelay, and spread evenly across the full interval\nwith the jittered startDelay.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n* perf(healthcheck-http): make connect probe background-only; label estimate in UI\n\nLive OTel phase histograms showed the remaining latency outliers (e.g. 1300ms\nruns while the median was ~400ms) came from the connect-timing probe: pinned to\none resolved IP it can be far slower than the reused fetch (intermittently\n~1500ms vs ~90ms for the same host, an IPv6 SYN-retry the real request never\npays), and exec still `await`ed it on the once-per-TTL refresh run. That violates\nthe collector rule that best-effort timing must never delay the check.\n\n- The per-origin probe now refreshes the connect/TLS sample fully in the\n  BACKGROUND and is never awaited on a request''s critical path. Same-origin\n  checks dedupe so only one refresh probe runs per origin at a time. Each run\n  reads whatever sample is cached (possibly none on the first run to an origin,\n  until the background probe resolves), so a slow probe can no longer inflate a\n  check''s latency or the reported connect/TLS phase.\n- The run detail UI now labels the estimate: DNS/wait/transfer are measured on\n  the request, while connection and TLS setup are sampled from a periodic\n  per-host probe and cached briefly (about a minute), so the cached value is not\n  misread as a per-run measurement.\n\nAdds a regression test proving exec never awaits the probe (a probe that never\nresolves does not delay or hang the check).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n* docs(observability): show enabling metrics via `bun run dev`\n\nThe metrics enablement section only showed the `--filter start` form; add the\ndev-cockpit form (`CHECKSTACK_METRICS_ENABLED=1 bun run dev`), which is how the\nflag is set locally and propagates to the spawned backend. Regenerate the AI\ndocs index to match.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n* feat(observability): queue-backlog gauge + fix in-memory backlog undercount\n\nAdds the queue depth metric and makes it trustworthy under the exact saturation\nit exists to detect. Surfaced by a scale harness driving the real hot path.\n\n- New `checkstack.queue.jobs` observable gauge (state=pending|processing),\n  registered once the QueueManager exists. `pending` is the scale signal: a\n  backlog that climbs without draining means work arrives faster than the queue\n  concurrency can execute it. No-op unless metrics are enabled.\n- Fix: the in-memory queue undercounted `pending`. `processNext` removed a job\n  from the pending list and only then awaited a concurrency slot in `processJob`,\n  so jobs blocked on a slot were invisible (not pending, not processing). Under\n  saturation the backlog read ~0 while hundreds of jobs were queued. Slot-waiters\n  are now counted in `pending`; `processing` still counts only executing jobs.\n- Docs: observability guide gains the metric + guidance to read `pending`\n  against lock-pool waiting to tell slot-bound from database-bound.\n\nMeasured with the harness: 20% unreachable checks (each pinning a slot for the\nfull timeout) drove the backlog 0 -> 700+ in 35s with lock-pool waiting at 0 -\nthe first scaling ceiling is concurrency-slot saturation by slow checks, not the\ndatabase. A separate lock-pool micro-benchmark shows rollup-write throughput\nplateaus at ~1900/s (lockPool max 10) regardless of queue concurrency.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n* test(healthcheck): add scale harness for slot + advisory-lock ceilings\n\nA developer diagnostic that drives the real health-check hot path so the two\nscaling ceilings can be measured, not guessed. Composes the real components:\nthe concurrency-gated in-memory queue, the real HTTP strategy (real TCP/TLS to\na local echo server), the real advisory-lock rollup on the real lock/admin\npools, and a faithful copy of the scoped-db transaction shape. Enables metrics\nso it also exercises the queue-backlog gauge live.\n\n- MODE=load: N recurring checks at concurrency C with a SLOW_FRAC of\n  unreachable targets (they abort at the timeout, pinning a concurrency slot).\n  Surfaces slot saturation: `pending` backlog climbs while `lockWait` stays 0.\n- MODE=lockpool: M advisory-xact-lock rollup writes at increasing concurrency\n  K. Surfaces the lock-pool ceiling: throughput plateaus once K passes the pool\n  max and extra concurrency only adds lock-wait.\n\nRoot-script constraints honored: workspace code imported via relative source\npaths, the DB layer reuses the backend''s exported pools (no bare pg/drizzle),\nisolated `scale_harness` schema dropped on exit.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n* feat(healthcheck): slow-check bulkhead primitives (adaptive timeout, classifier, lane)\n\nPure, tested building blocks for the per-env slow-check bulkhead + adaptive\ntimeout (wired into the executor in a follow-up):\n\n- adaptive-timeout: shrink a suspect check''s timeout toward its OWN measured\n  healthy latency, never below it; four guardrails make a recovery deadlock\n  impossible (no-baseline => no shrink, healthy-only baseline, periodic\n  full-timeout recovery probe, one-success snap-back).\n- slow-check-classifier: per-env classification from durable recent runs -\n  suspect = last K runs are SLOW transport failures (latency >= timeout x\n  fraction), so a fast connect-refused is not penalised; plus p95 healthy\n  baseline and recovery-probe cadence.\n- suspect-lane: pod-local work-conserving bulkhead - non-blocking tryAdmit with\n  capacity cap + single-flight per key.\n- slow-check-config: env-driven runtime (lane capacity + tuning), kill-switch.\n- checkstack.healthcheck.deferred counter (reason=lane_full|in_flight).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n* chore(rebase): drop getSystemHealthStatus batching test, regen docs-index\n\nRebase onto #417 (fan-out-aware notifications/counts). #417 rewrote\ngetSystemHealthStatus with per-env slice accounting; the earlier\nwithScopedTransaction batching of that method was taken out in favour of #417''s\ntested version, so its batching test is removed. Re-applying read batching on\nthe fan-out-aware method is tracked as a follow-up. docs-index regenerated.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n* wip(healthcheck): single-env executor + convergence reconciler (incomplete)\n\nSAVEPOINT - does not compile yet; callers not wired. Preserves:\n- executor runs exactly one env from payload.environmentId (fan-out loop +\n  inline rollup + anyEnvNotified removed; rollup moves to an event-driven\n  consumer).\n- schedule-reconciler.ts: convergence controller (planReconcile diff +\n  reconcileHealthCheckJobs, full/system-scoped).\n\nRemaining: wire index/router/gitops/run_now to the reconciler, rework executor\ntests for single-env, Phase 2 rollup consumer, bulkhead per-job wiring.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n* feat(healthcheck): per-environment recurring jobs + convergence reconciler\n\nMigrate health-check scheduling from one recurring job per (config, system)\nthat fans out over environments inside a single tick to ONE recurring job per\n(config, system, environment) slice. Each job now runs exactly its own\nenvironment and holds a concurrency slot only for that one probe, so a slow or\nfailing environment can no longer stall its siblings (per-env isolation is now\nstructural, not loop-guarded).\n\n- queue-executor: single-env executor. `HealthCheckJobPayload.environmentId`\n  is required; the executor validates the payload''s env against the CURRENT\n  effective set (assignment `environmentIds` INTERSECT catalog membership) and\n  SKIPS a stale tick (env-less job for a system that gained envs, or an env no\n  longer effective), leaving the reconciler to converge. A catalog-resolution\n  failure keeps an env-scoped probe running with degraded (empty) fields and\n  still broadcasts ENVIRONMENT_RESOLUTION_FAILED. `bootstrapHealthChecks` and\n  the post-loop system-rollup write are removed (rollup moves to a Phase 2\n  event-driven consumer).\n- schedule-reconciler: k8s-controller-style convergence. Desired per-env job\n  set derives entirely from Postgres + catalog membership, so every pod plans\n  the same. Full reconcile (boot/periodic) serializes on the `health:reconcile`\n  advisory lock and cancels orphaned jobs; a system-scoped reconcile only\n  adds/updates. Wired into boot, assignment mutations (router), and GitOps\n  system reconcile.\n- run_now automation: enqueues one one-off job per effective environment slice\n  (env-less assignment => a single `environmentId: null` run).\n- service: `resolveEnqueueEnvironmentIds` shared helper mirrors the executor''s\n  fan-out resolution for run_now.\n\nBoot/GitOps/router now call `reconcileHealthCheckJobs`; the notification\nrollup dedup (#417) becomes structural under per-env jobs (env-scoped runs\nnever notify the bare rollup).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n* test(healthcheck): convergence reconciler unit + integration coverage\n\nplanReconcile pure-diff cases (schedule missing, no-op unchanged, reschedule on\ninterval change, cancel orphans only on full reconcile, never touch foreign\njobs, system-scoped add/update-only) plus reconcileHealthCheckJobs driven\nagainst in-memory queue/db/catalog mocks (per-env fan-out, env-less collapse,\norphan cancellation, health:reconcile advisory lock, scoped no-cancel).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n* feat(healthcheck): event-driven debounced system-rollup consumer (Phase 2)\n\nUnder per-environment jobs each run writes only its own\n`<systemId>::<env>` health entity; the bare `<systemId>` ROLLUP entity (the\nworst-status view badges, SLO rules, dependency maps and system-level\nautomations reference) is no longer recomputed inline. This consumer closes\nthat gap without re-coupling the executor to the rollup:\n\n- Subscribes to per-env `health` ENTITY_CHANGED with work-queue delivery\n  (once per cluster per change) and ignores bare-rollup changes, so the\n  consumer''s own rollup write can never re-trigger it (no feedback loop).\n- Debounces per system into a fixed time-bucket jobId\n  (`healthrollup:<systemId>:<bucket>`), so a burst of env transitions coalesces\n  to one recompute on both queue backends; the fresh bucket id per window means\n  BullMQ completed-job retention never drops a later window''s job.\n- The rollup-queue consumer recomputes the bare `<systemId>` entity via\n  `recomputeSystemRollupHealth`, which diffs prev→next under the\n  `health:<systemId>` advisory lock, emits the rollup ENTITY_CHANGED, and (on a\n  real change) invalidates the cache + broadcasts SYSTEM_STATUS_CHANGED.\n\nNotifications stay owned by env runs (structural #417 dedup): the consumer\nnever notifies, so a fanned-out system''s rollup notification is always\nsuppressed, while an env-less system notifies directly from its run.\n\n`recomputeSystemRollupHealth` gains optional signalService/cache params (the\nprev read + SYSTEM_STATUS_CHANGED broadcast only run when a caller wants the\nsystem-level signal; pause/resume keeps its single-read entity-only recompute).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n* feat(healthcheck): wire slow-check bulkhead + adaptive timeout into executor\n\nEngage the previously-built primitives (suspect lane, per-env classifier,\nadaptive timeout) in the single-env executor so a slot-hogging check can no\nlonger starve healthy ones:\n\n- Before each run, classify the (config, system, env) slice''s recent runs.\n  A slice whose last K runs were SLOW transport failures (held their slot ~the\n  full timeout) is \"suspect\".\n- A suspect slice is admitted to a capped, pod-local lane (single-flight keyed\n  on the slice) and probed with a timeout SHRUNK toward its own healthy-latency\n  baseline (adaptiveTimeout guardrails: no baseline → no shrink, healthy-only\n  baseline, periodic full-timeout recovery probe, absolute floor). When the\n  lane is full or a prior run of the same slice is still in flight, the tick is\n  DEFERRED: records nothing (so it can''t pile up) and increments\n  `checkstack.healthcheck.deferred{reason}`. The recurring job stays scheduled,\n  so the next tick retries once the lane drains.\n- A healthy slice never touches the lane and always runs at the full timeout.\n- The lane slot is released in a finally on every exit path.\n\nThe decision is extracted into `evaluateSlowCheckAdmission` (pure but for the\nlane acquire) for direct unit testing; the executor just reads the slice''s\nrecent runs, applies the verdict, and releases the slot. Resolved once at\nworker startup from env (kill switch: CHECKSTACK_HEALTHCHECK_SLOW_LANE_ENABLED=0);\n`setupHealthCheckWorker` accepts an explicit runtime for tests.\n\nPer-env isolation is now complete: one failing env of a multi-env system is\nbulkheaded and adaptively timed out on its OWN slice while sibling envs (their\nown jobs) run untouched.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n* perf(healthcheck): batch getSystemHealthStatus 1+N reads into one transaction\n\n`getSystemHealthStatus` reads the system''s enabled associations then one\nrun-window per association — a 1+N scoped-db fan-out where each standalone query\nopened its own BEGIN/SET LOCAL/COMMIT and checked a connection out. Wrap the\nwhole read fan-out (associations + every per-check window, plus the pure per-env\nevaluation) in a single `withScopedTransaction`, collapsing 1+N transactions to\none connection held for the batch. Re-applies the batching that was dropped\nresolving the rebase conflict against the #417 fan-out rewrite, now on top of\nthe per-environment worst-wins rollup.\n\nAdds a regression test pinning that exactly one `db.transaction` is opened per\ncall regardless of check count.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n* test(harness): add BULKHEAD mode to the health-check scale harness\n\nThe load harness modelled only the baseline (no slow-check protection). Add\n`BULKHEAD=1` (+ `LANE_CAP`) which engages the real `evaluateSlowCheckAdmission`\n(suspect lane + adaptive timeout) in the consume handler: a suspect system is\nadmitted to a capped lane or DEFERRED (recording nothing), so slot-hogging\nchecks stop starving healthy ones. Slow systems are seeded as already-suspect\n(the \"host already down at pod start\" case; production has this history on tick\none). Reports `deferrals` in the summary for a direct before/after.\n\nMeasured (N=240, 20% unreachable, conc 10, 5s timeout, 35s):\n  BULKHEAD=0: finalBacklog=774 (climbing), completions=288, timeouts=60\n  BULKHEAD=1: finalBacklog=0 (bounded),   completions=862, timeouts=12, deferrals=207\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n* docs(healthcheck): document per-env execution, reconciler, rollup, bulkhead\n\nAdd a health-check execution & scheduling page covering per-environment\nrecurring jobs, the convergence reconciler, the event-driven debounced rollup\nconsumer, and the slow-check bulkhead + adaptive timeout (with harness numbers).\nAdd the checkstack.healthcheck.deferred counter to the observability metrics\nreference and regenerate the AI docs search index. Include the per-environment\njobs + bulkhead changeset.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n* fix(harness): clamp request-controlled echo delay to bound resource use\n\nCodeQL flagged the scale harness''s loopback echo server passing a\nrequest-query-derived duration straight to setTimeout (js/resource-exhaustion).\nClamp the delay to a fixed 120s ceiling and reject non-finite values before\nscheduling the timer.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n* fix(harness): key echo delay on path, not a request-supplied value\n\nThe Math.min clamp still left a request-query -> setTimeout dataflow that CodeQL\nflags as js/resource-exhaustion. Route the echo server on the path (`/slow` vs\n`/fast`) and delay by a fixed server-side constant, so no request-controlled\nvalue ever reaches setTimeout.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n* chore(changeset): cover @checkstack/test-utils-backend withTransactionMock\n\nThe batching regression test relies on a new `withTransactionMock` export in\n@checkstack/test-utils-backend, a versioned (non-private) package, so bump it\npatch and document the helper.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M8p5PjRH1VS7iLXECyHos5\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqTe1SCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAqE8QABg2ooMRU9ZkqAWZIQ3Ns4HR\n5WZvZteC7Dx7DDm8aGIq3lD1KS2xqMlhIlzXeiQYi2XjvTr5T91vTNK3kROGM0n9\n6ZdbFNpOM2Ibux1+Jrifaw3HlwVMQPeCjffmkKfAiSPis/Yu85ANjONcXs8JaHun\nBXjrAZUknm1iTEjghhc1no13TGHTmN0L22E1xjOO8DqBAVAQ2pv5hrrqx1wFaKL3\nbARPrsDM8Ep5Owt8dUkNbaxMB80ybmT4epwve3Sw9vW9O0ww3OSsHJM7oEo2oSa+\nXWs29GvhajZ3cUDHZ7RPxkt1MTz6eUmnCmaTeL/3nLyfKpDWI4ewfG/HdBH+MX6t\nGWOYd+07JUoAAvC/8OzasYcjZS5zjkukh5doAl30d7P5NLgouz97JUHr0/a6lSjp\nGBRbXkpoLbQPlOGWhoGilewj5w+Escnl7+3N2HegXo837MY49gypG4h0LgodT4uR\nepchG4akQMimKhkWM9DWucm1jR9XYonwmEi5BB+XdewStVvNtNlCvZCjSBrA6Hgn\nQBAdPIsrqolZwhY+gDooUv9M3DAd8H3mOCz/VbatXSE5rGAGwDqcqVhBLLpEWwD4\n51cf0h/H2AIub/PBq7+lFnFHfMpnqOd0fe4WbcX93D+8ct12smKed1iJx25uZaM6\n4PK8bct6stXeQdKuF539\n=92AY\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-07-08T06:25:23Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/enyineer/checkstack/commit/d0eddc9d59b6e01574294b98043375d425d00c85","node_id":"C_kwDOQrp5TdoAKGQwZWRkYzlkNTliNmUwMTU3NDI5NGI5ODA0MzM3NWQ0MjVkMDBjODU","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/enyineer/checkstack/commit/8aae4e2eeb35078b0251726708607f4c547f9338","sha":"8aae4e2eeb35078b0251726708607f4c547f9338","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/enyineer/checkstack/commits/8aae4e2eeb35078b0251726708607f4c547f9338"}],"sha":"d0eddc9d59b6e01574294b98043375d425d00c85","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/enyineer/checkstack/commits/d0eddc9d59b6e01574294b98043375d425d00c85"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/27201677?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/enyineer","id":27201677,"login":"enyineer","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI3MjAxNjc3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/enyineer/checkstack/commits/8aae4e2eeb35078b0251726708607f4c547f9338/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-07-07T22:13:07Z","email":"nico.enking@gmail.com","name":"Nico"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-07-07T22:13:07Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(healthcheck): fan-out aware notifications, counts, and last-healthy stamp (#417)\n\nThree related environment-fan-out improvements to the health UI:\n\n- Dedupe unhealthy notifications: a fanned-out system notified once per\n  environment AND again for the system rollup in the same tick. The rollup\n  notification is now suppressed when any environment already notified this\n  tick (the rollup is always driven by that environment), while the rollup\n  transition record and SYSTEM_STATUS_CHANGED signal still fire so SLO,\n  dependency graph, frontend, and automations are unchanged. Env-less systems\n  keep their single system-level notification.\n\n- \"X of Y checks failing\" now counts (check x environment) slices, not checks:\n  a 1-check/3-env system with one env failing reads \"1 of 3\", not \"1 of 1\".\n  SystemCheckStatus gains sliceCount + failingSliceCount, summed in\n  deriveHealthcheckSignals.\n\n- Systems overview shows the last successful run per check (or check+env when\n  fanned out): non-healthy rows render \"Healthy until 2h ago\" / \"Never\n  healthy\". getSystemHealthOverview computes lastSuccessfulRunAt via a\n  max-per-env aggregate outside the sparkline window, so it stays accurate for\n  long-failing checks.\n\nAdds regression tests at every layer (rollup-notification dedup truth table,\nenv-slice counting, service slice counts, last-successful-run threading),\nupdates the notifications concept + across-environments guide, and regenerates\nthe docs index. Changesets included.\n\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01PYkvVXtgJGcoxTSDSuQETR\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"a7108422729cc4424f045ccace9dc321f1c176c4","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/enyineer/checkstack/git/trees/a7108422729cc4424f045ccace9dc321f1c176c4"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/enyineer/checkstack/git/commits/8aae4e2eeb35078b0251726708607f4c547f9338","verification":{"payload":"tree a7108422729cc4424f045ccace9dc321f1c176c4\nparent f93ee7a41460aaeb903a3ee38c3a4ee795acc6ff\nauthor Nico <nico.enking@gmail.com> 1783462387 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1783462387 +0200\n\nfeat(healthcheck): fan-out aware notifications, counts, and last-healthy stamp (#417)\n\nThree related environment-fan-out improvements to the health UI:\n\n- Dedupe unhealthy notifications: a fanned-out system notified once per\n  environment AND again for the system rollup in the same tick. The rollup\n  notification is now suppressed when any environment already notified this\n  tick (the rollup is always driven by that environment), while the rollup\n  transition record and SYSTEM_STATUS_CHANGED signal still fire so SLO,\n  dependency graph, frontend, and automations are unchanged. Env-less systems\n  keep their single system-level notification.\n\n- \"X of Y checks failing\" now counts (check x environment) slices, not checks:\n  a 1-check/3-env system with one env failing reads \"1 of 3\", not \"1 of 1\".\n  SystemCheckStatus gains sliceCount + failingSliceCount, summed in\n  deriveHealthcheckSignals.\n\n- Systems overview shows the last successful run per check (or check+env when\n  fanned out): non-healthy rows render \"Healthy until 2h ago\" / \"Never\n  healthy\". getSystemHealthOverview computes lastSuccessfulRunAt via a\n  max-per-env aggregate outside the sparkline window, so it stays accurate for\n  long-failing checks.\n\nAdds regression tests at every layer (rollup-notification dedup truth table,\nenv-slice counting, service slice counts, last-successful-run threading),\nupdates the notifications concept + across-environments guide, and regenerates\nthe docs index. Changesets included.\n\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01PYkvVXtgJGcoxTSDSuQETR\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqTXnzCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA2ZEQACBpquSzKiF+xertV/Umskff\nkhYoLJFzhx932y+033Q5TnRoIndr0kO54gkgFY+RuoktzlvD2zBg33vMd8emGXoB\nslTiOXgJkigIfflxzNAKhVMPS5MMTkyG4Y9wFWpP8UBM8xyiM/LNfIN6MWSff2N3\nP7GUkPab4uZlBX7/bMrr67Vl2mN/XjhOoagkSJKuOR9KCNEBb4Vd4k5DDGnywdUC\ntp2TaXicYwt5K7aWWdhtmyfUUItDihztQHGaPkVag5RNESBMY70ZnbscRqs0TEEe\nncdOLparqz5gUWkVdvtqIn4KFMqpCk7oNvM1N6dLS8u112tCS2DkLY7gVow98XJI\nfvG9nQkEFNDjizHa9En8KORx/Xju0IJW6R1c7q5cQogvLwhKZyM/5Wo3HT4HURSX\nYH6s4BWx6JUvHwOZU55EZwDuxH9bwsG8PgaGSTFmxg6eExrMPt2sRZelibq+DgP7\nVGZCUYxSyNYFWR8BS1JCFDu8ZSPps5kK4Htd00b1rw3wEFMKjCF6cYl0JdYaJ/bL\n3xOex6lYnjXsY2znueytfFVN4t8w/N81Qr7bib7Xo3dRzk/+hHnU8/P9sh1WmCxU\naBCzERPx9xzsfZLW5rklPoLwY2X7+n0CyxblSB5wchK2wW367fL2VPm/o/Z78tbc\nuRGcMloBPSL+P1auQNoq\n=tELJ\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-07-07T22:13:08Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/enyineer/checkstack/commit/8aae4e2eeb35078b0251726708607f4c547f9338","node_id":"C_kwDOQrp5TdoAKDhhYWU0ZTJlZWIzNTA3OGIwMjUxNzI2NzA4NjA3ZjRjNTQ3ZjkzMzg","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/enyineer/checkstack/commit/f93ee7a41460aaeb903a3ee38c3a4ee795acc6ff","sha":"f93ee7a41460aaeb903a3ee38c3a4ee795acc6ff","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/enyineer/checkstack/commits/f93ee7a41460aaeb903a3ee38c3a4ee795acc6ff"}],"sha":"8aae4e2eeb35078b0251726708607f4c547f9338","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/enyineer/checkstack/commits/8aae4e2eeb35078b0251726708607f4c547f9338"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/27201677?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/enyineer","id":27201677,"login":"enyineer","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI3MjAxNjc3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/enyineer","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/enyineer/checkstack/commits/f93ee7a41460aaeb903a3ee38c3a4ee795acc6ff/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-07-07T20:05:18Z","email":"nico.enking@gmail.com","name":"Nico"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-07-07T20:05:18Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(auth): team-scoped healthcheck managers get 403 on editor utilities (#415)\n\n* fix(auth): team-scoped healthcheck managers get 403 on editor utilities\n\nA user who could manage a health check via a team grant, but did not hold the\nglobal healthcheck.configuration.read rule, got a 403 on the editor''s utility\nendpoints (getStrategies/getCollectors/testCollectorScript and the\nscript-package SDK/type routes) and could not open the editor.\n\nRoot cause: those endpoints were gated with instanceAccess { global: true }\n(enforced only against global access rules - team grants never satisfy it) or a\nseparate global script-packages.read rule.\n\n- Add a `typeScoped` instanceAccess mode: authorize a no-instance utility/catalog\n  endpoint by ANY team grant of the rule''s resource type (viewer/editor/owner, or\n  a creator/create-capability grant) OR the global rule. Thread an\n  `includeCreator` flag through hasAnyTypeGrant (store -> auth S2S -> AuthService)\n  so create-capability counts only where intended. Boot validator recognises it.\n- Apply typeScoped to healthcheck getStrategies/getCollectors (read) and\n  testCollectorScript (manage).\n- Relax script-packages getInstallState and the two raw type routes\n  (sdk-types/:version, types/:hash/:spec) to authenticated-only; they serve\n  IntelliSense metadata, not secrets. Manage endpoints stay restricted.\n- Document typeScoped + the global:true hazard in the RLAC rule (why no existing\n  gate flagged this: check:manage-capabilities covers routes/nav only, and the\n  boot validator treats global:true as a deliberate opt-out).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014tB8vZmB9gDDpf285XoeLy\n\n* test(auth): real-DB coverage for hasAnyTypeGrant team grants + includeCreator\n\nIntegration test (CHECKSTACK_IT lane) exercising the actual SQL WHERE clause the\nmocked-DB unit tests cannot: a real viewer/editor/owner tuple authorizes a\nteam-scoped caller (read vs manage), a type-level `creator` tuple counts ONLY\nwith includeCreator (the create-first path behind `typeScoped`), and unrelated\nteams / other resource types are denied. This proves the fix end-to-end for a\nreal user with team grants, not just the middleware wiring.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014tB8vZmB9gDDpf285XoeLy\n\n* fix(auth): derive access gates from contract; close global-only team-grant 403s\n\nFix a class of authorization drift where team-scoped managers were denied\nsurfaces/actions they were authorized for, because the frontend gate hand\nre-encoded the backend contract''s access mode and could pick the wrong one.\n\nContract-derived gating (core/common, frontend-api, auth-frontend):\n- New `resolveProcedureGate` reads a procedure''s `access` + `instanceAccess`\n  metadata and returns the single gate the backend enforces (open/global/\n  idParam/create/typeScoped; parentScope normalized onto a synthetic parent\n  rule, so no contract change was needed).\n- New `useProcedureAccess` / `useSurfaceAccess`; migrate every create-button\n  gate off `useCanCreate` and remove `useCanCreate` from `AccessApi`. Also\n  fixes a latent create.parent gap (global parent-managers now see create).\n\nGate fusion (frontend-api, auth-frontend, backend-api):\n- `useGatedMutation` / `useGatedQuery` weld the authorization verdict onto the\n  RPC call from the same contract procedure + input, so a control can''t get\n  `mutate` without the verdict and a gated query never fires a guaranteed 403.\n- Automation editor is the reference surface; RunDetailPage \"Cancel run\" fused\n  (real drift fix). `checkstack/prefer-gated-mutation` lint rule + dev-only\n  runtime drift detector make fusion the default and surface stragglers.\n\nGlobal-only team-grant audit (automation/healthcheck/anomaly/incident/\nmaintenance): endpoints on team-scopable types that were `global: true` move to\n`typeScoped` (editor utilities/catalogs) or `parentScope` (run sub-resources,\nanomaly config, incident/maintenance removeLink) so team grants authorize them;\ngenuine admin endpoints stay global.\n\nIncludes unit + e2e coverage, docs (access-gating, teams, rlac rule) and\nregenerated docs-index, and changesets for the affected packages.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014tB8vZmB9gDDpf285XoeLy\n\n* chore(changeset): cover frontend/ui/catalog-common AccessApi adaptations\n\nAdd `@checkstack/frontend`, `@checkstack/ui`, and `@checkstack/catalog-common`\n(all patch) to the derive-access-gates changeset. They carry only mechanical\nadaptations to the AccessApi interface change - the host-app fallback stubs, the\nStorybook mock, and a doc comment now reference `useProcedureAccess` /\n`useSurfaceAccess` instead of the removed `useCanCreate` - so the changeset\ncoverage reminder is satisfied.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014tB8vZmB9gDDpf285XoeLy\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"bd9d85e73fec068f047d6c2e0ea4a3a1212f0df0","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/enyineer/checkstack/git/trees/bd9d85e73fec068f047d6c2e0ea4a3a1212f0df0"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/enyineer/checkstack/git/commits/f93ee7a41460aaeb903a3ee38c3a4ee795acc6ff","verification":{"payload":"tree bd9d85e73fec068f047d6c2e0ea4a3a1212f0df0\nparent 66ee199bed1a156709d6ab5767994b4bb7d5422a\nauthor Nico <nico.enking@gmail.com> 1783454718 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1783454718 +0200\n\nfix(auth): team-scoped healthcheck managers get 403 on editor utilities (#415)\n\n* fix(auth): team-scoped healthcheck managers get 403 on editor utilities\n\nA user who could manage a health check via a team grant, but did not hold the\nglobal healthcheck.configuration.read rule, got a 403 on the editor''s utility\nendpoints (getStrategies/getCollectors/testCollectorScript and the\nscript-package SDK/type routes) and could not open the editor.\n\nRoot cause: those endpoints were gated with instanceAccess { global: true }\n(enforced only against global access rules - team grants never satisfy it) or a\nseparate global script-packages.read rule.\n\n- Add a `typeScoped` instanceAccess mode: authorize a no-instance utility/catalog\n  endpoint by ANY team grant of the rule''s resource type (viewer/editor/owner, or\n  a creator/create-capability grant) OR the global rule. Thread an\n  `includeCreator` flag through hasAnyTypeGrant (store -> auth S2S -> AuthService)\n  so create-capability counts only where intended. Boot validator recognises it.\n- Apply typeScoped to healthcheck getStrategies/getCollectors (read) and\n  testCollectorScript (manage).\n- Relax script-packages getInstallState and the two raw type routes\n  (sdk-types/:version, types/:hash/:spec) to authenticated-only; they serve\n  IntelliSense metadata, not secrets. Manage endpoints stay restricted.\n- Document typeScoped + the global:true hazard in the RLAC rule (why no existing\n  gate flagged this: check:manage-capabilities covers routes/nav only, and the\n  boot validator treats global:true as a deliberate opt-out).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014tB8vZmB9gDDpf285XoeLy\n\n* test(auth): real-DB coverage for hasAnyTypeGrant team grants + includeCreator\n\nIntegration test (CHECKSTACK_IT lane) exercising the actual SQL WHERE clause the\nmocked-DB unit tests cannot: a real viewer/editor/owner tuple authorizes a\nteam-scoped caller (read vs manage), a type-level `creator` tuple counts ONLY\nwith includeCreator (the create-first path behind `typeScoped`), and unrelated\nteams / other resource types are denied. This proves the fix end-to-end for a\nreal user with team grants, not just the middleware wiring.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014tB8vZmB9gDDpf285XoeLy\n\n* fix(auth): derive access gates from contract; close global-only team-grant 403s\n\nFix a class of authorization drift where team-scoped managers were denied\nsurfaces/actions they were authorized for, because the frontend gate hand\nre-encoded the backend contract''s access mode and could pick the wrong one.\n\nContract-derived gating (core/common, frontend-api, auth-frontend):\n- New `resolveProcedureGate` reads a procedure''s `access` + `instanceAccess`\n  metadata and returns the single gate the backend enforces (open/global/\n  idParam/create/typeScoped; parentScope normalized onto a synthetic parent\n  rule, so no contract change was needed).\n- New `useProcedureAccess` / `useSurfaceAccess`; migrate every create-button\n  gate off `useCanCreate` and remove `useCanCreate` from `AccessApi`. Also\n  fixes a latent create.parent gap (global parent-managers now see create).\n\nGate fusion (frontend-api, auth-frontend, backend-api):\n- `useGatedMutation` / `useGatedQuery` weld the authorization verdict onto the\n  RPC call from the same contract procedure + input, so a control can''t get\n  `mutate` without the verdict and a gated query never fires a guaranteed 403.\n- Automation editor is the reference surface; RunDetailPage \"Cancel run\" fused\n  (real drift fix). `checkstack/prefer-gated-mutation` lint rule + dev-only\n  runtime drift detector make fusion the default and surface stragglers.\n\nGlobal-only team-grant audit (automation/healthcheck/anomaly/incident/\nmaintenance): endpoints on team-scopable types that were `global: true` move to\n`typeScoped` (editor utilities/catalogs) or `parentScope` (run sub-resources,\nanomaly config, incident/maintenance removeLink) so team grants authorize them;\ngenuine admin endpoints stay global.\n\nIncludes unit + e2e coverage, docs (access-gating, teams, rlac rule) and\nregenerated docs-index, and changesets for the affected packages.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014tB8vZmB9gDDpf285XoeLy\n\n* chore(changeset): cover frontend/ui/catalog-common AccessApi adaptations\n\nAdd `@checkstack/frontend`, `@checkstack/ui`, and `@checkstack/catalog-common`\n(all patch) to the derive-access-gates changeset. They carry only mechanical\nadaptations to the AccessApi interface change - the host-app fallback stubs, the\nStorybook mock, and a doc comment now reference `useProcedureAccess` /\n`useSurfaceAccess` instead of the removed `useCanCreate` - so the changeset\ncoverage reminder is satisfied.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014tB8vZmB9gDDpf285XoeLy\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqTVv+CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAASVEQABz/jGkgB+nCHBXtloQaEkCH\nMISHs2fUNg4As+4h2o5LmoQ05PgezRH4s9HDNHNBtouFu4QIilIL8WUe+WisN/Oi\nYRQj/lXMrQ//yfky5L3Clno6DYeQwk6fLKZI9FZjPhuKN02daQ/PnI2N2DhjwedK\nOftSaUZIlbaoM4bgesIRarr6/6jCiGoiLHemFYjBimayLRYmT8Beq87bQyT4hkYk\nHl7+IPcDi0OWI/7E3ecfQ3f1Xq8CIbD8CLNnYQ4jJIq5udmsw0uwhSUotnqiJ04F\n+izO1OHFBftQn8Mvwpflwdm0ZzwE6jOsMOC0B0/FIQexvjWpUnPysFEojln4NyGj\niotS7uSy+A4/3NYwr/GXxvK3+ss0z2tzjczAn0DK/DH1jk4pTysiQsHXzTS6nt18\n3ESnjisbZbk3xFtctZFcCUb8igrP/rH+/dPCi20ZxtW